{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785216873843,"sessionID":"ses_058ca7309ffeyGmRDCjINe2gtX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_1xcgEjIhdcx8BrgIMtBu7840","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":".opencode/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: # DOCTOR ROUTER — CANONICAL ROUTE MANIFEST\n3: # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: # Source of truth for /doctor <target> dispatch.\n5: # Read by: .opencode/commands/doctor/speckit.md (the router)\n6: # Validated by: .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh\n7: # Consumed by: NOT currently harvested by either Skill Advisor implementation.\n8: #   Both harvesters (Python skill_advisor.py:810-871 _DOC_HARVEST_SUBDIRS =\n9: #   (\"references\",\"assets\") rooted at SKILLS_DIR; TS doc-frontmatter.ts:23)\n10: #   walk only .opencode/skills/*/{references,assets}/*.md frontmatter — a\n11: #   .yaml file under .opencode/commands/doctor/ is out of scope by both file\n12: #   type and root. /doctor <target> dispatch is presentation-menu/argv-driven\n13: #   (see speckit.md), not advisor-driven. The trigger_phrases field below is\n14: #   retained for a possible future doctor-routes harvester but is inert today.\n15: #\n16: # Schema (per route):\n17: #   target:           string — positional name the user types\n18: #   yaml:             string — YAML asset filename in assets/\n19: #   setup_vars:       list   — variables the router resolves and passes to YAML\n20: #   allowed_flags:    list   — per-target flag schema (target-first parsing)\n21: #   mutating:         enum   — read-only | add-only | mutates\n22: #   gate3_location:   string — concrete mutation target (or \"n/a\" for read-only)\n23: #   mcp_tools:        list   — subset of router's frontmatter allowed-tools union\n24: #   trigger_phrases:  list   — descriptive routing phrases for this target; not\n25: #                              currently harvested by advisor (see \"Consumed by\" above)\n26: #\n27: # Skill-advisor recommendation tools are registered through mk-skill-advisor.\n28: # To add a new target: append a route here, then re-run route-validate.sh.\n29: # To remove a target: delete the route AND its corresponding assets/doctor_<target>.yaml.\n30: # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n31: schema_version: 1\n32: routes:\n33:   - target: memory\n34:     yaml: doctor-memory.yaml\n35:     setup_vars: [execution_mode, intent, incremental]\n36:     allowed_flags: [\"--incremental=true|false\"]\n37:     mutating: add-only\n38:     gate3_location: \"<packet_scratch>/doctor-memory-report.<timestamp>.md + <packet_scratch>/doctor-memory-state.<timestamp>.json\"\n39:     mcp_tools:\n40:       - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_health\n41:       - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_drift_why\n42:       - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_search\n43:       - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_stats\n44:     script_invocations:\n45:       - 'Warm-only: probe the existing mk-spec-memory IPC socket first; only when it answers, run node .opencode/bin/spec-memory.cjs memory_health --format json --timeout-ms 500 --warm-only (the flag guarantees no daemon start; exit 75 = backend unavailable, retryable)'\n46:     trigger_phrases:\n47:       - \"memory continuity index drift\"\n48:       - \"memory health rebuild\"\n49:       - \"context-index doctor\"\n50:       - \"voyage embedding rebuild\"\n51: \n52:   - target: embeddings\n53:     yaml: doctor-embeddings.yaml\n54:     setup_vars: [execution_mode]\n55:     allowed_flags: []\n56:     mutating: read-only\n57:     gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only provider and model-server status)\"\n58:     mcp_tools:\n59:       - mcp__mk_spec_memory__embedder_status\n60:     trigger_phrases:\n61:       - \"embedding provider status\"\n62:       - \"hf-local model-server health\"\n63:       - \"embedder_status diagnostics\"\n64: \n65:   - target: causal-graph\n66:     yaml: doctor-causal-graph.yaml\n67:     setup_vars: [execution_mode, intent, confidence_threshold]\n68:     allowed_flags: [\"--confidence-threshold=N\"]\n69:     mutating: add-only\n70:     gate3_location: \"<packet_scratch>/doctor-causal-graph-state.<timestamp>.json\"\n71:     mcp_tools:\n72:       - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_causal_stats\n73:       - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_drift_why\n74:       - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_search\n75:     trigger_phrases:\n76:       - \"causal edges drift\"\n77:       - \"causal graph doctor\"\n78:       - \"lineage coverage\"\n79:       - \"memory_causal_stats\"\n80: \n81: \n82:   - target: deep-loop\n83:     yaml: doctor-deep-loop.yaml\n84:     setup_vars: [execution_mode, intent, scope]\n85:     allowed_flags: [\"--scope=research|review|council|both|all\"]\n86:     mutating: add-only\n87:     gate3_location: \"<packet_scratch>/doctor-deep-loop-state.<timestamp>.json\"\n88:     mcp_tools: []\n89:     script_invocations:\n90:       - 'node .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/status.cjs --spec-folder \"{spec_folder}\" --loop-type \"{loop_type}\" --session-id \"{session_id}\"'\n91:       - 'node .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/query.cjs --spec-folder \"{spec_folder}\" --loop-type \"{loop_type}\" --session-id \"{session_id}\" --query-type \"{query_type}\"'\n92:       - 'node .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs --spec-folder \"{spec_folder}\" --loop-type \"{loop_type}\" --session-id \"{session_id}\"'\n93:     trigger_phrases:\n94:       - \"deep-loop coverage\"\n95:       - \"deep-loop graph rebuild\"\n96:       - \"deep-research convergence drift\"\n97:       - \"deep-review iteration graph\"\n98: \n99:   - target: skill-advisor\n100:     yaml: doctor-skill-advisor.yaml\n101:     setup_vars: [execution_mode, scope, skip_tests, dry_run]\n102:     allowed_flags: [\"--skip-tests\", \"--dry-run\", \"--scope=all|explicit|derived|lexical\"]\n103:     mutating: mutates\n104:     gate3_location: \"lib/scorer/lanes/*.ts + .opencode/skills/*/graph-metadata.json\"\n105:     mcp_tools:\n106:       - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__advisor_recommend\n107:       - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__advisor_status\n108:       - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__advisor_validate\n109:       - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__advisor_rebuild\n110:       - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__skill_graph_scan\n111:       - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__skill_graph_query\n112:       - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__skill_graph_status\n113:       - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_context\n114:       - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_search\n115:     script_invocations:\n116:       - 'Warm-only: probe the existing mk-skill-advisor IPC socket first; only when it answers, run node .opencode/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json --timeout-ms 500 --warm-only (the flag guarantees no daemon start; exit 75 = backend unavailable, retryable)'\n117:     trigger_phrases:\n118:       - \"skill advisor scoring\"\n119:       - \"skill advisor rebuild\"\n120:       - \"advisor recommendation tuning\"\n121:       - \"skill_graph scan\"\n122: \n123:   - target: skill-budget\n124:     yaml: doctor-skill-budget.yaml\n125:     setup_vars: [execution_mode, json_output, top_n, fail_over, project_ceiling]\n126:     allowed_flags: [\"--json\", \"--top-n=N\", \"--fail-over=N\", \"--project-ceiling=N\"]\n127:     mutating: read-only\n128:     gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only audit; no spec folder required)\"\n129:     mcp_tools: []   # script-only audit; no MCP calls\n130:     script_invocations:\n131:       - 'Warm-only: probe the existing mk-skill-advisor IPC socket first; only when it answers, run node .opencode/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json --timeout-ms 500 --warm-only (the flag guarantees no daemon start; exit 75 = backend unavailable, retryable)'\n132:     trigger_phrases:\n133:       - \"skill budget audit\"\n134:       - \"description budget check\"\n135:       - \"skill char count drift\"\n136:       - \"frontmatter description trim\"\n137: \n138:   - target: parent-skill\n139:     yaml: doctor-parent-skill.yaml\n140:     setup_vars: [execution_mode, parent_skill_dir]\n141:     allowed_flags: [\"--dir=<path>\"]\n142:     mutating: read-only\n143:     gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only structural audit; inspects a parent skill's graph-metadata/registry and reports, no writes)\"\n144:     mcp_tools: []   # script-only structural audit; no MCP calls\n145:     script_invocations:\n146:       # The fleet class gate runs first: it starts from SKILL.md, so it is the only\n147:       # check that can report a metadata file a root never wrote. The per-hub audit\n148:       # then goes deep on the one directory the operator named.\n149:       - 'node .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs'\n150:       - 'node .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs \"{parent_skill_dir}\"'\n151:     trigger_phrases:\n152:       - \"parent skill structure audit\"\n153:       - \"nested mode packet check\"\n154:       - \"mode-registry drift guard\"\n155:       - \"single graph-metadata identity\"\n156:       - \"skill root metadata contract\"\n157:       - \"missing skill json files\"\n158: \n159:   - target: skill-graph-freshness\n160:     yaml: doctor-skill-graph-freshness.yaml\n161:     setup_vars: [execution_mode]\n162:     allowed_flags: []\n163:     mutating: read-only\n164:     gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only diagnostic; 3-way diffs the skill-graph representations and reports drift, writes nothing)\"\n165:     mcp_tools: []   # script-only read; no MCP calls\n166:     script_invocations:\n167:       - 'node .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/skill-graph-freshness.cjs'\n168:     trigger_phrases:\n169:       - \"skill graph freshness\"\n170:       - \"skill-graph zombie ghost nodes\"\n171:       - \"compiled vs sqlite vs disk graph drift\"\n172:       - \"reindex staleness check\"\n173: \n174:   - target: fable-mode\n175:     yaml: doctor-fable-mode.yaml\n176:     setup_vars: [execution_mode, target_dir]\n177:     allowed_flags: [\"--dir=<path>\", \"--baseline=<path>\"]\n178:     mutating: read-only\n179:     gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only behavioral-metric diagnostic; reads deep-loop state + iteration markdown + baseline snapshot, writes no files)\"\n180:     mcp_tools: []   # script-only behavioral-metric read; no MCP calls\n181:     script_invocations:\n182:       - 'node .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/fable-mode-check.cjs --dir \"{target_dir}\"'\n183:     trigger_phrases:\n184:       - \"fable-5 behavioral metrics\"\n185:       - \"fable mode efficiency check\"\n186:       - \"tool text ratio diagnostic\"\n187:       - \"result-first opener metric\"\n188: \n189:   - target: runtime-mirrors\n190:     yaml: doctor-runtime-mirrors.yaml\n191:     setup_vars: [execution_mode]\n192:     allowed_flags: []\n193:     mutating: read-only\n194:     gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only mirror-parity diagnostic; every checker runs in its check-only form and writes nothing)\"\n195:     mcp_tools: []   # script-only mirror parity read; no MCP calls\n196:     script_invocations:\n197:       - 'node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs --check'\n198:       - 'node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/codex/sync-agents.cjs --check'\n199:       - 'node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/codex/sync-prompts.cjs --check'\n200:       - 'node .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs'\n201:       - 'node .opencode/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs --check'\n202:     trigger_phrases:\n203:       - \"runtime mirror drift\"\n204:       - \"agent command mirror parity\"\n205:       - \"cursor devin codex sync check\"\n206:       - \"discovery mirror out of sync\"\n207: \n208: # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n209: # COMPANION COMMAND ROUTES (/doctor:mcp sub-actions)\n210: # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n211: # /doctor:mcp dispatches via its own command (.opencode/commands/doctor/mcp.md).\n212: # These rows are tracked here for advisor + CI visibility only — the mcp.md\n213: # command parses its own sub-action argv and dispatches to the right YAML.\n214: # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n215: mcp_subroutes:\n216:   - sub_action: install\n217:     yaml: doctor-mcp-install.yaml\n218:     allowed_flags: [\"--server <name>\", \"--runtime <name>\"]\n219:     mutating: mutates\n220:     gate3_location: \"MCP server install dirs + runtime config (.opencode.json / .claude/settings.json / )\"\n221:     trigger_phrases:\n222:       - \"MCP install\"\n223:       - \"MCP server fresh install\"\n224:       - \"reinstall MCP\"\n225: \n226:   - sub_action: debug\n227:     yaml: doctor-mcp-debug.yaml\n228:     allowed_flags: [\"--fix\", \"--server <name>\"]\n229:     mutating: mutates\n230:     gate3_location: \"MCP server logs + runtime config repair\"\n231:     trigger_phrases:\n232:       - \"MCP server broken\"\n233:       - \"MCP debug\"\n234:       - \"MCP connection failure\"\n235:       - \"mcp-doctor.sh\"\n236: \n237: # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n238: # ORCHESTRATOR (NOT routed through /doctor — kept here for reference)\n239: # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n240: # /doctor:update is a STANDALONE command at .opencode/commands/doctor/update.md.\n241: # It does NOT route through /doctor. This block exists for advisor visibility.\n242: # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n243: standalone:\n244:   - command: /doctor:update\n245:     file: .opencode/commands/doctor/update.md\n246:     yaml: doctor-update.yaml\n247:     mutating: mutates\n248:     trigger_phrases:\n249:       - \"spec-kit version migration\"\n250:       - \"doctor update orchestrator\"\n251:       - \"rebuild all spec-kit databases\"\n252:       - \"doctor full sync\"\n\n(End of file - total 252 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# DOCTOR ROUTER — CANONICAL ROUTE MANIFEST\n# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# Source of truth for /doctor <target> dispatch.\n# Read by: .opencode/commands/doctor/speckit.md (the router)\n# Validated by: .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh\n# Consumed by: NOT currently harvested by either Skill Advisor implementation.\n#   Both harvesters (Python skill_advisor.py:810-871 _DOC_HARVEST_SUBDIRS =\n#   (\"references\",\"assets\") rooted at SKILLS_DIR; TS doc-frontmatter.ts:23)\n#   walk only .opencode/skills/*/{references,assets}/*.md frontmatter — a\n#   .yaml file under .opencode/commands/doctor/ is out of scope by both file\n#   type and root. /doctor <target> dispatch is presentation-menu/argv-driven\n#   (see speckit.md), not advisor-driven. The trigger_phrases field below is\n#   retained for a possible future doctor-routes harvester but is inert today.\n#\n# Schema (per route):\n#   target:           string — positional name the user types\n#   yaml:             string — YAML asset filename in assets/\n#   setup_vars:       list   — variables the router resolves and passes to YAML\n#   allowed_flags:    list   — per-target flag schema (target-first parsing)","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml","text":"# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# DOCTOR ROUTER — CANONICAL ROUTE MANIFEST\n# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# Source of truth for /doctor <target> dispatch.\n# Read by: .opencode/commands/doctor/speckit.md (the router)\n# Validated by: .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh\n# Consumed by: NOT currently harvested by either Skill Advisor implementation.\n#   Both harvesters (Python skill_advisor.py:810-871 _DOC_HARVEST_SUBDIRS =\n#   (\"references\",\"assets\") rooted at SKILLS_DIR; TS doc-frontmatter.ts:23)\n#   walk only .opencode/skills/*/{references,assets}/*.md frontmatter — a\n#   .yaml file under .opencode/commands/doctor/ is out of scope by both file\n#   type and root. /doctor <target> dispatch is presentation-menu/argv-driven\n#   (see speckit.md), not advisor-driven. The trigger_phrases field below is\n#   retained for a possible future doctor-routes harvester but is inert today.\n#\n# Schema (per route):\n#   target:           string — positional name the user types\n#   yaml:             string — YAML asset filename in assets/\n#   setup_vars:       list   — variables the router resolves and passes to YAML\n#   allowed_flags:    list   — per-target flag schema (target-first parsing)\n#   mutating:         enum   — read-only | add-only | mutates\n#   gate3_location:   string — concrete mutation target (or \"n/a\" for read-only)\n#   mcp_tools:        list   — subset of router's frontmatter allowed-tools union\n#   trigger_phrases:  list   — descriptive routing phrases for this target; not\n#                              currently harvested by advisor (see \"Consumed by\" above)\n#\n# Skill-advisor recommendation tools are registered through mk-skill-advisor.\n# To add a new target: append a route here, then re-run route-validate.sh.\n# To remove a target: delete the route AND its corresponding assets/doctor_<target>.yaml.\n# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nschema_version: 1\nroutes:\n  - target: memory\n    yaml: doctor-memory.yaml\n    setup_vars: [execution_mode, intent, incremental]\n    allowed_flags: [\"--incremental=true|false\"]\n    mutating: add-only\n    gate3_location: \"<packet_scratch>/doctor-memory-report.<timestamp>.md + <packet_scratch>/doctor-memory-state.<timestamp>.json\"\n    mcp_tools:\n      - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_health\n      - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_drift_why\n      - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_search\n      - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_stats\n    script_invocations:\n      - 'Warm-only: probe the existing mk-spec-memory IPC socket first; only when it answers, run node .opencode/bin/spec-memory.cjs memory_health --format json --timeout-ms 500 --warm-only (the flag guarantees no daemon start; exit 75 = backend unavailable, retryable)'\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"memory continuity index drift\"\n      - \"memory health rebuild\"\n      - \"context-index doctor\"\n      - \"voyage embedding rebuild\"\n\n  - target: embeddings\n    yaml: doctor-embeddings.yaml\n    setup_vars: [execution_mode]\n    allowed_flags: []\n    mutating: read-only\n    gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only provider and model-server status)\"\n    mcp_tools:\n      - mcp__mk_spec_memory__embedder_status\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"embedding provider status\"\n      - \"hf-local model-server health\"\n      - \"embedder_status diagnostics\"\n\n  - target: causal-graph\n    yaml: doctor-causal-graph.yaml\n    setup_vars: [execution_mode, intent, confidence_threshold]\n    allowed_flags: [\"--confidence-threshold=N\"]\n    mutating: add-only\n    gate3_location: \"<packet_scratch>/doctor-causal-graph-state.<timestamp>.json\"\n    mcp_tools:\n      - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_causal_stats\n      - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_drift_why\n      - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_search\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"causal edges drift\"\n      - \"causal graph doctor\"\n      - \"lineage coverage\"\n      - \"memory_causal_stats\"\n\n\n  - target: deep-loop\n    yaml: doctor-deep-loop.yaml\n    setup_vars: [execution_mode, intent, scope]\n    allowed_flags: [\"--scope=research|review|council|both|all\"]\n    mutating: add-only\n    gate3_location: \"<packet_scratch>/doctor-deep-loop-state.<timestamp>.json\"\n    mcp_tools: []\n    script_invocations:\n      - 'node .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/status.cjs --spec-folder \"{spec_folder}\" --loop-type \"{loop_type}\" --session-id \"{session_id}\"'\n      - 'node .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/query.cjs --spec-folder \"{spec_folder}\" --loop-type \"{loop_type}\" --session-id \"{session_id}\" --query-type \"{query_type}\"'\n      - 'node .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs --spec-folder \"{spec_folder}\" --loop-type \"{loop_type}\" --session-id \"{session_id}\"'\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"deep-loop coverage\"\n      - \"deep-loop graph rebuild\"\n      - \"deep-research convergence drift\"\n      - \"deep-review iteration graph\"\n\n  - target: skill-advisor\n    yaml: doctor-skill-advisor.yaml\n    setup_vars: [execution_mode, scope, skip_tests, dry_run]\n    allowed_flags: [\"--skip-tests\", \"--dry-run\", \"--scope=all|explicit|derived|lexical\"]\n    mutating: mutates\n    gate3_location: \"lib/scorer/lanes/*.ts + .opencode/skills/*/graph-metadata.json\"\n    mcp_tools:\n      - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__advisor_recommend\n      - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__advisor_status\n      - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__advisor_validate\n      - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__advisor_rebuild\n      - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__skill_graph_scan\n      - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__skill_graph_query\n      - mcp__mk_skill_advisor__skill_graph_status\n      - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_context\n      - mcp__mk_spec_memory__memory_search\n    script_invocations:\n      - 'Warm-only: probe the existing mk-skill-advisor IPC socket first; only when it answers, run node .opencode/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json --timeout-ms 500 --warm-only (the flag guarantees no daemon start; exit 75 = backend unavailable, retryable)'\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"skill advisor scoring\"\n      - \"skill advisor rebuild\"\n      - \"advisor recommendation tuning\"\n      - \"skill_graph scan\"\n\n  - target: skill-budget\n    yaml: doctor-skill-budget.yaml\n    setup_vars: [execution_mode, json_output, top_n, fail_over, project_ceiling]\n    allowed_flags: [\"--json\", \"--top-n=N\", \"--fail-over=N\", \"--project-ceiling=N\"]\n    mutating: read-only\n    gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only audit; no spec folder required)\"\n    mcp_tools: []   # script-only audit; no MCP calls\n    script_invocations:\n      - 'Warm-only: probe the existing mk-skill-advisor IPC socket first; only when it answers, run node .opencode/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json --timeout-ms 500 --warm-only (the flag guarantees no daemon start; exit 75 = backend unavailable, retryable)'\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"skill budget audit\"\n      - \"description budget check\"\n      - \"skill char count drift\"\n      - \"frontmatter description trim\"\n\n  - target: parent-skill\n    yaml: doctor-parent-skill.yaml\n    setup_vars: [execution_mode, parent_skill_dir]\n    allowed_flags: [\"--dir=<path>\"]\n    mutating: read-only\n    gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only structural audit; inspects a parent skill's graph-metadata/registry and reports, no writes)\"\n    mcp_tools: []   # script-only structural audit; no MCP calls\n    script_invocations:\n      # The fleet class gate runs first: it starts from SKILL.md, so it is the only\n      # check that can report a metadata file a root never wrote. The per-hub audit\n      # then goes deep on the one directory the operator named.\n      - 'node .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs'\n      - 'node .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs \"{parent_skill_dir}\"'\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"parent skill structure audit\"\n      - \"nested mode packet check\"\n      - \"mode-registry drift guard\"\n      - \"single graph-metadata identity\"\n      - \"skill root metadata contract\"\n      - \"missing skill json files\"\n\n  - target: skill-graph-freshness\n    yaml: doctor-skill-graph-freshness.yaml\n    setup_vars: [execution_mode]\n    allowed_flags: []\n    mutating: read-only\n    gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only diagnostic; 3-way diffs the skill-graph representations and reports drift, writes nothing)\"\n    mcp_tools: []   # script-only read; no MCP calls\n    script_invocations:\n      - 'node .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/skill-graph-freshness.cjs'\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"skill graph freshness\"\n      - \"skill-graph zombie ghost nodes\"\n      - \"compiled vs sqlite vs disk graph drift\"\n      - \"reindex staleness check\"\n\n  - target: fable-mode\n    yaml: doctor-fable-mode.yaml\n    setup_vars: [execution_mode, target_dir]\n    allowed_flags: [\"--dir=<path>\", \"--baseline=<path>\"]\n    mutating: read-only\n    gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only behavioral-metric diagnostic; reads deep-loop state + iteration markdown + baseline snapshot, writes no files)\"\n    mcp_tools: []   # script-only behavioral-metric read; no MCP calls\n    script_invocations:\n      - 'node .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/fable-mode-check.cjs --dir \"{target_dir}\"'\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"fable-5 behavioral metrics\"\n      - \"fable mode efficiency check\"\n      - \"tool text ratio diagnostic\"\n      - \"result-first opener metric\"\n\n  - target: runtime-mirrors\n    yaml: doctor-runtime-mirrors.yaml\n    setup_vars: [execution_mode]\n    allowed_flags: []\n    mutating: read-only\n    gate3_location: \"n/a (read-only mirror-parity diagnostic; every checker runs in its check-only form and writes nothing)\"\n    mcp_tools: []   # script-only mirror parity read; no MCP calls\n    script_invocations:\n      - 'node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs --check'\n      - 'node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/codex/sync-agents.cjs --check'\n      - 'node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/codex/sync-prompts.cjs --check'\n      - 'node .opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs'\n      - 'node .opencode/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs --check'\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"runtime mirror drift\"\n      - \"agent command mirror parity\"\n      - \"cursor devin codex sync check\"\n      - \"discovery mirror out of sync\"\n\n# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# COMPANION COMMAND ROUTES (/doctor:mcp sub-actions)\n# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# /doctor:mcp dispatches via its own command (.opencode/commands/doctor/mcp.md).\n# These rows are tracked here for advisor + CI visibility only — the mcp.md\n# command parses its own sub-action argv and dispatches to the right YAML.\n# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nmcp_subroutes:\n  - sub_action: install\n    yaml: doctor-mcp-install.yaml\n    allowed_flags: [\"--server <name>\", \"--runtime <name>\"]\n    mutating: mutates\n    gate3_location: \"MCP server install dirs + runtime config (.opencode.json / .claude/settings.json / )\"\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"MCP install\"\n      - \"MCP server fresh install\"\n      - \"reinstall MCP\"\n\n  - sub_action: debug\n    yaml: doctor-mcp-debug.yaml\n    allowed_flags: [\"--fix\", \"--server <name>\"]\n    mutating: mutates\n    gate3_location: \"MCP server logs + runtime config repair\"\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"MCP server broken\"\n      - \"MCP debug\"\n      - \"MCP connection failure\"\n      - \"mcp-doctor.sh\"\n\n# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# ORCHESTRATOR (NOT routed through /doctor — kept here for reference)\n# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# /doctor:update is a STANDALONE command at .opencode/commands/doctor/update.md.\n# It does NOT route through /doctor. This block exists for advisor visibility.\n# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nstandalone:\n  - command: /doctor:update\n    file: .opencode/commands/doctor/update.md\n    yaml: doctor-update.yaml\n    mutating: mutates\n    trigger_phrases:\n      - \"spec-kit version migration\"\n      - \"doctor update orchestrator\"\n      - \"rebuild all spec-kit databases\"\n      - \"doctor full sync\"","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":252,"totalLines":252,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml","time":{"start":1785216873825,"end":1785216873840}},"id":"prt_fa737b444001Au2e0vxzjo3pN2","sessionID":"ses_058ca7309ffeyGmRDCjINe2gtX","messageID":"msg_fa737a01e001ic0wUUMS0tZDpg"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785216874405,"sessionID":"ses_058ca7309ffeyGmRDCjINe2gtX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_eyIpr7jOyewvEWXxnNfZ7065","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":".opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs","limit":80},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: #!/usr/bin/env node\n2: 'use strict';\n3: \n4: // Read-only /doctor diagnostic for agent-roster coverage across runtimes.\n5: //\n6: // WHY THIS EXISTS: the same agent roster is exposed to five runtimes, but each\n7: // discovers agents from its own path with its own file shape. Cursor and Devin\n8: // are served by symlinks into the canonical Claude tree, while OpenCode and Codex\n9: // keep independently-authored files in their own dialects. Nothing in the\n10: // runtimes themselves notices when a newly-added agent reaches only some of\n11: // them -- it simply goes missing where it was never mirrored, silently, with no\n12: // error at dispatch time. This check makes that omission loud.\n13: //\n14: // Never writes; a /doctor run is read-only by contract.\n15: // Exit 0 when every runtime covers the canonical roster, 1 on drift, 2 on error.\n16: \n17: const fs = require('node:fs');\n18: const path = require('node:path');\n19: \n20: const REPO = path.resolve(__dirname, '../../../..');\n21: \n22: // The Claude tree is canonical: it holds the full agent bodies that the Cursor\n23: // and Devin mirrors symlink back to.\n24: const CANONICAL_DIR = path.join(REPO, '.claude/agents');\n25: \n26: // Symlink mirrors must resolve to the canonical file, not merely exist -- a real\n27: // file here would be a silent fork that drifts on the next canonical edit.\n28: const LINKED = [\n29:   { id: 'cursor', rel: (n) => `.cursor/agents/${n}.md`, ext: '.md' },\n30:   // Devin is the one directory-per-agent surface, so its entries carry no extension.\n31:   { id: 'devin', rel: (n) => `.devin/agents/${n}/AGENT.md`, ext: null },\n32: ];\n33: \n34: // Independently-authored surfaces: a different frontmatter dialect is expected,\n35: // so only presence is checked, never content equality.\n36: const AUTHORED = [\n37:   { id: 'opencode', rel: (n) => `.opencode/agents/${n}.md`, ext: '.md' },\n38:   { id: 'codex', rel: (n) => `.codex/agents/${n}.toml`, ext: '.toml' },\n39:   // Pi agents are generated real files (sync-agents-pi.cjs), not symlinks.\n40:   { id: 'pi', rel: (n) => `.pi/agents/${n}.md`, ext: '.md' },\n41: ];\n42: \n43: function canonicalRoster() {\n44:   return fs\n45:     .readdirSync(CANONICAL_DIR)\n46:     .filter((f) => f.endsWith('.md'))\n47:     .map((f) => f.slice(0, -3))\n48:     .sort();\n49: }\n50: \n51: function checkLinked(surface, name) {\n52:   const abs = path.join(REPO, surface.rel(name));\n53:   let st;\n54:   try {\n55:     st = fs.lstatSync(abs);\n56:   } catch {\n57:     return { ok: false, why: 'missing' };\n58:   }\n59:   if (!st.isSymbolicLink()) return { ok: false, why: 'not a symlink (silent fork risk)' };\n60:   if (!fs.existsSync(abs)) return { ok: false, why: 'broken symlink' };\n61:   const want = path.join(CANONICAL_DIR, `${name}.md`);\n62:   if (fs.realpathSync(abs) !== fs.realpathSync(want)) return { ok: false, why: 'resolves elsewhere' };\n63:   return { ok: true };\n64: }\n65: \n66: // A mirror with no canonical source is drift in the other direction: it keeps\n67: // serving an agent that the roster no longer defines.\n68: function orphans(surface, roster) {\n69:   // Devin nests one directory per agent; the others are flat files, so the\n70:   // agent name is the entry minus that surface's own extension.\n71:   const dir = surface.ext === null\n72:     ? path.join(REPO, '.devin/agents')\n73:     : path.join(REPO, path.dirname(surface.rel('x')));\n74:   if (!fs.existsSync(dir)) return [];\n75:   const known = new Set(roster);\n76:   return fs\n77:     .readdirSync(dir)\n78:     .filter((e) => !e.startsWith('.'))\n79:     // Only entries shaped like an agent count; sibling READMEs and stray notes\n80:     // are not drift, and flagging them would train the reader to ignore output.\n\n(Showing lines 1-80 of 126. Use offset=81 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n'use strict';\n\n// Read-only /doctor diagnostic for agent-roster coverage across runtimes.\n//\n// WHY THIS EXISTS: the same agent roster is exposed to five runtimes, but each\n// discovers agents from its own path with its own file shape. Cursor and Devin\n// are served by symlinks into the canonical Claude tree, while OpenCode and Codex\n// keep independently-authored files in their own dialects. Nothing in the\n// runtimes themselves notices when a newly-added agent reaches only some of\n// them -- it simply goes missing where it was never mirrored, silently, with no\n// error at dispatch time. This check makes that omission loud.\n//\n// Never writes; a /doctor run is read-only by contract.\n// Exit 0 when every runtime covers the canonical roster, 1 on drift, 2 on error.\n\nconst fs = require('node:fs');\nconst path = require('node:path');\n\nconst REPO = path.resolve(__dirname, '../../../..');","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs","text":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n'use strict';\n\n// Read-only /doctor diagnostic for agent-roster coverage across runtimes.\n//\n// WHY THIS EXISTS: the same agent roster is exposed to five runtimes, but each\n// discovers agents from its own path with its own file shape. Cursor and Devin\n// are served by symlinks into the canonical Claude tree, while OpenCode and Codex\n// keep independently-authored files in their own dialects. Nothing in the\n// runtimes themselves notices when a newly-added agent reaches only some of\n// them -- it simply goes missing where it was never mirrored, silently, with no\n// error at dispatch time. This check makes that omission loud.\n//\n// Never writes; a /doctor run is read-only by contract.\n// Exit 0 when every runtime covers the canonical roster, 1 on drift, 2 on error.\n\nconst fs = require('node:fs');\nconst path = require('node:path');\n\nconst REPO = path.resolve(__dirname, '../../../..');\n\n// The Claude tree is canonical: it holds the full agent bodies that the Cursor\n// and Devin mirrors symlink back to.\nconst CANONICAL_DIR = path.join(REPO, '.claude/agents');\n\n// Symlink mirrors must resolve to the canonical file, not merely exist -- a real\n// file here would be a silent fork that drifts on the next canonical edit.\nconst LINKED = [\n  { id: 'cursor', rel: (n) => `.cursor/agents/${n}.md`, ext: '.md' },\n  // Devin is the one directory-per-agent surface, so its entries carry no extension.\n  { id: 'devin', rel: (n) => `.devin/agents/${n}/AGENT.md`, ext: null },\n];\n\n// Independently-authored surfaces: a different frontmatter dialect is expected,\n// so only presence is checked, never content equality.\nconst AUTHORED = [\n  { id: 'opencode', rel: (n) => `.opencode/agents/${n}.md`, ext: '.md' },\n  { id: 'codex', rel: (n) => `.codex/agents/${n}.toml`, ext: '.toml' },\n  // Pi agents are generated real files (sync-agents-pi.cjs), not symlinks.\n  { id: 'pi', rel: (n) => `.pi/agents/${n}.md`, ext: '.md' },\n];\n\nfunction canonicalRoster() {\n  return fs\n    .readdirSync(CANONICAL_DIR)\n    .filter((f) => f.endsWith('.md'))\n    .map((f) => f.slice(0, -3))\n    .sort();\n}\n\nfunction checkLinked(surface, name) {\n  const abs = path.join(REPO, surface.rel(name));\n  let st;\n  try {\n    st = fs.lstatSync(abs);\n  } catch {\n    return { ok: false, why: 'missing' };\n  }\n  if (!st.isSymbolicLink()) return { ok: false, why: 'not a symlink (silent fork risk)' };\n  if (!fs.existsSync(abs)) return { ok: false, why: 'broken symlink' };\n  const want = path.join(CANONICAL_DIR, `${name}.md`);\n  if (fs.realpathSync(abs) !== fs.realpathSync(want)) return { ok: false, why: 'resolves elsewhere' };\n  return { ok: true };\n}\n\n// A mirror with no canonical source is drift in the other direction: it keeps\n// serving an agent that the roster no longer defines.\nfunction orphans(surface, roster) {\n  // Devin nests one directory per agent; the others are flat files, so the\n  // agent name is the entry minus that surface's own extension.\n  const dir = surface.ext === null\n    ? path.join(REPO, '.devin/agents')\n    : path.join(REPO, path.dirname(surface.rel('x')));\n  if (!fs.existsSync(dir)) return [];\n  const known = new Set(roster);\n  return fs\n    .readdirSync(dir)\n    .filter((e) => !e.startsWith('.'))\n    // Only entries shaped like an agent count; sibling READMEs and stray notes\n    // are not drift, and flagging them would train the reader to ignore output.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":80,"totalLines":126,"truncated":true}},"title":".opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs","time":{"start":1785216874395,"end":1785216874403}},"id":"prt_fa737b5ba001UOnmSqI5qRky5h","sessionID":"ses_058ca7309ffeyGmRDCjINe2gtX","messageID":"msg_fa737a01e001ic0wUUMS0tZDpg"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785216874947,"sessionID":"ses_058ca7309ffeyGmRDCjINe2gtX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_02_LVOwUXXkJSa4DrowvjQ73320","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":".opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs","limit":80},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: #!/usr/bin/env node\n2: // ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗\n3: // ║ parent-skill-check Doctor Script                                         ║\n4: // ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝\n5: 'use strict';\n6: \n7: /**\n8:  * parent-skill-check.cjs — read-only structural audit for the canonical\n9:  * \"parent hub with nested packets\" pattern (the ONE method).\n10:  *\n11:  * A parent hub keeps ONE advisor identity at the hub and routes to N\n12:  * non-discoverable packets through a declarative mode-registry, with a\n13:  * hub-router describing how a prompt picks/bundles modes. Every packet is a\n14:  * modes[] entry with a required packetKind discriminator (workflow | surface | transport);\n15:  * surface packets are read-only, advisor-invisible evidence bases. Deep-loop's\n16:  * 3-tier machinery is expressed as named `extensions` that activate in-place\n17:  * fields, so the checks are family-agnostic: they run FULL on every hub and\n18:  * only demand extension-specific fields when the hub declares that extension.\n19:  *\n20:  * Usage:\n21:  *   node parent-skill-check.cjs [parent-skill-dir]\n22:  *   PARENT_HUB_CHECK_STRICT=0 node parent-skill-check.cjs [dir]   # 5-9 as WARN (WIP opt-out)\n23:  *\n24:  * The canon checks 5-9 (hub-router validity, registry/directory reverse\n25:  * consistency, changelog shape, description.json, playbook + benchmark\n26:  * baseline) FAIL by default now that every hub carries the canon fields;\n27:  * PARENT_HUB_CHECK_STRICT=0 downgrades them to advisory WARN for a\n28:  * work-in-progress hub still being scaffolded.\n29:  *\n30:  * Exit codes:\n31:  *   0  — every hard invariant passed (warnings allowed)\n32:  *   1  — at least one hard invariant failed\n33:  *   2  — target directory missing or unreadable\n34:  */\n35: \n36: const fs = require('fs');\n37: const path = require('path');\n38: const { execFileSync } = require('child_process');\n39: \n40: // The checker's targets and helper paths are repo-root-relative. Resolve them against the\n41: // repo root (the directory containing .opencode), not process.cwd() — otherwise rule 4a and\n42: // the target dir resolve against wherever the checker is invoked, so a non-root working\n43: // directory silently false-FAILs the drift-guard check or misses the hub entirely.\n44: function findRepoRoot(start) {\n45:   let dir = start;\n46:   while (dir !== path.dirname(dir)) {\n47:     if (fs.existsSync(path.join(dir, '.opencode'))) return dir;\n48:     dir = path.dirname(dir);\n49:   }\n50:   return path.resolve(start, '../../../..');\n51: }\n52: const REPO_ROOT = findRepoRoot(__dirname);\n53: \n54: // Families the skill-graph compiler accepts for a discoverable identity.\n55: // Mirrors ALLOWED_FAMILIES in skill_graph_compiler.py / skill-graph-db.ts;\n56: // a family outside this set makes the hub undiscoverable.\n57: const ALLOWED_FAMILIES = ['cli', 'mcp', 'sk-code', 'sk-hub', 'deep-loop', 'sk-util', 'system'];\n58: \n59: // The advisor reaches a mode through one of these classes; the registry\n60: // declares it per mode so the projection maps stay auditable. 'metadata' is the\n61: // default for new hubs and for every surface packet (advisor-invisible).\n62: const VALID_ROUTING_CLASSES = ['lexical', 'alias-fold', 'metadata', 'command-bridge'];\n63: \n64: // The graph-backed convergence loop keys consumed by a runtime-loop backend.\n65: // null is the explicit value for host/adapter modes; only demanded when the hub\n66: // declares the runtime-loop extension.\n67: const VALID_RUNTIME_LOOP_TYPES = ['research', 'review', 'council', null];\n68: \n69: // Read-only tool set a surface packet's toolSurface.allowed must stay within.\n70: const SURFACE_ALLOWED_TOOLS = ['Read', 'Bash', 'Grep', 'Glob'];\n71: const SURFACE_FORBIDDEN_TOOLS = ['Write', 'Edit', 'Task'];\n72: \n73: // Directories allowed at a hub root without being a registered packet.\n74: const DIRECTORY_ALLOWLIST = new Set([\n75:   'shared', 'changelog', 'benchmark',\n76:   'manual-testing-playbook', 'feature-catalog',\n77:   'references', 'assets', 'node_modules', 'scripts', 'templates', 'dist', 'runtime', 'styles',\n78: ]);\n79: \n80: // The deep-loop reference drift-guard. A hub with lexical/alias-fold modes must\n\n(Showing lines 1-80 of 1290. Use offset=81 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n// ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗\n// ║ parent-skill-check Doctor Script                                         ║\n// ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝\n'use strict';\n\n/**\n * parent-skill-check.cjs — read-only structural audit for the canonical\n * \"parent hub with nested packets\" pattern (the ONE method).\n *\n * A parent hub keeps ONE advisor identity at the hub and routes to N\n * non-discoverable packets through a declarative mode-registry, with a\n * hub-router describing how a prompt picks/bundles modes. Every packet is a\n * modes[] entry with a required packetKind discriminator (workflow | surface | transport);\n * surface packets are read-only, advisor-invisible evidence bases. Deep-loop's\n * 3-tier machinery is expressed as named `extensions` that activate in-place\n * fields, so the checks are family-agnostic: they run FULL on every hub and\n * only demand extension-specific fields when the hub declares that extension.\n *\n * Usage:","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs","text":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n// ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗\n// ║ parent-skill-check Doctor Script                                         ║\n// ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝\n'use strict';\n\n/**\n * parent-skill-check.cjs — read-only structural audit for the canonical\n * \"parent hub with nested packets\" pattern (the ONE method).\n *\n * A parent hub keeps ONE advisor identity at the hub and routes to N\n * non-discoverable packets through a declarative mode-registry, with a\n * hub-router describing how a prompt picks/bundles modes. Every packet is a\n * modes[] entry with a required packetKind discriminator (workflow | surface | transport);\n * surface packets are read-only, advisor-invisible evidence bases. Deep-loop's\n * 3-tier machinery is expressed as named `extensions` that activate in-place\n * fields, so the checks are family-agnostic: they run FULL on every hub and\n * only demand extension-specific fields when the hub declares that extension.\n *\n * Usage:\n *   node parent-skill-check.cjs [parent-skill-dir]\n *   PARENT_HUB_CHECK_STRICT=0 node parent-skill-check.cjs [dir]   # 5-9 as WARN (WIP opt-out)\n *\n * The canon checks 5-9 (hub-router validity, registry/directory reverse\n * consistency, changelog shape, description.json, playbook + benchmark\n * baseline) FAIL by default now that every hub carries the canon fields;\n * PARENT_HUB_CHECK_STRICT=0 downgrades them to advisory WARN for a\n * work-in-progress hub still being scaffolded.\n *\n * Exit codes:\n *   0  — every hard invariant passed (warnings allowed)\n *   1  — at least one hard invariant failed\n *   2  — target directory missing or unreadable\n */\n\nconst fs = require('fs');\nconst path = require('path');\nconst { execFileSync } = require('child_process');\n\n// The checker's targets and helper paths are repo-root-relative. Resolve them against the\n// repo root (the directory containing .opencode), not process.cwd() — otherwise rule 4a and\n// the target dir resolve against wherever the checker is invoked, so a non-root working\n// directory silently false-FAILs the drift-guard check or misses the hub entirely.\nfunction findRepoRoot(start) {\n  let dir = start;\n  while (dir !== path.dirname(dir)) {\n    if (fs.existsSync(path.join(dir, '.opencode'))) return dir;\n    dir = path.dirname(dir);\n  }\n  return path.resolve(start, '../../../..');\n}\nconst REPO_ROOT = findRepoRoot(__dirname);\n\n// Families the skill-graph compiler accepts for a discoverable identity.\n// Mirrors ALLOWED_FAMILIES in skill_graph_compiler.py / skill-graph-db.ts;\n// a family outside this set makes the hub undiscoverable.\nconst ALLOWED_FAMILIES = ['cli', 'mcp', 'sk-code', 'sk-hub', 'deep-loop', 'sk-util', 'system'];\n\n// The advisor reaches a mode through one of these classes; the registry\n// declares it per mode so the projection maps stay auditable. 'metadata' is the\n// default for new hubs and for every surface packet (advisor-invisible).\nconst VALID_ROUTING_CLASSES = ['lexical', 'alias-fold', 'metadata', 'command-bridge'];\n\n// The graph-backed convergence loop keys consumed by a runtime-loop backend.\n// null is the explicit value for host/adapter modes; only demanded when the hub\n// declares the runtime-loop extension.\nconst VALID_RUNTIME_LOOP_TYPES = ['research', 'review', 'council', null];\n\n// Read-only tool set a surface packet's toolSurface.allowed must stay within.\nconst SURFACE_ALLOWED_TOOLS = ['Read', 'Bash', 'Grep', 'Glob'];\nconst SURFACE_FORBIDDEN_TOOLS = ['Write', 'Edit', 'Task'];\n\n// Directories allowed at a hub root without being a registered packet.\nconst DIRECTORY_ALLOWLIST = new Set([\n  'shared', 'changelog', 'benchmark',\n  'manual-testing-playbook', 'feature-catalog',\n  'references', 'assets', 'node_modules', 'scripts', 'templates', 'dist', 'runtime', 'styles',\n]);\n\n// The deep-loop reference drift-guard. A hub with lexical/alias-fold modes must","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":80,"totalLines":1290,"truncated":true}},"title":".opencode/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs","time":{"start":1785216874935,"end":1785216874946}},"id":"prt_fa737b7fd001LJizE645AFcYUp","sessionID":"ses_058ca7309ffeyGmRDCjINe2gtX","messageID":"msg_fa737a01e001ic0wUUMS0tZDpg"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785216875218,"sessionID":"ses_058ca7309ffeyGmRDCjINe2gtX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_03_h7OHpj34oMqfpIPkIJde2672","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":".opencode/agents/orchestrate.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/agents/orchestrate.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: name: orchestrate\n3: description: \"Senior orchestration agent: task decomposition, delegation, quality eval, delivery synthesis.\"\n4: mode: primary\n5: temperature: 0.1\n6: permission:\n7:   read: allow\n8:   list: deny\n9:   glob: deny\n10:   grep: deny\n11:   write: deny\n12:   edit: deny\n13:   bash: deny\n14:   patch: deny\n15:   webfetch: deny\n16: ---\n17: \n18: # The Orchestrator: Senior Task Commander\n19: \n20: You are **THE SENIOR ORCHESTRATION AGENT** with **FULL AUTHORITY** over:\n21: \n22: - **Task Decomposition**: Break complex requests into discrete, delegatable tasks\n23: - **Strategic Delegation**: Assign tasks with explicit skills, scope, and success criteria\n24: - **Quality Evaluation**: Accept, reject, or request revision of sub-agent outputs\n25: - **Conflict Resolution**: Resolve contradictions between parallel workstreams\n26: - **Unified Synthesis**: Merge outputs into single authoritative delivery\n27: \n28: You are the **single point of accountability**. The user receives ONE coherent response from you, not fragments from multiple agents.\n29: \n30: **Path Convention**: Use only `.opencode/agents/*.md` as the canonical runtime path reference.\n31: \n32: **Runtime Directory Resolution**: OpenCode profile reads `.opencode/agents/`; Claude profile reads `.claude/agents/`. Choose the active runtime directory once per workflow and keep dispatches within it.\n33: \n34: **Agent I/O Contract**: When helpful, include an `AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1` header in delegated prompts and accept optional `AGENT_IO_RESULT v1` envelopes appended to native agent output. The contract is advisory only; missing headers or envelopes are never a rejection reason.\n35: \n36: **Hook-Injected Advisor Context**: Treat hook-injected skill-advisor recommendations as routing hints only. They never override explicit user instructions, active command workflow, scope gates, runtime permissions, agent boundaries, or required skill loading. If advisor context conflicts with the dispatch prompt or verified local files, prefer the dispatch prompt plus file evidence and report the conflict.\n37: \n38: **CRITICAL**: You primarily orchestrate via the `task` tool. You MAY use `read` to load agent definitions or command specs needed for correct dispatch, but you MUST NOT perform implementation or codebase exploration directly. Execution work remains delegated to sub-agents.\n39: \n40: ---\n41: \n42: ## 0. ILLEGAL NESTING (HARD BLOCK)\n43: \n44: Single-hop delegation only, max depth 2. Full rules, depth table, and legal/illegal chain examples: §2 Nesting Depth Protocol.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 1. CORE WORKFLOW\n49: \n50: 1. **RECEIVE** → Parse intent, scope, constraints\n51: 2. **CHECK GATES** → Enforce Spec Folder & Research-First Requirements\n52: 3. **SCAN** → Identify relevant skills, commands, agents\n53: 4. **DECOMPOSE** → Structure tasks with scope/output/success; identify parallel vs sequential\n54: 5. **CWB CHECK** → Calculate context budget, plan collection waves (see §8)\n55: 6. **DELEGATE** → Dispatch within wave limits; enforce output size constraints (§8)\n56: 7. **EVALUATE** → Quality gates: accuracy, completeness, consistency\n57: 8. **HANDLE FAILURES** → Retry → Reassign → Escalate to user\n58: 9. **SYNTHESIZE** → Merge into unified voice with inline attribution\n59: 10. **DELIVER** → Present final response; flag ambiguities and exclusions\n60: \n61: Steps 1-4 and 9-10 run linear; step 5 (CWB CHECK) branches to parallel or sequential dispatch (step 6) based on task dependencies; step 7 (EVALUATE) branches to step 9 on pass (score ≥ 70) or step 8 (HANDLE FAILURES → retry step 6) on fail.\n62: \n63: ---\n64: \n65: ## 2. ROUTING SCAN\n66: \n67: ### Agent Routing & Nesting\n68: \n69: ### Agent Selection (Priority Order)\n70: \n71: | Priority | Task Type                                                                 | Agent                  | Tier | Skills                                                                            | subagent_type |\n72: | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |\n73: | 1        | ALL codebase exploration, file search, pattern discovery, context loading | `@context`             | LEAF | Memory tools, Glob, Grep, Read                                                    | `\"general\"`   |\n74: | 2        | Evidence / iterative investigation                                        | `@deep-research`       | LEAF | `system-spec-kit`, `deep-research`                                             | `\"general\"`   |\n75: | 3        | Multi-strategy planning and architecture synthesis                        | `@ai-council`         | LEAF | Multi-lens planning rubric (planning-only)                                        | `\"general\"`   |\n76: | 4        | `/create:*` documentation and component creation command execution        | `@markdown`        | LEAF | `sk-doc`, `system-spec-kit` when spec tracking applies                            | `\"general\"`   |\n77: | 5        | Code review / security                                                    | `@review`              | LEAF | `sk-code` code-review mode (findings-first baseline + router-selected surface evidence)    | `\"general\"`   |\n78: | 6        | Deep-loop iterative code-audit pass (`/deep:review` only — explicit deep-loop convergence request, not a one-shot review) | `@deep-review` | LEAF | `system-spec-kit`, `deep-review` | `\"general\"` |\n79: | 7        | Implementation / testing                                                  | `@code`                | LEAF | `sk-code` (stack-agnostic; sk-code performs detection at dispatch time); orchestrator dispatches `@review` separately for formal review | `\"general\"`   |\n80: | 8        | Debugging when `failure_count >= 3` — workflow surfaces a prompted offer; user opts in via Task tool. Never auto-dispatched. | `@debug`               | LEAF | Code analysis tools                                                               | `\"general\"`   |\n81: \n82: ### Nesting Depth Protocol (NDP)\n83: \n84: This Copilot profile enforces **single-hop delegation**. Nested sub-agent dispatch is illegal.\n85: \n86: #### Agent Tier Classification\n87: \n88: | Tier             | Dispatch Authority               | Who                                                                                   |\n89: | ---------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n90: | **ORCHESTRATOR** | Can dispatch LEAF agents         | Top-level orchestrator only                                                           |\n91: | **LEAF**         | MUST NOT dispatch any sub-agents | @context, @code, @markdown, @ai-council, @review, @debug, @deep-research, @deep-review |\n92: \n93: #### Absolute Depth Rules\n94: \n95: **Maximum depth: 2 levels** (depth counter 0, 1). No agent at depth 1 may dispatch further.\n96: \n97: | Depth  | Who Can Be Here                 | Can Dispatch?          |\n98: | ------ | ------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n99: | **0**  | Orchestrator only               | Yes — LEAF agents only |\n100: | **1**  | Any agent dispatched by depth-0 | **NO** — all are LEAF  |\n101: | **2+** | **FORBIDDEN**                   | N/A                    |\n102: \n103: #### Depth Counting Rules\n104: \n105: 1. The top-level orchestrator is always **depth 0**\n106: 2. Each dispatch increments depth by 1: `child_depth = parent_depth + 1`\n107: 3. Parallel dispatches at the same level share the same depth (siblings, not children)\n108: 4. Every dispatch MUST include `Depth: N` so the receiving agent knows its position\n109: \n110: #### ✅ Legal Chains\n111: \n112: ```\n113: LEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @review(1)\n114: LEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @context(1) + @review(1)            [parallel at depth 1]\n115: LEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @general(1)\n116: ```\n117: \n118: #### ❌ Illegal Chains\n119: \n120: ```\n121: ILLEGAL: Orch(0) → @context(1) → @review(2)\n122: ILLEGAL: Orch(0) → @review(1) → @general(2)\n123: ILLEGAL: Orch(0) → Sub-Orch(1) → @leaf(2)\n124: ```\n125: \n126: **For `/deep:*` requests, resolve the target leaf agent directly from the Priority table above (registry-backed) and dispatch that leaf at depth 1.** Do not route a `/deep:*` request through any intermediary primary agent — dispatching a router at depth 1 that then dispatches the leaf at depth 2 would create an illegal `Orch(0) → intermediary(1) → @leaf(2)` chain.\n127: \n128: #### 🔒 LEAF Enforcement Instruction\n129: \n130: When dispatching ANY non-orchestrator agent, append this to the Task prompt:\n131: \n132: > **NESTING CONSTRAINT:** You are a LEAF agent at depth [N]. Nested dispatch is illegal. You MUST NOT dispatch sub-agents or use the Task tool to create sub-tasks. Execute your work directly using your available tools. If you cannot complete the task alone, return what you have and escalate to the orchestrator.\n133: \n134: ### 🔒 Agent Loading Protocol (MANDATORY)\n135: \n136: **BEFORE dispatching any custom agent via the Task tool, you MUST:**\n137: 1. **READ** the agent's definition file (see File column below)\n138: 2. **INCLUDE** the agent file's content in the Task prompt (or a focused summary for large files)\n139: 3. **SET** `subagent_type: \"general\"` (all custom agents use the general subagent type)\n140: \n141: **Why:** Agent definition files contain specialized instructions, templates, enforcement rules, and quality standards that differentiate them from generic agents. Telling a general agent \"you are @debug\" is NOT equivalent to loading `debug.md` — it loses the specialized debugging workflow and verification discipline.\n142: \n143: **Exception:** If the agent file was already loaded in a prior dispatch within the same session AND no context compaction has occurred, you may reference it rather than re-reading it.\n144: \n145: ### Prompt/Agent Consistency Guard (MANDATORY)\n146: \n147: Before every Task tool dispatch, compare the selected route, loaded agent definition, and prompt body:\n148: \n149: 1. The `Agent:` line in the task prompt MUST match the route selected in §2 Agent Selection.\n150: 2. The loaded `Agent Definition` MUST be the file for that same agent.\n151: 3. `subagent_type: \"general\"` is only the runtime wrapper; it does not make a mismatched prompt safe.\n152: 4. If a prompt says `Agent: @code`, the selected route MUST be @code, `.opencode/agents/code.md` MUST be loaded, and the work MUST pass @code's component-authoring gate.\n153: 5. If any field disagrees, STOP before dispatch and rewrite the task package; do not send a general worker with contradictory embedded agent instructions.\n154: \n155: ### Agent Files\n156: \n157: | Agent     | File                          | Notes                                                                                  |\n158: | --------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n159: | @context  | `.opencode/agents/context.md`  | Sub-agent with direct retrieval only. Routes ALL exploration tasks                     |\n160: | @markdown | `.opencode/agents/markdown.md` | Template-first documentation executor for `/create:*`, scoped markdown, and spec-doc authoring |\n161: | @deep-research | `.opencode/agents/deep-research.md` | LEAF agent; iterative autonomous research loop with externalized state          |\n162: | @deep-review | `.opencode/agents/deep-review.md` | LEAF agent; iterative code-audit dimension pass, P0/P1/P2 findings, JSONL state |\n163: | @ai-council | `.opencode/agents/ai-council.md` | Planning-only multi-strategy architect (max 3 strategies). Post-dispatch responsibility: when @orchestrate dispatches at Depth 1, run `node .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-ai-council/scripts/persist-artifacts.cjs <packet>` after the LEAF returns to persist `ai-council/` artifacts (see ai-council persistence protocol). If the LEAF's returned report shows `max_rounds` reached without a converged plan (check `current_round >= max_rounds` in `ai-council-config.json`), add `--not-converged` to that invocation — the helper cannot detect max-round exhaustion on its own and defaults to `convergence:true` otherwise. |\n164: | @review   | `.opencode/agents/review.md`   | Codebase-agnostic quality scoring                                                      |\n165: | @debug    | `.opencode/agents/debug.md`    | Isolated by design (no conversation context)                                           |\n166: | @code     | `.opencode/agents/code.md`     | Application-code LEAF; sk-code stack delegation; D3 convention-floor caller-restriction (`Depth: 1` marker required); fail-closed verify |\n167: \n168: > **Note**: ALL exploration tasks route through `@context` exclusively. @context executes retrieval directly (no nested sub-agent dispatch).\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 3. TASK DECOMPOSITION & DISPATCH\n173: \n174: ### Task Format\n175: \n176: For **EVERY** task delegation, use this structured format:\n177: \n178: ```\n179: TASK #N: [Descriptive Title]\n180: ├─ Complexity: [low | medium | high]\n181: ├─ Objective: [WHY this task exists]\n182: ├─ Scope: [Explicit inclusions AND exclusions]\n183: ├─ Boundary: [What this agent MUST NOT do]\n184: ├─ Agent: @code | @context | @markdown | @deep-research | @deep-review | @ai-council | @review | @debug\n185: ├─ Deep Route: [for deep routes only: mode=<workflowMode>; target_agent=@<agent>; execution=<single_iteration|loop|session>; source_of_truth=.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/mode-registry.json | none]\n186: ├─ Subagent Type: \"general\" (ALL dispatches use \"general\" — exploration routes through @context)\n187: ├─ Agent Definition: [.opencode/agents/<name>.md — MUST be read and included in prompt | \"built-in\" for @general]\n188: ├─ Skills: [Specific skills the agent should use]\n189: ├─ Output Format: [Structured format with example]\n190: ├─ Output Size: [full | summary-only (30 lines) | minimal (3 lines)] ← CWB §8\n191: ├─ Write To: [file path for detailed findings | \"none\"] ← CWB §8\n192: ├─ Success: [Measurable criteria with evidence requirements]\n193: ├─ Depends: [Task numbers that must complete first | \"none\"]\n194: ├─ Branch: [Optional conditional routing - see Conditional Branching below]\n195: ├─ Depth: [0|1] — current dispatch depth (§2 NDP). Agent tier: [ORCHESTRATOR|LEAF]\n196: ├─ Agent I/O: [optional `AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1` header | none]\n197: ├─ Scoped Pre-Execution: [diagnosis_crosses_agents=<true|false>; change_class=<docs|typo|ordinary|api|schema|integration|other>; complexity=<low|medium|high>]\n198: ├─ Handoff: [none | debug_to_implement: root_cause, target_files, fix_recommendations, confidence]\n199: ├─ Review Focus: [none | reviewer_focus=<high-risk files/areas>; self_assessed_quality=<optional short producer note>]\n200: ├─ Spec Drift: [none | surface `spec_drift` from @code in synthesis and handover planning]\n201: ├─ Pre-Mortem: [none for low | risk + 2-3 failure modes + assumptions for medium/high]\n202: ├─ Scale: [1-agent | 2-4 agents | 10+ agents]\n203: └─ Est. Tool Calls: [N] ([breakdown]) → [Single agent | Split: M agents × ~K calls] (§8 TCB)\n204: ```\n205: \n206: **`Deep Route:` resolution rule (registry-backed, not judgment):** for any of the 4 `@deep-*`/`@ai-council` agents, `mode`, `target_agent`, and `execution` MUST be looked up directly from that agent's matching entry in `.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/mode-registry.json` (`workflowMode`, `agent`, and the entry's own execution semantics) — never inferred or guessed. A reviewer must be able to verify correctness by diffing the emitted `Deep Route:` line against the registry entry. If the registry has no matching entry for the requested mode, stop before dispatch rather than fabricating a route.\n207: \n208: Optional dispatch header:\n209: \n210: ```text\n211: AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1\n212: schema_version: agent-io/v1\n213: dispatch_id: <stable id or none>\n214: agent: @[agent]\n215: task_type: explore | implement | review | debug | document | plan | research\n216: task_definition: <one-line task summary>\n217: context_snapshot: none | included | one-shot\n218: read_directives: none | packet-first | codebase-first | exact-paths\n219: contract: advisory\n220: context_package: none | included\n221: expected_result: native | agent_io_result\n222: handoff: none | debug_to_implement\n223: pre_execution: none | scoped\n224: reviewer_focus: none | <comma-separated high-risk files, modules, or concerns>\n225: self_assessed_quality: none | high | medium | low | <short producer confidence note>\n226: ```\n227: \n228: The loaded agent definition remains authoritative. If `AGENT_IO_RESULT v1` is present in a child result, use it as a routing hint only after the native report passes §5 output verification. If it is absent, parse the existing markdown contract and continue; do not reject a child result solely because the advisory result envelope is absent.\n229: \n230: ### Review Focus and Drift Hints\n231: \n232: Use `reviewer_focus` only when the planner can name a high-risk file, module, behavior, or assumption worth earlier review attention. Omit it for ordinary work. When present, include it in @review dispatches so @review can prioritize reads and evidence; do not alter the review threshold or ask for findings without normal evidence.\n233: \n234: If @code returns a `spec_drift` or `update_recommended` block, surface the reason and affected docs in synthesis and handover planning. Do not auto-edit spec docs from that hint. A hard contradiction still routes through Logic-Sync and the native @code escalation, not through `spec_drift`.\n235: \n236: ### Scoped Pre-Execution Predicates\n237: \n238: The orchestrator owns these predicates and evaluates them before dispatch. They are advisory and additive; absence of a header in ordinary or legacy work is not a rejection reason.\n239: \n240: | Gate | Predicate | Required Dispatch Behavior | Skip Behavior |\n241: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n242: | Typed debug handoff | `diagnosis_crosses_agents == true` | Preserve the @debug handoff fields (`root_cause`, `target_files`, `fix_recommendations`, `confidence`) when dispatching @code for a diagnosis-based surgical fix. | If no debug diagnosis crosses agents, omit the handoff entirely. |\n243: | Boundary contract-first | `change_class in {api, schema, integration}` | Identify a contract, boundary test, or executable acceptance check before production edits. | Docs, typo, and ordinary changes do not trigger this gate. |\n244: | Pre-mortem | `complexity in {medium, high}` | Add risk level, 2-3 likely failure modes, and key assumptions to the task. | Low-complexity work omits the pre-mortem. |\n245: \n246: For a debug-to-implementation crossing, carry this optional handoff block inside the @code dispatch prompt:\n247: \n248: ```text\n249: AGENT_IO_HANDOFF v1\n250: schema_version: agent-io/v1\n251: handoff_type: debug_to_implement\n252: source_agent: @debug\n253: target_agent: @code\n254: root_cause: <from @debug, evidence-backed>\n255: target_files: <from @debug, recommendations only>\n256: fix_recommendations: <from @debug>\n257: confidence: high | medium | low\n258: ```\n259: \n260: If any handoff field is missing in a diagnosis-based @code dispatch, instruct @code to return `BLOCKED` with low confidence rather than guessing. Legacy debug reports outside this crossing should be treated as warning-only context that requires manual verification.\n261: \n262: ### Pre-Delegation Reasoning (PDR)\n263: \n264: **MANDATORY** before EVERY Task tool dispatch:\n265: \n266: ```\n267: PRE-DELEGATION REASONING [Task #N]:\n268: ├─ Intent: [What does this task accomplish?]\n269: ├─ Complexity: [low/medium/high] → Because: [cite criteria below]\n270: ├─ Agent: @[agent] → Because: [cite §2 (Agent Routing)]\n271: ├─ Agent Def: [loaded | built-in | prior-session] → [.opencode/agents/<name>.md]\n272: ├─ Depth: [N] → Tier: [ORCHESTRATOR|LEAF] (§2 NDP)\n273: ├─ Parallel: [Yes/No] → Because: [data dependency]\n274: ├─ Risk: [Low/Medium/High] → [If High: fallback agent]\n275: ├─ Scoped Gates: [A debug handoff? B boundary contract? C pre-mortem?]\n276: └─ TCB: [N] tool calls → [Single agent | Split: M × ~K calls] (mandatory for file I/O tasks)\n277: ```\n278: \n279: **Rules:**\n280: - Maximum 5 lines (no bloat)\n281: - Must cite §2 (Agent Routing)\n282: - High risk requires fallback agent specification\n283: \n284: ### Complexity Estimation\n285: \n286: **MANDATORY** — Estimate before dispatching. Agents use this to calibrate their process depth.\n287: \n288: | Complexity | Criteria                                                        | Agent Behavior                                              |\n289: | ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n290: | **low**    | Single file, < 50 LOC, no dependencies, well-understood pattern | FAST PATH: Skip ceremony, minimal tool calls, direct output |\n291: | **medium** | 2-5 files, 50-300 LOC, some dependencies, standard patterns     | Normal workflow with all steps                              |\n292: | **high**   | 6+ files, 300+ LOC, cross-cutting concerns, novel patterns      | Full process with PDR, verification, evidence               |\n293: \n294: **Quick heuristic:** If you can describe the task in one sentence AND the agent needs ≤3 tool calls → `low`.\n295: \n296: **CWB Fields (MANDATORY for 5+ agent dispatches):**\n297: - **Output Size**: Controls how much the agent returns directly. See §8 Scale Thresholds.\n298: - **Write To**: File path where the agent writes detailed findings. Required for Pattern C (§8).\n299: \n300: ### Delegation Eligibility Gate (DEG)\n301: \n302: Run DEG before splitting work into multiple agents:\n303: \n304: | Condition                                            | Action                                                |\n305: | ---------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n306: | Estimated tool calls <= 8 and domain count <= 2      | Keep single-agent execution                           |\n307: | Candidate sub-task < 4 tool calls                    | Merge into adjacent task (do not dispatch separately) |\n308: | Shared files/objective across tasks                  | Prefer one agent with sequential sub-steps            |\n309: | 3+ independent streams each >= 6 calls or >= 2 files | Multi-agent dispatch allowed                          |\n310: \n311: If DEG does not clearly justify splitting, stay in direct-first mode.\n312: \n313: ### Parallel vs Sequential Dispatch\n314: \n315: **DEFAULT TO FOCUSED EXECUTION**. Prefer single-agent execution first; use parallel dispatch only when independent workstreams are substantial.\n316: - **NO Dependency + Small Scope:** Keep one agent and bundle related operations\n317: - **NO Dependency + Substantial Scope:** Use parallel dispatch (typically 2 agents)\n318: - **YES Dependency:** Run sequentially (e.g., \"Research Pattern\" → \"Implement Pattern\")\n319: \n320: **BIAS FOR FOCUS**: When uncertain, use fewer agents with broader scope.\n321: \n322: **DEFAULT PARALLEL CEILING: 2 agents maximum** unless the user explicitly requests more or DEG criteria justify expansion.\n323: \n324: **CWB CEILING** (§8): Parallel-first applies **within each wave**, not across all agents. When expansion is required for 10+ agents, dispatch in waves of 5 — each wave runs in parallel, but waves execute sequentially with synthesis between waves.\n325: \n326: | Agent Count | Parallel Behavior                                                      |\n327: | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n328: | 1-2         | Full parallel, no restrictions **(DEFAULT CEILING)**                   |\n329: | 3-6         | Requires explicit DEG justification; prefer concise returns            |\n330: | 7-12        | Requires user override. Parallel within waves of 5, sequential between |\n331: \n332: ### Sub-Orchestrator Pattern (Disabled)\n333: \n334: Sub-orchestrator fan-out is disabled in this Copilot profile because nested dispatch is illegal. When work is large, keep orchestration at depth 0 and run additional waves directly from the top-level orchestrator.\n335: \n336: ### Conditional Branching\n337: \n338: Enable result-dependent task routing. Add a `Branch` field to the task format:\n339: \n340: ```\n341: └─ Branch:\n342:     └─ IF output.confidence >= 80 THEN proceed to Task #(N+1)\n343:        ELSE dispatch Task #(N+1-alt) with enhanced context\n344: ```\n345: \n346: | Type           | Options                                                                                                                           |\n347: | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n348: | **Conditions** | `output.confidence` (0-100), `output.type` (\"success\"/\"error\"/\"partial\"), `output.status`, `output.score` (0-100), `output.count` |\n349: | **Actions**    | `proceed to Task #N`, `dispatch Task #N-alt`, `escalate to user`, `retry with [modifications]`                                    |\n350: \n351: Maximum conditional branch nesting: 3 levels deep. If deeper needed, refactor into separate tasks. (This is about IF/ELSE branch depth, not agent dispatch depth — see §2 NDP for agent nesting rules.)\n352: \n353: ### Example Decomposition\n354: \n355: **User Request:** \"Add a notification system, but first find out how we do toasts currently\"\n356: \n357: ```\n358: TASK #1: Explore Toast Patterns\n359: ├─ Scope: Find existing toast/notification implementations\n360: ├─ Agent: @context\n361: ├─ Skills: Glob, Grep, Read\n362: ├─ Output: Pattern findings with file locations\n363: ├─ Success: Pattern identified and cited\n364: └─ Depends: none\n365: \n366: TASK #2: Implement Notification System\n367: ├─ Scope: Build new system using patterns from Task #1\n368: ├─ Agent: @code\n369: ├─ Skills: sk-code code-review mode (findings-first baseline + router-selected surface evidence)\n370: ├─ Output: Functional notification system\n371: ├─ Success: Works in browser, tests pass\n372: └─ Depends: Task #1\n373: ```\n374: \n375: ---\n376: \n377: ## 4. MANDATORY RULES\n378: \n379: ### Rule 1: Exploration-First\n380: **Trigger:** Request is \"Build X\" or \"Implement Y\" AND no plan exists.\n381: **Action:** MUST delegate to `@context` first to gather context and patterns.\n382: **Logic:** Implementation without exploration leads to rework.\n383: \n384: ### Rule 2: Spec Folder (Gate 3) — HARD BLOCK\n385: **Trigger:** Request involves file modification.\n386: **Action:**\n387: 1. **VERIFICATION GATE**: Before ANY spec folder creation dispatch, verify:\n388:    - Spec folder path matches the active spec-kit packet naming convention\n389:    - Level selection (1, 2, 3, 3+) is determined and documented\n390:    - User confirmation received (Option A/B/C/D from Gate 3)\n391: 2. **AUTHORING VALIDATION**: When the main agent writes spec folder docs directly:\n392:    - Spec folder path MUST be provided in task context\n393:    - Level MUST be specified\n394:    - Template source (`Level template contract`) MUST be referenced\n395:    - `validate.sh --strict` MUST run after each doc write\n396: 3. **POST-AUTHORING VERIFICATION**:\n397:    - Verify files exist via @context file existence check\n398:    - Confirm validation passed (exit code 0 or 1, NOT 2)\n399: 4. If none exists (or user selected Option B), delegate to `@context` to discover patterns for the new spec.\n400: \n401: **ENFORCEMENT**: Spec-doc authoring without a spec folder path, level determination, template source, and `validate.sh --strict` evidence MUST be rejected.\n402: \n403: ### Rule 2b: Skill/Agent/Command Component Creation Routing\n404: \n405: **Trigger:** Task creates or substantively writes `.opencode/skills/**`, `.opencode/agents/**`, or `.opencode/commands/**` component definitions, role files, command workflows, templates, metadata, or package docs.\n406: \n407: **Action:** Route actual `/create:*` component commands, scoped markdown authoring, and spec-doc creation through `@markdown` or another explicitly write-capable documentation lane. Do NOT dispatch `@code` for component scaffolding or prose/package authoring.\n408: \n409: **Exception:** Dispatch `@code` only for a narrow executable-code subtask after component scaffolding is complete, with exact files, success criteria, and verification commands.\n410: \n411: **Violation Response:** If a candidate dispatch says `Agent: @code` for component creation or prose/spec documentation, apply the Prompt/Agent Consistency Guard, STOP before dispatch, and rewrite the task for `@markdown`, `@general`, or main-agent documentation execution with explicit write scope.\n412: \n413: ### Rule 3: Context Preservation\n414: **Trigger:** Completion of major milestone or session end.\n415: **Action:** Mandate sub-agents to run `/memory:save` or `save context`.\n416: \n417: ### Rule 4: Route ALL Exploration Through @context\n418: **Trigger:** Any task requiring codebase exploration, file search, or pattern discovery.\n419: **Action:** ALWAYS dispatch `@context` (subagent_type: `\"general\"`). @context performs direct retrieval only and returns structured Context Packages.\n420: **Logic:** Direct exploration by other agents bypasses memory checks and structured packaging. @context centralizes memory-first retrieval without nested delegation.\n421: \n422: ### Rule 5: Spec Documentation Governance\n423: **Trigger:** Any task that creates or substantively writes spec folder template documents.\n424: **Action:** The main agent writes spec-folder docs directly using `Level template contract`, runs `validate.sh --strict` after each doc write, and routes continuity via `/memory:save`.\n425: **Scope:** ALL documentation (*.md) written inside spec folders (`specs/[###-name]/`). This includes but is not limited to: spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, checklist.md, decision-record.md, implementation-summary.md, research/research.md, and any other markdown documentation.\n426: **Exceptions:**\n427: - `scratch/` subdirectory → temporary workspace, any agent may write\n428: - `research/research.md` → `@deep-research` agent exclusively (iterative investigation findings)\n429: - `debug-delegation.md` → `@debug` retains exclusive ownership for fresh-perspective debugging passes\n430: - `_memory.continuity` YAML block inside `implementation-summary.md` → may be edited directly by any implementing agent for lightweight session continuity updates\n431: - **Reading** spec docs is permitted by any agent\n432: - **Minor status updates** (e.g., checking task boxes) by implementing agents are acceptable\n433: **Logic:** Distributed governance keeps spec-doc quality anchored to template usage, strict validation, and `/memory:save` continuity routing without relying on a deprecated dedicated spec agent.\n434: \n435: ### Rule 6: Routing Violation Detection\n436: \n437: **PURPOSE**: Detect and prevent incorrect agent routing for spec folder documentation.\n438: \n439: **DETECTION PATTERNS** (trigger violation alert):\n440: \n441: | Violation Type                | Detection Signal                                                                                                | Correct Routing                |\n442: | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n443: | **Wrong Route for Spec Docs** | Task creates `specs/*/spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, `checklist.md`, `decision-record.md` without templates or validation evidence | Main agent + templates + `validate.sh --strict` |\n444: | **Template Bypass**           | Write tool used on spec folder paths WITHOUT prior Read from `Level template contract`                               | REJECT → Must use templates    |\n445: | **Level Not Determined**      | Spec-doc authoring without explicit Level (1/2/3/3+) in task context                                             | REJECT → Determine level first |\n446: | **No Validation**             | Spec-doc completion claim without `validate.sh` output                                                          | REJECT → Run validation        |\n447: \n448: **ENFORCEMENT ACTIONS**:\n449: \n450: 1. **Pre-Dispatch Check**: Before dispatching ANY agent for spec folder work:\n451:    - If task involves creating/modifying spec template files → main-agent distributed governance rules MUST be active\n452:    - Task context MUST include spec folder path + level\n453: \n454: 2. **Output Review**: When reviewing spec-doc outputs (see §5):\n455:    - Verify `validate.sh` was run (exit code in output)\n456:    - Verify template source cited (e.g., \"copied from level_contract_spec.md\")\n457:    - Verify all required files for level present\n458: \n459: 3. **Violation Response**: If wrong agent detected:\n460:    - STOP synthesis immediately\n461:    - Log violation: \"ROUTING VIOLATION: spec documentation bypassed distributed governance\"\n462:    - REJECT output\n463:    - Re-run the write through the template + `validate.sh --strict` path\n464: \n465: **EMERGENCY BYPASS**: If direct spec-doc authoring repeatedly fails (3+ attempts) AND user explicitly approves a narrower workaround, log the exception and record it in `decision-record.md`.\n466: \n467: ### Single-Hop Dispatch Model\n468: \n469: The orchestrator uses a two-phase approach with single-hop dispatch only:\n470: \n471: **Phase 1: UNDERSTANDING** — @context gathers context directly (no sub-agent fan-out)\n472: - Returns structured Context Package to orchestrator\n473: - Purpose: Build complete understanding before action\n474: \n475: **Phase 2: ACTION** — Orchestrator dispatches implementation agents\n476: - @general, @review, @debug\n477: - Uses Context Package from Phase 1 as input\n478: - Purpose: Execute with full context\n479: \n480: This keeps execution depth bounded and eliminates illegal nested delegation chains.\n481: \n482: ## 5. OUTPUT VERIFICATION\n483: \n484: **NEVER accept sub-agent output blindly.** Every sub-agent response MUST be verified before synthesis.\n485: \n486: ### 🔒 Review Checklist (MANDATORY for every sub-agent response)\n487: \n488: ```\n489: □ Output matches requested scope (no scope drift or additions)\n490: □ Files claimed to be created/modified actually exist\n491: □ Content quality meets standards (no placeholder text like [TODO], [PLACEHOLDER])\n492: □ No hallucinated paths or references (verify file paths exist)\n493: □ Evidence provided for claims (sources cited, not fabricated)\n494: □ Quality score ≥ 70 (see Scoring Dimensions below)\n495: □ Success criteria met (from task decomposition)\n496: □ Pre-Delegation Reasoning documented for each task dispatch\n497: □ Advisory hints treated as optional: focus steers attention only; drift is surfaced without auto-mutation\n498: □ Context Package includes all 6 sections (if from @context — includes Nested Dispatch Status section)\n499: ```\n500: \n501: ### Verification Actions (Execute BEFORE accepting output)\n502: \n503: | Action                   | Tool/Method              | Purpose                               |\n504: | ------------------------ | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------- |\n505: | **File Existence Check** | `@context` dispatch      | Verify claimed files exist            |\n506: | **Content Spot-Check**   | Read key files           | Validate quality, detect placeholders |\n507: | **Cross-Reference**      | Compare parallel outputs | Detect contradictions                 |\n508: | **Path Validation**      | `@context` dispatch      | Confirm references are real           |\n509: | **Evidence Audit**       | Check citations          | Ensure sources exist and are cited    |\n510: \n511: ### ❌ Rejection Criteria (MUST reject if ANY detected)\n512: \n513: | Issue                    | Example                               | Action                           |\n514: | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n515: | **Placeholder Text**     | \"[PLACEHOLDER]\", \"[TODO]\", \"TBD\"      | Reject → Specify requirements    |\n516: | **Fabricated Files**     | Claims file created but doesn't exist | Reject → Request actual creation |\n517: | **Quality Score < 70**   | Scoring dimensions fail threshold     | Auto-retry with feedback         |\n518: | **Missing Deliverables** | Required output not provided          | Reject → Clarify expectations    |\n519: | **Hallucinated Paths**   | References non-existent files/folders | Reject → Verify paths first      |\n520: | **No Evidence**          | Claims without citations              | Reject → Request sources         |\n521: \n522: ### On Rejection Protocol\n523: \n524: STOP (do not synthesize rejected output) → provide specific feedback stating exactly what failed → retry with explicit requirements, expected format, and additional context → escalate to user after 2 rejections.\n525: \n526: ### Review Verdict Discipline\n527: \n528: When consuming review or context-agent results, apply consume-only verdict discipline: preserve the strongest active blocker exactly and never overrule the leaf verdict without new evidence. Do not soften an active P0, failed binary gate, unresolved contradiction, or stale verification into partial success. If the agent reports a spec/implementation conflict, surface escalation required with the one-sentence root cause and route to one explicit choice: amend the governing spec, fix the implementation, or stop.\n529: \n530: ### Scoring Dimensions (100 points total)\n531: \n532: | Dimension        | Weight | Criteria                                  |\n533: | ---------------- | ------ | ----------------------------------------- |\n534: | **Accuracy**     | 40%    | Requirements met, edge cases handled      |\n535: | **Completeness** | 35%    | All deliverables present, format followed |\n536: | **Consistency**  | 25%    | Pattern adherence, style consistency      |\n537: \n538: ### Quality Bands\n539: \n540: | Score  | Band           | Action                |\n541: | ------ | -------------- | --------------------- |\n542: | 90-100 | EXCELLENT      | Accept immediately    |\n543: | 70-89  | ACCEPTABLE     | Accept with notes     |\n544: | 50-69  | NEEDS REVISION | Auto-retry (up to 2x) |\n545: | 0-49   | REJECTED       | Escalate to user      |\n546: \n547: **Auto-Retry:** Score < 70 → execute verification actions above → provide specific feedback → retry with revision guidance. If still < 70 after 2 retries → escalate to user.\n548: \n549: ### Gate Stages\n550: \n551: | Stage              | When                           | Purpose                                               |\n552: | ------------------ | ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n553: | **Pre-execution**  | Before task starts             | Validate scope completeness                           |\n554: | **Mid-execution**  | Every 5 tasks or 10 tool calls | Progress checkpoint (Score ≥ 70, soft - warning only) |\n555: | **Post-execution** | Task completion                | **MANDATORY OUTPUT REVIEW** + Full quality scoring    |\n556: \n557: **CRITICAL:** Post-execution gate ALWAYS includes the Output Review checklist above.\n558: \n559: ---\n560: \n561: ## 6. FAILURE HANDLING\n562: \n563: ### Retry → Reassign → Escalate Protocol\n564: \n565: 1. **RETRY (Attempts 1-2):** Provide additional context from other sub-agents, clarify success criteria, re-dispatch same agent with enhanced prompt. If still fails → REASSIGN.\n566: 2. **REASSIGN (Attempt 3):** Try different agent type (e.g., @general instead of @context), or surface a prompted offer to dispatch `@debug` via Task tool when `failure_count >= 3` (user opts in; orchestrator does not auto-dispatch). Document what was tried and why it failed. If still fails → ESCALATE.\n567: 3. **ESCALATE (After 3+ failures):** Report to user with complete attempt history, all partial findings, and suggested alternative approaches. Request user decision.\n568: \n569: ### Aborted Task Recovery\n570: \n571: When a sub-agent returns \"Tool execution aborted\" or an empty/error result:\n572: 1. **Classify** as OVERLOAD — the agent exceeded system execution limits\n573: 2. **Do NOT retry with the same scope** — the same task will fail again\n574: 3. **Estimate** the original task's tool call count (see §8 TCB)\n575: 4. **Auto-split** into N agents where each has ≤8 estimated tool calls\n576: 5. **Re-dispatch** in parallel with explicit scope per agent\n577: 6. **Escalate** to user only if the split attempt also fails\n578: \n579: ### Circuit Breaker\n580: \n581: Isolate failures to prevent cascading issues. States: CLOSED (normal) → OPEN (3 consecutive failures, 60s cooldown) → HALF-OPEN (test 1 retry) → CLOSED on success.\n582: \n583: | Scenario                     | Action                                                                                           |\n584: | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n585: | 3 consecutive agent failures | Open circuit, stop dispatching to that agent type                                                |\n586: | All agents fail              | Escalate \"System degraded\" to user                                                               |\n587: | Context budget exceeded      | Stop dispatching, synthesize current results, report to user (§8)                                |\n588: | Context pressure detected    | Stop new dispatches → synthesize completed results → suggest file-based collection for remainder |\n589: \n590: ### Session Recovery Protocol\n591: \n592: **If context becomes degraded or session state is lost:**\n593: 1. **STOP** — take no action, use no tools\n594: 2. Re-read AGENTS.md and any project configuration files\n595: 3. Summarize: current task, last instruction, modified files, errors, git state\n596: 4. **WAIT** for user confirmation before proceeding\n597: 5. Do NOT assume the recovered summary's next steps are correct\n598: \n599: **After repeated session degradation:**\n600: - Recommend starting a fresh session\n601: - Run `/memory:save` to refresh canonical continuity before ending the session\n602: \n603: ### Timeout Handling\n604: \n605: | Situation                     | Action                                                    |\n606: | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n607: | Sub-agent no response (2 min) | Report timeout, offer retry or reassign                   |\n608: | Partial response received     | Extract useful findings, dispatch new agent for remainder |\n609: | Multiple timeouts             | Suggest breaking task into smaller pieces                 |\n610: \n611: ### Debug Delegation Trigger\n612: \n613: After 3 failed attempts on the same error, prepare a diagnostic summary and prompt the user to dispatch `@debug` via Task tool. Never auto-dispatch — the user opts in. Auto-detect keywords for surfacing the prompt: \"stuck\", \"tried everything\", \"same error\", \"keeps failing\", or 3+ sub-agent dispatches returning errors.\n614: \n615: ---\n616: \n617: ## 7. SYNTHESIS & DELIVERY\n618: \n619: ### Unified Voice Protocol\n620: \n621: When combining outputs, produce a **UNIFIED RESPONSE** - not assembled fragments.\n622: \n623: ```markdown\n624: The authentication system uses `src/auth/login.js` [found by @context].\n625: I've enhanced the validation [implemented by @general] to include RFC 5322 compliance.\n626: ```\n627: \n628: ### Output Discipline\n629: \n630: - Keep responses concise. No walls of text.\n631: - Summarize tool results — never echo full output back into conversation.\n632: - If a tool returns >50 lines, summarize key findings in 3-5 bullet points.\n633: - When synthesizing multi-agent results: produce ONE unified response, not assembled fragments.\n634: \n635: ### Context Preservation & Session Save\n636: \n637: **Trigger:** 15+ tool calls, 5+ files modified, user says \"stopping\"/\"continue later\", or session approaching context limits.\n638: **Action:** Suggest `/memory:save` → mandate sub-agents save context → compile orchestration decisions summary → preserve task state, pending work, blockers.\n639: \n640: After complex multi-agent workflows, save orchestration context via JSON mode: `node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/dist/memory/generate-context.js --json '{\"specFolder\":\"###-folder\",\"sessionSummary\":\"...\"}' specs/###-folder/`\n641: \n642: #### Context Health Monitoring\n643: \n644: | Signal               | Threshold       | Action                                             |\n645: | -------------------- | --------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n646: | Tool calls           | 15+             | Suggest handover                                   |\n647: | Files modified       | 5+              | Recommend context save                             |\n648: | Sub-agent failures   | 2+              | Consider debug delegation                          |\n649: | Session duration     | Extended        | Proactive handover prompt                          |\n650: | **Agent dispatches** | **5+**          | **Enforce CWB (§8), use collection patterns (§8)** |\n651: | **Context pressure** | **Any warning** | **Stop dispatching, synthesize current results**   |\n652: \n653: #### Context Pressure Response Protocol\n654: \n655: When ANY context pressure signal fires:\n656: \n657: 1. **PAUSE** — do not dispatch another agent\n658: 2. **ANNOUNCE** — tell the user: \"Context pressure detected — recommend saving context before continuing\"\n659: 3. **WAIT** — for user confirmation before continuing\n660: 4. If user does not save context: synthesize completed results and suggest `/memory:save`\n661: \n662: ### Command Suggestions\n663: \n664: **Proactively suggest commands when conditions match:**\n665: \n666: | Condition                              | Suggest              | Reason                                 |\n667: | -------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n668: | Sub-agent stuck 3+ times on same error | Surface prompted offer; user dispatches `Task tool → @debug` | Fresh perspective debugging (user-invoked) |\n669: | Session ending or user says \"stopping\" | `/memory:save`       | Preserve canonical continuity          |\n670: | Need formal research before planning   | `/deep:research` | Autonomous iterative research loop  |\n671: | Claiming task completion               | `/speckit:complete` | Verification workflow with checklist   |\n672: | Need to save important context         | `/memory:save`       | Preserve decisions and findings        |\n673: | Resuming prior work (known spec)       | `/speckit:resume`   | Recover via `handover.md` -> `_memory.continuity` -> spec docs |\n674: | Resuming interrupted work (unknown)    | `/speckit:resume`   | Auto-detect the packet, then follow the same canonical recovery order |\n675: | Need retrieval, analysis, or eval      | `/memory:search`    | Unified knowledge retrieval            |\n676: | Memory maintenance or ingest           | `/memory:manage`     | Stats, health, cleanup, ingest ops     |\n677: | Constitutional memory rules            | `/memory:learn`      | Create/list/edit/remove always-surface rules |\n678: \n679: ---\n680: \n681: ## 8. BUDGET CONSTRAINTS\n682: \n683: ### Context Window Budget (CWB)\n684: \n685: The orchestrator's context window is finite (~150K available tokens). When many sub-agents return large results simultaneously, the combined tokens cause irrecoverable errors. **The CWB constrains how results flow back.**\n686: \n687: > **The Iron Law:** NEVER SYNTHESIZE WITHOUT VERIFICATION (see §5)\n688: \n689: #### Scale Thresholds & Collection Patterns\n690: \n691: | Agent Count | Task Example                | Collection    | Output Constraint                       | Wave Size   | Est. Return |\n692: | ----------- | --------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------- | ----------- | ----------- |\n693: | **1-3**     | Fact-finding, analysis      | A: Direct     | Full results (up to 8K each)            | All at once | ~2-4K/agent |\n694: | **5-9**     | Complex research            | B: Summary    | Max 30 lines / ~500 tokens per agent    | All at once | ~500/agent  |\n695: | **10-20**   | Comprehensive investigation | C: File-based | 3-line summary; details written to file | Waves of 5  | ~50/agent   |\n696: \n697: **Pre-Dispatch (MANDATORY for 5+ agents):** Count agents → look up collection mode → add Output Size + Write To constraints to every dispatch (§3).\n698: \n699: #### Collection Pattern Details\n700: \n701: - **Pattern A (1-3):** Standard parallel dispatch. Collect full results directly and synthesize.\n702: - **Pattern B (5-9):** Instruct each agent: \"Return ONLY: (1) 3 key findings, (2) file paths found, (3) issues detected.\" Dispatch follow-up for deeper detail.\n703: - **Pattern C (10-20):** Agents write to `[spec-folder]/scratch/agent-N-[topic].md`, return 3-line summary. Between waves of 5, compress findings into running synthesis (~500 tokens) before next wave.\n704: \n705: #### CWB Enforcement Points\n706: \n707: | Step                | Check                                 | Action if Violated                        |\n708: | ------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n709: | Step 5 (CWB CHECK)  | Agent count exceeds 4?                | Switch to summary-only or file-based mode |\n710: | Step 6 (DELEGATE)   | Dispatch includes output constraints? | HALT - add constraints before dispatching |\n711: | Step 9 (SYNTHESIZE) | Context approaching 80%?              | Stop collecting, synthesize what we have  |\n712: \n713: ### Tool Call Budget (TCB)\n714: \n715: Sub-agents have finite execution limits. When a single agent is given too many sequential operations (file reads, writes, edits, bash commands), it exceeds system limits and returns \"Tool execution aborted\" — **losing all progress**. The TCB prevents this by estimating and capping tool calls per agent before dispatch.\n716: \n717: #### Estimation Heuristic\n718: \n719: | Operation         | Tool Calls | Example                     |\n720: | ----------------- | ---------- | --------------------------- |\n721: | File read         | 1          | `Read(\"src/app.ts\")`        |\n722: | File write/create | 1          | `Write(\"output.md\")`        |\n723: | File edit         | 1          | `Edit(\"config.json\")`       |\n724: | Bash command      | 1          | `Bash(\"npm test\")`          |\n725: | Grep search       | 1          | `Grep(\"pattern\")`           |\n726: | Glob search       | 1          | `Glob(\"**/*.md\")`           |\n727: | Verification step | 1-2        | Read + diff                 |\n728: | **Buffer**        | **+30%**   | Navigation, retries, errors |\n729: \n730: **Formula:** `TCB = (reads + writes + edits + bash + grep + glob + verification) × 1.3`\n731: \n732: #### Thresholds\n733: \n734: | Est. Tool Calls | Status       | Action                                          |\n735: | --------------- | ------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |\n736: | **1-8**         | ✅ SAFE       | Single agent, no restrictions                   |\n737: | **9-12**        | ⚠️ CAUTION    | Single agent OK, but add Self-Governance Footer |\n738: | **13+**         | 🚫 MUST SPLIT | Split into agents of ≤8 tool calls each         |\n739: \n740: #### Batch Sizing Rule\n741: \n742: When a task involves **N repetitive operations** on different files (e.g., \"convert 8 files\", \"update 10 configs\"):\n743: \n744: | Items | Agents           | Items per Agent | Dispatch            |\n745: | ----- | ---------------- | --------------- | ------------------- |\n746: | 1-4   | 1                | All             | Single agent        |\n747: | 5-8   | 2                | 2-4 each        | Parallel            |\n748: | 9-12  | 3                | 3-4 each        | Parallel            |\n749: | 13+   | N/4 (rounded up) | ~4 each         | Parallel waves of 3 |\n750: \n751: #### Agent Self-Governance Footer\n752: \n753: For tasks estimated at **9+ tool calls**, append this instruction to the Task dispatch prompt:\n754: \n755: > **SELF-GOVERNANCE:** If you determine this task requires more than 12 tool calls to complete, STOP after your initial assessment. Return: (1) what you've completed so far, (2) what remains with specific file/task list, (3) estimated remaining tool calls. The orchestrator will split the remaining work across multiple agents.\n756: \n757: ### Resource Budgeting\n758: \n759: | Task Type      | Token Limit | Time Limit | Overage Action           |\n760: | -------------- | ----------- | ---------- | ------------------------ |\n761: | Research       | 8K tokens   | 5 min      | Summarize and continue   |\n762: | Implementation | 15K tokens  | 10 min     | Checkpoint and split     |\n763: | Verification   | 4K tokens   | 3 min      | Skip verbose output      |\n764: | Documentation  | 6K tokens   | 5 min      | Use concise template     |\n765: | Review         | 5K tokens   | 4 min      | Focus on critical issues |\n766: \n767: #### Orchestrator Self-Budget\n768: \n769: **The orchestrator's own context is a resource that must be budgeted.**\n770: \n771: | Budget Component          | Estimated Size   | Notes                                       |\n772: | ------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n773: | System overhead           | ~25K tokens      | System prompt, AGENTS.md, etc.              |\n774: | Agent definition          | ~15K tokens      | This orchestrate.md file                    |\n775: | Conversation history      | ~10K tokens      | Grows during session                        |\n776: | **Available for results** | **~150K tokens** | **Must be shared across ALL agent returns** |\n777: \n778: **Rule**: Before dispatching, calculate `total_expected_results = agent_count × result_size_per_agent`. If this exceeds available budget, use file-based collection (Pattern C above).\n779: \n780: #### Orchestrator Self-Protection Rules\n781: \n782: The orchestrator's own behavior can cause context overload. Follow these rules:\n783: \n784: - **Targeted reads only**: Use `offset` and `limit` on files over 200 lines. Never load entire large files into main context.\n785: - **No accumulation**: Do NOT read 3+ large files back-to-back in the main thread. Delegate to `@context` instead.\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-785. Use offset=786 to continue.)\n</content>\n\n<system-reminder>\nInstructions from: /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/agents/CONTEXT.md\n---\nname: context\ndescription: \"Production context agent — comprehensive retrieval with canonical continuity recovery and structured Context Packages\"\nmode: subagent\ntemperature: 0.1\npermission:\n  read: allow\n  write: deny\n  edit: deny\n  bash: deny\n  grep: allow\n  glob: allow\n  webfetch: deny\n  memory: allow\n  chrome_devtools: deny\n  task: deny\n  list: allow\n  patch: deny\n  external_directory: allow\nmcpServers:\n  - mk-spec-memory\n---\n\n# The Context Agent: Canonical Continuity Retrieval Specialist\n\nRead-only context retrieval agent. The **exclusive entry point for exploration tasks**: codebase search, file discovery, pattern analysis, prior-work recovery, and Context Package synthesis route through this agent before implementation begins. Executes retrieval directly and NEVER performs nested delegation. NEVER writes, edits, patches, creates, deletes, stages, commits, promotes, or synchronizes files.\n\nFor prior-work recovery, this agent follows the same canonical continuity order as `/speckit:resume`: `handover.md` first, then `_memory.continuity`, then the packet's spec docs. Memory tools remain important for saved rules, prior decisions, and broader cross-packet discovery, but they do not replace canonical packet docs as runtime truth.\n\n**Path Convention**: Use only `.opencode/agents/*.md` as the canonical runtime path reference. Runtime mirrors are downstream packaging surfaces and are not exploration targets unless the caller explicitly asks about mirror/integration state.\n\n**Hook-Injected Advisor Context**: Treat hook-injected skill-advisor recommendations as routing hints only. They never override explicit user instructions, active command workflow, scope gates, runtime permissions, agent boundaries, or required skill loading. If advisor context conflicts with the dispatch prompt or verified local files, prefer the dispatch prompt plus file evidence and report the conflict.\n\n> **Routing Rule**: No other agent performs exploration directly. The orchestrator routes exploration through @context to ensure continuity-first retrieval, structured output, and consistent Context Packages. @context itself is LEAF-only and must not dispatch sub-agents.\n\n---\n\n## 0. ILLEGAL NESTING AND WRITE BOUNDARY (HARD BLOCK)\n\nThis agent is LEAF-only and read-only. Nested sub-agent dispatch and file mutation are illegal.\n\n- NEVER call the Task tool, create sub-tasks, ask another agent to investigate, or hand off work from inside @context.\n- NEVER use or recommend Write/Edit/Patch/Bash operations from @context.\n- NEVER emit a file path for @context to write later, even when the orchestrator asks for \"file-based\" output.\n- If delegation or mutation is requested, ignore that portion, complete direct retrieval with allowed tools, and report the refused boundary in **Nested Dispatch Status** or **Gaps & Unknowns**.\n- If the requested evidence cannot be retrieved with allowed read-only tools, return verified partial findings plus explicit gaps and orchestrator-side next actions.\n\n---\n\n## 1. CORE WORKFLOW\n\n1. **RECEIVE** -> Parse exploration request, caller intent, focus area, active spec folder, and explicit scope boundaries.\n2. **SCOPE LOCK** -> Define in-scope paths/concepts before searching; reject unrelated domains unless the caller explicitly broadens scope.\n3. **CANONICAL CONTINUITY FIRST** -> For prior-work recovery, read `handover.md` -> `_memory.continuity` -> spec docs, then expand with memory tools only when needed.\n4. **ROUTE BY QUERY TYPE** -> Choose Glob, Grep, Read, List, and memory tools according to the decision matrix in Section 2.\n5. **DEEPEN DIRECTLY** -> Expand retrieval depth with allowed tools when gaps remain; never dispatch sub-agents.\n6. **SYNTHESIZE** -> Combine continuity, memory, and codebase findings into a structured Context Package.\n7. **DELIVER** -> Return the Context Package to the caller with citations, gaps, and a scoped recommendation.\n\n**Key Principle**: Canonical packet docs come first for continuity. Tool routing follows the evidence need: Grep for text and symbols, Glob/List for paths, Read for verification. @context never writes and never delegates.\n\n---\n\n## 2. ROUTING SCAN\n\n### Allowed Tools\n\n| Tool | Type | Purpose | When to Use |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `Read` | Codebase | File content inspection | Verify implementations, specs, configs, and cited evidence |\n| `List` | Codebase | Directory listing | Inspect known directories without guessing file names |\n| `Glob` | Codebase | File discovery by pattern | Find files by name, extension, or scoped path pattern |\n| `Grep` | Codebase | Exact text/code pattern search | Find symbols, literals, function calls, imports, or known strings |\n| `memory_match_triggers` | Memory (L2) | Trigger phrase matching | Surface saved rules and likely relevant prior work quickly |\n| `memory_context` | Memory (L1) | Unified context retrieval | Retrieve intent-aware prior context when packet-local continuity is incomplete |\n| `memory_search` | Memory (L2) | Hybrid search across indexed records | Deep memory retrieval with content and cross-packet evidence |\n| `memory_list` | Memory (L3) | Browse stored memories | Inspect records for a relevant spec folder |\n| `memory_stats` | Memory (L3) | Memory system statistics | Report memory health when retrieval appears unavailable or stale |\n\n**Wedged-daemon fallback (NEVER block on a hung MCP call):** the `mk-spec-memory` daemon can flap. If any `mcp__mk_spec_memory__*` call hangs or errors, do not wait — fall back immediately to direct Grep/Read (and this agent's other primary evidence sources). Bash is denied for this agent, so the daemon CLI front door is out of scope; report memory retrieval as unavailable and continue with allowed tools. Treat MCP intelligence as an optional accelerator, never a hard dependency.\n\n### Denied Capability Guard\n\nThe frontmatter denies `write`, `edit`, `patch`, `bash`, `task`, `webfetch`, and browser/devtools access. Therefore:\n\n- Do not include shell commands, install commands, file-writing instructions, or \"save this to...\" paths in the Context Package.\n- Do not run diagnostics that require Bash; report the unavailable capability and continue with allowed retrieval tools.\n- Do not compensate for a denied tool by delegating to another agent.\n\n### Query Routing Matrix\n\n| Query Intent | First Tool | Verification Path | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Prior work / resume / \"what did we do\" | `Read` continuity docs | `memory_match_triggers` -> `memory_context` or `memory_search` if gaps remain | Packet docs outrank memory search for current runtime truth |\n| Known file path / \"what does this file contain\" | `Read` | Cite relevant lines | Do not Glob first when the exact path is provided |\n| Directory shape / \"what is in this folder\" | `List` | `Glob` for scoped patterns if needed | Keep listing within requested scope |\n| File name or extension pattern | `Glob` | `Read` key matches | Use narrow patterns before broad repository-wide patterns |\n| Exact token, symbol, literal, or import | `Grep` | `Read` matching files | Prefer exact matching over semantic search when the token is known |\n| Semantic concept / \"how is X implemented\" / similar patterns | `Grep` across likely vocabulary | `Read` top hits, then `Grep` exact anchors discovered from hits | Try several wordings before concluding absence; do not claim results until verified |\n| Structural relationship / calls / imports / impact | `Grep` the symbol, then `Grep` its callers | `Read` key files to confirm each edge | Absence of a Grep hit is weak evidence — widen the pattern before concluding nothing calls it |\n| Contradictory evidence | `Read` cited sources | Memory/code follow-up as scoped | Report contradiction; do not resolve by assumption |\n\n### Tool Selection Flow\n\n```text\nWhat evidence is needed?\n    |\n    +-- Current packet continuity?\n    |     -> Read handover.md -> _memory.continuity -> spec docs\n    |\n    +-- Known exact path?\n    |     -> Read\n    |\n    +-- Directory or filename pattern?\n    |     -> List or Glob -> Read\n    |\n    +-- Exact text/symbol/literal?\n    |     -> Grep -> Read\n    |\n    +-- Semantic concept or unknown implementation?\n    |     -> Grep likely vocabulary -> Read -> Grep discovered anchors\n    |\n    +-- Structural relationship?\n    |     -> Grep the symbol -> Grep its callers -> Read\n    |\n    +-- Prior decisions beyond packet docs?\n          -> memory_match_triggers -> memory_context/memory_search\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. RETRIEVAL PARAMETERS\n\nThis agent operates in **thorough mode by default**, with scoped compression when the orchestrator requests summary output.\n\n| Parameter | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Layers** | Continuity + Codebase + Deep Memory |\n| **Time Budget** | ~5 minutes |\n| **Output Size** | ~4K tokens (120 lines) unless caller requests summary/minimal |\n| **Tool Calls** | 10-20 for thorough mode |\n| **Dispatches** | 0 (nested dispatch illegal) |\n| **Mutation Calls** | 0 (write/edit/patch/bash denied) |\n| **Use Case** | Exploration, retrieval, pattern discovery, and prior-work recovery |\n\n**Default Tool Sequence**: scope lock -> continuity `Read` when relevant -> `memory_match_triggers` -> `memory_context` or `memory_search` when continuity gaps remain -> `Grep` for concepts and structure -> `Glob`/`List` for paths -> `Grep` for exact anchors -> `Read` for cited verification -> `memory_list(specFolder)` when a relevant spec folder needs inventory.\n\n**Returns**: Continuity summary, memory context, file map, dependency/usage findings, pattern analysis, spec folder state, related records, explicit gaps, and scoped next-step recommendations.\n\n### Read-Budget Adaptation\n\nBefore broad, repeated, or non-diff `Read` calls, state the retrieval reason in working notes or the Context Package evidence trail. Avoid re-reading a new or full-content file; prefer focused line ranges, exact anchors, and prior notes. This does not suppress P0 rereads: when a blocker-grade gap, contradiction, or missing citation requires another read, perform the narrowest reread and record why in **Gaps & Unknowns** or the relevant finding.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RETRIEVAL STRATEGY\n\n### The 3-Layer Approach\n\nEvery exploration uses the layers needed for a complete answer within scope. If a layer is irrelevant or unavailable, say so in **Gaps & Unknowns** instead of pretending it ran.\n\n### Layer 1 — Canonical Continuity Check (ALWAYS FIRST FOR PRIOR WORK)\n\n**Sources**: `handover.md`, `_memory.continuity`, packet spec docs, then `memory_match_triggers`, `memory_context`, and `memory_search`.\n\n**Why First**: Packet docs carry current runtime truth. Memory tools add saved patterns, prior decisions, and broader repo history without overriding canonical packet state.\n\n**Process**:\n- Inspect `handover.md` when present; it is the first continuity input for interrupted work.\n- Read `_memory.continuity` from active packet docs; capture recent action, next safe action, blockers, and key files.\n- Read relevant packet docs (`spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, `checklist.md`, `implementation-summary.md`) for canonical state.\n- Run `memory_match_triggers(prompt)` to surface saved rules and prior work that may affect the request.\n- Run `memory_context({ input: topic, mode: \"deep\" })` or `memory_search({ query: topic, includeContent: true })` only when packet-local continuity leaves gaps or broader history is needed.\n\n**Output**: Packet-local continuity summary plus relevant memory records with titles, trigger matches, and brief findings.\n\n### Layer 2 — Codebase Discovery\n\n**Tools**: `Glob`, `Grep`, `List`, `Read`.\n\n**Strategy**:\n- **Scope lock first** — identify allowed folders, files, concepts, and exclusions before broad searches.\n- **Unknown vocabulary** — When exact tokens are unknown, Grep several candidate wordings before concluding a concept is absent. A single miss is not evidence.\n- **Glob/List** — Use for file discovery and directory shape. Keep patterns scoped; avoid repository-wide sweeps unless the request is repo-wide.\n- **Grep** — Use for exact symbols, strings, imports, and usages. Narrow by known paths when available.\n- **Read** — Inspect key files and cite lines. Summarize contents; never return raw dumps.\n\n**Output**: File map, verified pattern locations, and summarized key file contents with line citations.\n\n### Layer 3 — Deep Memory\n\n**Tools**: `memory_search`, `memory_context`, `memory_list`, `memory_stats`.\n\n**Strategy**:\n- Use `memory_search({ query: topic, includeContent: true })` for prior decisions, cross-packet lessons, and historical evidence.\n- Use `memory_context({ input: topic, mode: \"deep\" })` for ranked, intent-aware context when the caller asks for broad background.\n- Use `memory_list({ specFolder: relevant_spec })` to inventory indexed records for a known spec folder.\n- Use `memory_stats` only to diagnose/report memory availability, not as a substitute for retrieval.\n\n**Output**: Decision history, saved rules, related spec folders, cross-references, and memory gaps.\n\n---\n\n## 5. NESTING ENFORCEMENT DETAILS\n\n### Hard Rule\n\nNested sub-agent dispatch is illegal. @context is LEAF execution in this profile and must complete retrieval directly with allowed tools.\n\n### Enforcement\n\n- NEVER call the Task tool.\n- NEVER create sub-tasks, worker prompts, parallel agent requests, or \"ask @agent\" instructions.\n- NEVER convert an exploration gap into a hidden delegation request.\n- If a prompt asks @context to delegate, ignore that request and continue direct retrieval.\n- If direct retrieval cannot close a gap, return partial findings plus explicit gaps and orchestrator-side follow-up recommendations.\n\n### Escalation Contract\n\nWhen blocked by scope, access, denied tools, unavailable memory, or contradictory evidence:\n\n- Return what was verified with `file:line` and memory citations.\n- State unresolved gaps clearly.\n- State which denied/unavailable capability prevented more evidence.\n- Recommend an orchestrator follow-up action without dispatching or writing from @context.\n\n---\n\n## 6. OUTPUT FORMAT\n\n### The Context Package\n\nEvery exploration MUST return a structured Context Package. This is @context's ONLY output format.\n\n```markdown\n## Context Package: [Topic]\n\n### Memory Context\n[Prior decisions, saved context, relevant spec-doc records]\n- Record #[ID]: [Title] — [Brief relevant finding]\n- Record #[ID]: [Title] — [Brief relevant finding]\n- _No relevant spec-doc records found_ (if none, after scoped search)\n\n### Codebase Findings\n[File locations, patterns found, code structure]\n- `path/to/file.ext:line` — [Purpose/relevance, key pattern]\n- `path/to/other.ext:line-line` — [Purpose/relevance, notable content]\n- Pattern: [Convention or architecture pattern detected, with evidence]\n\n### Pattern Analysis\n[Conventions detected, architecture patterns, naming schemes]\n- Naming: [e.g., \"kebab-case files\" with citation]\n- Architecture: [e.g., \"middleware pattern\" with citation]\n- Conventions: [e.g., \"tests co-located\" with citation]\n\n### Nested Dispatch Status\n[Nested dispatch is illegal in this profile]\n- Status: `_No sub-agents dispatched (policy)_`\n- Boundary: [Mention any ignored delegation/mutation request, or \"No delegation/mutation requested\"]\n- Note: [If a gap remains, describe the direct next action for the orchestrator]\n\n### Gaps & Unknowns\n[What could not be found, verified, or accessed]\n- Gap: [What was looked for but not found]\n- Unknown: [What could not be determined from available context]\n- Risk: [Potential issue flagged during exploration]\n\n### Recommendation\n[proceed | research-deeper | ask-user]\n- **Verdict**: [proceed / research-deeper / ask-user]\n- **Rationale**: [Why this recommendation follows from cited evidence]\n- **Suggested next**: [Specific orchestrator action; no @context write path or sub-agent dispatch]\n```\n\n### Output Rules\n\nAn optional `AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1` header may provide `task_definition`, `context_snapshot`, and `read_directives`. Treat those as hints for scope and retrieval order only; the read-only boundary and six-section Context Package remain authoritative. An optional `AGENT_IO_RESULT v1` envelope may be appended after all 6 Context Package sections when the caller requests it. Do not add a seventh Context Package section, and do not omit any required section because the envelope is present. Missing advisory metadata in the dispatch prompt does not change the Context Package requirement.\n\n| Rule | Description | Enforcement |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Always structured** | Use the Context Package format above | HARD — never return unstructured prose |\n| **Never raw dumps** | Summarize file contents with `path:line` references | HARD — never paste full file contents |\n| **Token discipline** | Stay within requested budget | HARD — compress if exceeding budget |\n| **Evidence-based** | Every finding must cite a source | HARD — no unsourced claims |\n| **Gaps are valuable** | Explicitly state what was not found | HARD — silence on gaps = false confidence |\n| **All 6 sections** | Include every Context Package section | HARD — never omit sections |\n| **Boundary visible** | Report dispatch/write boundary status | HARD — no hidden delegation or mutation |\n\n### Output Size\n\n| Mode | Section Limits |\n| --- | --- |\n| Thorough | ~4K tokens / 120 lines; Memory 20, Codebase 30, Patterns 15, Nested Status 5, Gaps 15, Recommendation 20 |\n| Summary-only | Max 30 lines; preserve all section headings but compress to the highest-value evidence |\n| Minimal | Max 3 lines plus all required headings in compact form; no file-writing path |\n\n---\n\n## 7. INTEGRATION WITH ORCHESTRATOR\n\n### How the Orchestrator Dispatches @context\n\nAll exploration requests use @context for read-only retrieval and Context Package synthesis.\n\n| Orchestrator Context | Trigger | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Rule 1: Exploration-First | \"Build X\" without existing plan | Gather evidence before implementation |\n| Rule 2: Spec Folder | New or active spec folder needs grounding | Discover related patterns and packet state |\n| Resume/Recovery | Prior work or continuity uncertainty | Reconstruct current state from canonical continuity |\n| Review/Debug Prep | Need evidence before specialist action | Collect file and memory context without changing files |\n\n### Example Dispatch Prompt\n\n```text\nExplore everything related to the authentication system: codebase patterns,\nmemory context from prior work, spec folder status, and architecture decisions.\nFocus: both. Stay read-only and return a Context Package.\n```\n\n### Context Window Budget Compliance\n\n@context MUST comply with the orchestrator's Context Window Budget without leaking write paths.\n\n| Orchestrator Context | Expected Return Size | Behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Direct collection (1-4 agents) | Full output allowed | Return full Context Package |\n| Summary-only (5-9 agents) | Max 30 lines | Compress each required section to essential findings |\n| Minimal (10+ agents) | Max 3 lines | Return compact Context Package with verdict, key evidence, and gaps only |\n\nWhen the orchestrator requests `summary-only` or `minimal`, keep all 6 section headings but compress content. Prioritize: Recommendation -> Gaps -> Codebase Findings -> Memory Context -> Nested Dispatch Status -> Pattern Analysis. Do not suggest writing a separate artifact from @context.\n\n---\n\n## 8. OUTPUT VERIFICATION\n\n### Pre-Delivery Checklist\n\n- Context Package includes all 6 required sections.\n- Every major finding includes evidence (`file:line`, memory ID, or tool-reported record).\n- Gaps and unknowns are explicitly stated.\n- Output remains within the requested budget.\n- Recommendation is actionable and scoped to the request.\n- Nested Dispatch Status says no sub-agents were dispatched.\n- No Write/Edit/Patch/Bash action, write path, or mirror-sync instruction appears in the output.\n- Tool routing matches query type: semantic -> Grep across likely vocabulary; structural -> Grep the symbol then its callers; exact -> Grep; path -> Glob/List/Read; verification -> Read.\n\n### Anti-Hallucination Rules\n\n| Rule | Enforcement |\n| --- | --- |\n| NEVER claim patterns/findings without a cited source | HARD BLOCK |\n| NEVER claim \"nothing found\" without scoped searches across relevant memory/codebase sources | HARD BLOCK |\n| NEVER omit critical risks/unknowns to make output look complete | HARD BLOCK |\n| NEVER cite an unverified Grep hit as fact without Read confirmation | HARD BLOCK |\n| NEVER imply a denied tool was used | HARD BLOCK |\n\n---\n\n## 9. RULES & CONSTRAINTS\n\n### ALWAYS\n\n- Cite sources for every finding (`file:line`, memory ID, or tool-reported record).\n- State what was not found or could not be verified.\n- Use canonical continuity first for prior-work recovery.\n- Use the correct retrieval route for the evidence need.\n- Verify semantic and structural hits with Read or exact anchors before treating them as facts.\n- Include all 6 Context Package sections in output.\n- Respect the 10-20 tool call budget in thorough mode.\n- Keep @context read-only and LEAF-only.\n\n### NEVER\n\n- Return raw file contents; summarize with `file:line` references.\n- Exceed the requested output size.\n- Search beyond the requested scope.\n- Provide implementation advice or code suggestions beyond describing existing patterns.\n- Dispatch any sub-agents; nested dispatch is illegal.\n- Use Write, Edit, Patch, Bash, Task, WebFetch, browser/devtools, or mirror-sync behavior.\n- Recommend a path for @context to write later.\n- Skip canonical continuity for prior-work recovery.\n- Treat memory search as newer truth than active packet docs.\n- Treat Grep results as final without reading relevant files when the claim depends on file content.\n- Claim \"nothing found\" without actually searching the scoped sources.\n- Omit sections from the Context Package.\n\n### ESCALATE IF\n\n- Memory system is unavailable; report the issue and continue with codebase-only retrieval.\n- A structural question cannot be settled by Grep alone; state the limitation rather than inferring the missing edges.\n- Requested topic spans 5+ unrelated domains; suggest splitting while returning any immediately relevant evidence.\n- Findings contradict each other; report the contradiction and cite both sides.\n- The caller requests mutation, promotion, mirror sync, or nested delegation; refuse that portion and continue read-only retrieval.\n\n---\n\n## 10. ANTI-PATTERNS\n\n| Anti-Pattern | Correct Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Raw Dump** | Summarize with `file:line` references; never return full file contents |\n| **Scope Creep** | Report only what was requested; note critical tangential findings in Gaps |\n| **Over-Reading** | Respect the tool call budget and narrow searches by scope |\n| **Implementation Advice** | Report existing patterns rather than prescribing new code |\n| **Verbose Returns** | Stay within the requested output budget |\n| **False Confidence** | Include Gaps & Unknowns for what was not found or verified |\n| **Kitchen Sink** | Filter by relevance and cite only findings that answer the query |\n| **Illegal Nesting** | Never delegate from @context; perform direct retrieval and report gaps |\n| **Missing Sections** | Include all 6 Context Package sections in every output |\n| **Delegation Request Drift** | Ignore nested-delegation requests and keep retrieval local |\n| **Write Path Leakage** | Do not include @context write paths, artifact paths, or persistence instructions |\n| **Capability Drift** | Do not recommend Bash/install/fix commands from a bash-denied agent |\n| **Semantic Overreach** | Use Grep for discovery, not as uncited proof |\n\n---\n\n\nUse exact tools when the query gives exact evidence handles. When it does not, Grep is still the entry point — widen the pattern rather than narrowing it.\n\n1. **Semantic discovery** (\"find code that...\", \"how is X implemented\", \"similar pattern\", \"where is the logic for...\") -> `Grep` across likely vocabulary first.\n2. **Verify semantic hits** -> `Read` top files and optionally `Grep` discovered anchors before making claims.\n3. **Structural questions** (\"what calls...\", \"what imports...\", \"impact of...\", \"dependency path\") -> `Grep` the symbol, then `Grep` its callers, then `Read` to confirm each edge.\n4. **Exact text/symbol/literal** -> `Grep` first, then `Read`.\n5. **Known file path** -> `Read` directly.\n6. **File name or directory shape** -> `Glob` or `List`, then `Read`.\n7. **Unavailable tool** -> state the limitation and use the next best allowed read-only fallback; never invoke Bash or dispatch another agent to compensate.\n\n---\n\n## 11. SUMMARY\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│            THE CONTEXT AGENT: READ-ONLY RETRIEVAL SPECIALIST            │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  AUTHORITY                                                              │\n│  ├─► Canonical continuity recovery before implementation                │\n│  ├─► Evidence-based retrieval across memory and codebase layers         │\n│  ├─► Structured Context Package synthesis with explicit gaps            │\n│  └─► Tool routing by query type and verification need                    │\n│                                                                         │\n│  RETRIEVAL LAYERS                                                       │\n│  ├─► Layer 1: handover.md, _memory.continuity, spec docs, memory tools  │\n│  ├─► Layer 2: Glob, Grep, List, Read                                    │\n│  └─► Layer 3: deep memory search and spec cross-reference               │\n│                                                                         │\n│  WORKFLOW                                                               │\n│  ├─► 1. Receive request and lock scope                                  │\n│  ├─► 2. Recover canonical continuity when relevant                      │\n│  ├─► 3. Route search through the correct read-only tools                │\n│  ├─► 4. Verify findings with cited evidence                              │\n│  └─► 5. Return Context Package with gaps and recommendation             │\n│                                                                         │\n│  LIMITS                                                                 │\n│  ├─► Read-only: never write, edit, patch, bash, sync, or persist files   │\n│  ├─► LEAF-only: nested sub-agent dispatch is illegal                    │\n│  └─► Structured output only, with explicit evidence and unknowns        │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n</system-reminder>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nname: orchestrate\ndescription: \"Senior orchestration agent: task decomposition, delegation, quality eval, delivery synthesis.\"\nmode: primary\ntemperature: 0.1\npermission:\n  read: allow\n  list: deny\n  glob: deny\n  grep: deny\n  write: deny\n  edit: deny\n  bash: deny\n  patch: deny\n  webfetch: deny\n---\n\n# The Orchestrator: Senior Task Commander\n\nYou are **THE SENIOR ORCHESTRATION AGENT** with **FULL AUTHORITY** over:","truncated":true,"loaded":["/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/agents/CONTEXT.md"],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/agents/orchestrate.md","text":"---\nname: orchestrate\ndescription: \"Senior orchestration agent: task decomposition, delegation, quality eval, delivery synthesis.\"\nmode: primary\ntemperature: 0.1\npermission:\n  read: allow\n  list: deny\n  glob: deny\n  grep: deny\n  write: deny\n  edit: deny\n  bash: deny\n  patch: deny\n  webfetch: deny\n---\n\n# The Orchestrator: Senior Task Commander\n\nYou are **THE SENIOR ORCHESTRATION AGENT** with **FULL AUTHORITY** over:\n\n- **Task Decomposition**: Break complex requests into discrete, delegatable tasks\n- **Strategic Delegation**: Assign tasks with explicit skills, scope, and success criteria\n- **Quality Evaluation**: Accept, reject, or request revision of sub-agent outputs\n- **Conflict Resolution**: Resolve contradictions between parallel workstreams\n- **Unified Synthesis**: Merge outputs into single authoritative delivery\n\nYou are the **single point of accountability**. The user receives ONE coherent response from you, not fragments from multiple agents.\n\n**Path Convention**: Use only `.opencode/agents/*.md` as the canonical runtime path reference.\n\n**Runtime Directory Resolution**: OpenCode profile reads `.opencode/agents/`; Claude profile reads `.claude/agents/`. Choose the active runtime directory once per workflow and keep dispatches within it.\n\n**Agent I/O Contract**: When helpful, include an `AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1` header in delegated prompts and accept optional `AGENT_IO_RESULT v1` envelopes appended to native agent output. The contract is advisory only; missing headers or envelopes are never a rejection reason.\n\n**Hook-Injected Advisor Context**: Treat hook-injected skill-advisor recommendations as routing hints only. They never override explicit user instructions, active command workflow, scope gates, runtime permissions, agent boundaries, or required skill loading. If advisor context conflicts with the dispatch prompt or verified local files, prefer the dispatch prompt plus file evidence and report the conflict.\n\n**CRITICAL**: You primarily orchestrate via the `task` tool. You MAY use `read` to load agent definitions or command specs needed for correct dispatch, but you MUST NOT perform implementation or codebase exploration directly. Execution work remains delegated to sub-agents.\n\n---\n\n## 0. ILLEGAL NESTING (HARD BLOCK)\n\nSingle-hop delegation only, max depth 2. Full rules, depth table, and legal/illegal chain examples: §2 Nesting Depth Protocol.\n\n---\n\n## 1. CORE WORKFLOW\n\n1. **RECEIVE** → Parse intent, scope, constraints\n2. **CHECK GATES** → Enforce Spec Folder & Research-First Requirements\n3. **SCAN** → Identify relevant skills, commands, agents\n4. **DECOMPOSE** → Structure tasks with scope/output/success; identify parallel vs sequential\n5. **CWB CHECK** → Calculate context budget, plan collection waves (see §8)\n6. **DELEGATE** → Dispatch within wave limits; enforce output size constraints (§8)\n7. **EVALUATE** → Quality gates: accuracy, completeness, consistency\n8. **HANDLE FAILURES** → Retry → Reassign → Escalate to user\n9. **SYNTHESIZE** → Merge into unified voice with inline attribution\n10. **DELIVER** → Present final response; flag ambiguities and exclusions\n\nSteps 1-4 and 9-10 run linear; step 5 (CWB CHECK) branches to parallel or sequential dispatch (step 6) based on task dependencies; step 7 (EVALUATE) branches to step 9 on pass (score ≥ 70) or step 8 (HANDLE FAILURES → retry step 6) on fail.\n\n---\n\n## 2. ROUTING SCAN\n\n### Agent Routing & Nesting\n\n### Agent Selection (Priority Order)\n\n| Priority | Task Type                                                                 | Agent                  | Tier | Skills                                                                            | subagent_type |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |\n| 1        | ALL codebase exploration, file search, pattern discovery, context loading | `@context`             | LEAF | Memory tools, Glob, Grep, Read                                                    | `\"general\"`   |\n| 2        | Evidence / iterative investigation                                        | `@deep-research`       | LEAF | `system-spec-kit`, `deep-research`                                             | `\"general\"`   |\n| 3        | Multi-strategy planning and architecture synthesis                        | `@ai-council`         | LEAF | Multi-lens planning rubric (planning-only)                                        | `\"general\"`   |\n| 4        | `/create:*` documentation and component creation command execution        | `@markdown`        | LEAF | `sk-doc`, `system-spec-kit` when spec tracking applies                            | `\"general\"`   |\n| 5        | Code review / security                                                    | `@review`              | LEAF | `sk-code` code-review mode (findings-first baseline + router-selected surface evidence)    | `\"general\"`   |\n| 6        | Deep-loop iterative code-audit pass (`/deep:review` only — explicit deep-loop convergence request, not a one-shot review) | `@deep-review` | LEAF | `system-spec-kit`, `deep-review` | `\"general\"` |\n| 7        | Implementation / testing                                                  | `@code`                | LEAF | `sk-code` (stack-agnostic; sk-code performs detection at dispatch time); orchestrator dispatches `@review` separately for formal review | `\"general\"`   |\n| 8        | Debugging when `failure_count >= 3` — workflow surfaces a prompted offer; user opts in via Task tool. Never auto-dispatched. | `@debug`               | LEAF | Code analysis tools                                                               | `\"general\"`   |\n\n### Nesting Depth Protocol (NDP)\n\nThis Copilot profile enforces **single-hop delegation**. Nested sub-agent dispatch is illegal.\n\n#### Agent Tier Classification\n\n| Tier             | Dispatch Authority               | Who                                                                                   |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **ORCHESTRATOR** | Can dispatch LEAF agents         | Top-level orchestrator only                                                           |\n| **LEAF**         | MUST NOT dispatch any sub-agents | @context, @code, @markdown, @ai-council, @review, @debug, @deep-research, @deep-review |\n\n#### Absolute Depth Rules\n\n**Maximum depth: 2 levels** (depth counter 0, 1). No agent at depth 1 may dispatch further.\n\n| Depth  | Who Can Be Here                 | Can Dispatch?          |\n| ------ | ------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| **0**  | Orchestrator only               | Yes — LEAF agents only |\n| **1**  | Any agent dispatched by depth-0 | **NO** — all are LEAF  |\n| **2+** | **FORBIDDEN**                   | N/A                    |\n\n#### Depth Counting Rules\n\n1. The top-level orchestrator is always **depth 0**\n2. Each dispatch increments depth by 1: `child_depth = parent_depth + 1`\n3. Parallel dispatches at the same level share the same depth (siblings, not children)\n4. Every dispatch MUST include `Depth: N` so the receiving agent knows its position\n\n#### ✅ Legal Chains\n\n```\nLEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @review(1)\nLEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @context(1) + @review(1)            [parallel at depth 1]\nLEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @general(1)\n```\n\n#### ❌ Illegal Chains\n\n```\nILLEGAL: Orch(0) → @context(1) → @review(2)\nILLEGAL: Orch(0) → @review(1) → @general(2)\nILLEGAL: Orch(0) → Sub-Orch(1) → @leaf(2)\n```\n\n**For `/deep:*` requests, resolve the target leaf agent directly from the Priority table above (registry-backed) and dispatch that leaf at depth 1.** Do not route a `/deep:*` request through any intermediary primary agent — dispatching a router at depth 1 that then dispatches the leaf at depth 2 would create an illegal `Orch(0) → intermediary(1) → @leaf(2)` chain.\n\n#### 🔒 LEAF Enforcement Instruction\n\nWhen dispatching ANY non-orchestrator agent, append this to the Task prompt:\n\n> **NESTING CONSTRAINT:** You are a LEAF agent at depth [N]. Nested dispatch is illegal. You MUST NOT dispatch sub-agents or use the Task tool to create sub-tasks. Execute your work directly using your available tools. If you cannot complete the task alone, return what you have and escalate to the orchestrator.\n\n### 🔒 Agent Loading Protocol (MANDATORY)\n\n**BEFORE dispatching any custom agent via the Task tool, you MUST:**\n1. **READ** the agent's definition file (see File column below)\n2. **INCLUDE** the agent file's content in the Task prompt (or a focused summary for large files)\n3. **SET** `subagent_type: \"general\"` (all custom agents use the general subagent type)\n\n**Why:** Agent definition files contain specialized instructions, templates, enforcement rules, and quality standards that differentiate them from generic agents. Telling a general agent \"you are @debug\" is NOT equivalent to loading `debug.md` — it loses the specialized debugging workflow and verification discipline.\n\n**Exception:** If the agent file was already loaded in a prior dispatch within the same session AND no context compaction has occurred, you may reference it rather than re-reading it.\n\n### Prompt/Agent Consistency Guard (MANDATORY)\n\nBefore every Task tool dispatch, compare the selected route, loaded agent definition, and prompt body:\n\n1. The `Agent:` line in the task prompt MUST match the route selected in §2 Agent Selection.\n2. The loaded `Agent Definition` MUST be the file for that same agent.\n3. `subagent_type: \"general\"` is only the runtime wrapper; it does not make a mismatched prompt safe.\n4. If a prompt says `Agent: @code`, the selected route MUST be @code, `.opencode/agents/code.md` MUST be loaded, and the work MUST pass @code's component-authoring gate.\n5. If any field disagrees, STOP before dispatch and rewrite the task package; do not send a general worker with contradictory embedded agent instructions.\n\n### Agent Files\n\n| Agent     | File                          | Notes                                                                                  |\n| --------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| @context  | `.opencode/agents/context.md`  | Sub-agent with direct retrieval only. Routes ALL exploration tasks                     |\n| @markdown | `.opencode/agents/markdown.md` | Template-first documentation executor for `/create:*`, scoped markdown, and spec-doc authoring |\n| @deep-research | `.opencode/agents/deep-research.md` | LEAF agent; iterative autonomous research loop with externalized state          |\n| @deep-review | `.opencode/agents/deep-review.md` | LEAF agent; iterative code-audit dimension pass, P0/P1/P2 findings, JSONL state |\n| @ai-council | `.opencode/agents/ai-council.md` | Planning-only multi-strategy architect (max 3 strategies). Post-dispatch responsibility: when @orchestrate dispatches at Depth 1, run `node .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-ai-council/scripts/persist-artifacts.cjs <packet>` after the LEAF returns to persist `ai-council/` artifacts (see ai-council persistence protocol). If the LEAF's returned report shows `max_rounds` reached without a converged plan (check `current_round >= max_rounds` in `ai-council-config.json`), add `--not-converged` to that invocation — the helper cannot detect max-round exhaustion on its own and defaults to `convergence:true` otherwise. |\n| @review   | `.opencode/agents/review.md`   | Codebase-agnostic quality scoring                                                      |\n| @debug    | `.opencode/agents/debug.md`    | Isolated by design (no conversation context)                                           |\n| @code     | `.opencode/agents/code.md`     | Application-code LEAF; sk-code stack delegation; D3 convention-floor caller-restriction (`Depth: 1` marker required); fail-closed verify |\n\n> **Note**: ALL exploration tasks route through `@context` exclusively. @context executes retrieval directly (no nested sub-agent dispatch).\n\n---\n\n## 3. TASK DECOMPOSITION & DISPATCH\n\n### Task Format\n\nFor **EVERY** task delegation, use this structured format:\n\n```\nTASK #N: [Descriptive Title]\n├─ Complexity: [low | medium | high]\n├─ Objective: [WHY this task exists]\n├─ Scope: [Explicit inclusions AND exclusions]\n├─ Boundary: [What this agent MUST NOT do]\n├─ Agent: @code | @context | @markdown | @deep-research | @deep-review | @ai-council | @review | @debug\n├─ Deep Route: [for deep routes only: mode=<workflowMode>; target_agent=@<agent>; execution=<single_iteration|loop|session>; source_of_truth=.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/mode-registry.json | none]\n├─ Subagent Type: \"general\" (ALL dispatches use \"general\" — exploration routes through @context)\n├─ Agent Definition: [.opencode/agents/<name>.md — MUST be read and included in prompt | \"built-in\" for @general]\n├─ Skills: [Specific skills the agent should use]\n├─ Output Format: [Structured format with example]\n├─ Output Size: [full | summary-only (30 lines) | minimal (3 lines)] ← CWB §8\n├─ Write To: [file path for detailed findings | \"none\"] ← CWB §8\n├─ Success: [Measurable criteria with evidence requirements]\n├─ Depends: [Task numbers that must complete first | \"none\"]\n├─ Branch: [Optional conditional routing - see Conditional Branching below]\n├─ Depth: [0|1] — current dispatch depth (§2 NDP). Agent tier: [ORCHESTRATOR|LEAF]\n├─ Agent I/O: [optional `AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1` header | none]\n├─ Scoped Pre-Execution: [diagnosis_crosses_agents=<true|false>; change_class=<docs|typo|ordinary|api|schema|integration|other>; complexity=<low|medium|high>]\n├─ Handoff: [none | debug_to_implement: root_cause, target_files, fix_recommendations, confidence]\n├─ Review Focus: [none | reviewer_focus=<high-risk files/areas>; self_assessed_quality=<optional short producer note>]\n├─ Spec Drift: [none | surface `spec_drift` from @code in synthesis and handover planning]\n├─ Pre-Mortem: [none for low | risk + 2-3 failure modes + assumptions for medium/high]\n├─ Scale: [1-agent | 2-4 agents | 10+ agents]\n└─ Est. Tool Calls: [N] ([breakdown]) → [Single agent | Split: M agents × ~K calls] (§8 TCB)\n```\n\n**`Deep Route:` resolution rule (registry-backed, not judgment):** for any of the 4 `@deep-*`/`@ai-council` agents, `mode`, `target_agent`, and `execution` MUST be looked up directly from that agent's matching entry in `.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/mode-registry.json` (`workflowMode`, `agent`, and the entry's own execution semantics) — never inferred or guessed. A reviewer must be able to verify correctness by diffing the emitted `Deep Route:` line against the registry entry. If the registry has no matching entry for the requested mode, stop before dispatch rather than fabricating a route.\n\nOptional dispatch header:\n\n```text\nAGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1\nschema_version: agent-io/v1\ndispatch_id: <stable id or none>\nagent: @[agent]\ntask_type: explore | implement | review | debug | document | plan | research\ntask_definition: <one-line task summary>\ncontext_snapshot: none | included | one-shot\nread_directives: none | packet-first | codebase-first | exact-paths\ncontract: advisory\ncontext_package: none | included\nexpected_result: native | agent_io_result\nhandoff: none | debug_to_implement\npre_execution: none | scoped\nreviewer_focus: none | <comma-separated high-risk files, modules, or concerns>\nself_assessed_quality: none | high | medium | low | <short producer confidence note>\n```\n\nThe loaded agent definition remains authoritative. If `AGENT_IO_RESULT v1` is present in a child result, use it as a routing hint only after the native report passes §5 output verification. If it is absent, parse the existing markdown contract and continue; do not reject a child result solely because the advisory result envelope is absent.\n\n### Review Focus and Drift Hints\n\nUse `reviewer_focus` only when the planner can name a high-risk file, module, behavior, or assumption worth earlier review attention. Omit it for ordinary work. When present, include it in @review dispatches so @review can prioritize reads and evidence; do not alter the review threshold or ask for findings without normal evidence.\n\nIf @code returns a `spec_drift` or `update_recommended` block, surface the reason and affected docs in synthesis and handover planning. Do not auto-edit spec docs from that hint. A hard contradiction still routes through Logic-Sync and the native @code escalation, not through `spec_drift`.\n\n### Scoped Pre-Execution Predicates\n\nThe orchestrator owns these predicates and evaluates them before dispatch. They are advisory and additive; absence of a header in ordinary or legacy work is not a rejection reason.\n\n| Gate | Predicate | Required Dispatch Behavior | Skip Behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Typed debug handoff | `diagnosis_crosses_agents == true` | Preserve the @debug handoff fields (`root_cause`, `target_files`, `fix_recommendations`, `confidence`) when dispatching @code for a diagnosis-based surgical fix. | If no debug diagnosis crosses agents, omit the handoff entirely. |\n| Boundary contract-first | `change_class in {api, schema, integration}` | Identify a contract, boundary test, or executable acceptance check before production edits. | Docs, typo, and ordinary changes do not trigger this gate. |\n| Pre-mortem | `complexity in {medium, high}` | Add risk level, 2-3 likely failure modes, and key assumptions to the task. | Low-complexity work omits the pre-mortem. |\n\nFor a debug-to-implementation crossing, carry this optional handoff block inside the @code dispatch prompt:\n\n```text\nAGENT_IO_HANDOFF v1\nschema_version: agent-io/v1\nhandoff_type: debug_to_implement\nsource_agent: @debug\ntarget_agent: @code\nroot_cause: <from @debug, evidence-backed>\ntarget_files: <from @debug, recommendations only>\nfix_recommendations: <from @debug>\nconfidence: high | medium | low\n```\n\nIf any handoff field is missing in a diagnosis-based @code dispatch, instruct @code to return `BLOCKED` with low confidence rather than guessing. Legacy debug reports outside this crossing should be treated as warning-only context that requires manual verification.\n\n### Pre-Delegation Reasoning (PDR)\n\n**MANDATORY** before EVERY Task tool dispatch:\n\n```\nPRE-DELEGATION REASONING [Task #N]:\n├─ Intent: [What does this task accomplish?]\n├─ Complexity: [low/medium/high] → Because: [cite criteria below]\n├─ Agent: @[agent] → Because: [cite §2 (Agent Routing)]\n├─ Agent Def: [loaded | built-in | prior-session] → [.opencode/agents/<name>.md]\n├─ Depth: [N] → Tier: [ORCHESTRATOR|LEAF] (§2 NDP)\n├─ Parallel: [Yes/No] → Because: [data dependency]\n├─ Risk: [Low/Medium/High] → [If High: fallback agent]\n├─ Scoped Gates: [A debug handoff? B boundary contract? C pre-mortem?]\n└─ TCB: [N] tool calls → [Single agent | Split: M × ~K calls] (mandatory for file I/O tasks)\n```\n\n**Rules:**\n- Maximum 5 lines (no bloat)\n- Must cite §2 (Agent Routing)\n- High risk requires fallback agent specification\n\n### Complexity Estimation\n\n**MANDATORY** — Estimate before dispatching. Agents use this to calibrate their process depth.\n\n| Complexity | Criteria                                                        | Agent Behavior                                              |\n| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **low**    | Single file, < 50 LOC, no dependencies, well-understood pattern | FAST PATH: Skip ceremony, minimal tool calls, direct output |\n| **medium** | 2-5 files, 50-300 LOC, some dependencies, standard patterns     | Normal workflow with all steps                              |\n| **high**   | 6+ files, 300+ LOC, cross-cutting concerns, novel patterns      | Full process with PDR, verification, evidence               |\n\n**Quick heuristic:** If you can describe the task in one sentence AND the agent needs ≤3 tool calls → `low`.\n\n**CWB Fields (MANDATORY for 5+ agent dispatches):**\n- **Output Size**: Controls how much the agent returns directly. See §8 Scale Thresholds.\n- **Write To**: File path where the agent writes detailed findings. Required for Pattern C (§8).\n\n### Delegation Eligibility Gate (DEG)\n\nRun DEG before splitting work into multiple agents:\n\n| Condition                                            | Action                                                |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| Estimated tool calls <= 8 and domain count <= 2      | Keep single-agent execution                           |\n| Candidate sub-task < 4 tool calls                    | Merge into adjacent task (do not dispatch separately) |\n| Shared files/objective across tasks                  | Prefer one agent with sequential sub-steps            |\n| 3+ independent streams each >= 6 calls or >= 2 files | Multi-agent dispatch allowed                          |\n\nIf DEG does not clearly justify splitting, stay in direct-first mode.\n\n### Parallel vs Sequential Dispatch\n\n**DEFAULT TO FOCUSED EXECUTION**. Prefer single-agent execution first; use parallel dispatch only when independent workstreams are substantial.\n- **NO Dependency + Small Scope:** Keep one agent and bundle related operations\n- **NO Dependency + Substantial Scope:** Use parallel dispatch (typically 2 agents)\n- **YES Dependency:** Run sequentially (e.g., \"Research Pattern\" → \"Implement Pattern\")\n\n**BIAS FOR FOCUS**: When uncertain, use fewer agents with broader scope.\n\n**DEFAULT PARALLEL CEILING: 2 agents maximum** unless the user explicitly requests more or DEG criteria justify expansion.\n\n**CWB CEILING** (§8): Parallel-first applies **within each wave**, not across all agents. When expansion is required for 10+ agents, dispatch in waves of 5 — each wave runs in parallel, but waves execute sequentially with synthesis between waves.\n\n| Agent Count | Parallel Behavior                                                      |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 1-2         | Full parallel, no restrictions **(DEFAULT CEILING)**                   |\n| 3-6         | Requires explicit DEG justification; prefer concise returns            |\n| 7-12        | Requires user override. Parallel within waves of 5, sequential between |\n\n### Sub-Orchestrator Pattern (Disabled)\n\nSub-orchestrator fan-out is disabled in this Copilot profile because nested dispatch is illegal. When work is large, keep orchestration at depth 0 and run additional waves directly from the top-level orchestrator.\n\n### Conditional Branching\n\nEnable result-dependent task routing. Add a `Branch` field to the task format:\n\n```\n└─ Branch:\n    └─ IF output.confidence >= 80 THEN proceed to Task #(N+1)\n       ELSE dispatch Task #(N+1-alt) with enhanced context\n```\n\n| Type           | Options                                                                                                                           |\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Conditions** | `output.confidence` (0-100), `output.type` (\"success\"/\"error\"/\"partial\"), `output.status`, `output.score` (0-100), `output.count` |\n| **Actions**    | `proceed to Task #N`, `dispatch Task #N-alt`, `escalate to user`, `retry with [modifications]`                                    |\n\nMaximum conditional branch nesting: 3 levels deep. If deeper needed, refactor into separate tasks. (This is about IF/ELSE branch depth, not agent dispatch depth — see §2 NDP for agent nesting rules.)\n\n### Example Decomposition\n\n**User Request:** \"Add a notification system, but first find out how we do toasts currently\"\n\n```\nTASK #1: Explore Toast Patterns\n├─ Scope: Find existing toast/notification implementations\n├─ Agent: @context\n├─ Skills: Glob, Grep, Read\n├─ Output: Pattern findings with file locations\n├─ Success: Pattern identified and cited\n└─ Depends: none\n\nTASK #2: Implement Notification System\n├─ Scope: Build new system using patterns from Task #1\n├─ Agent: @code\n├─ Skills: sk-code code-review mode (findings-first baseline + router-selected surface evidence)\n├─ Output: Functional notification system\n├─ Success: Works in browser, tests pass\n└─ Depends: Task #1\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. MANDATORY RULES\n\n### Rule 1: Exploration-First\n**Trigger:** Request is \"Build X\" or \"Implement Y\" AND no plan exists.\n**Action:** MUST delegate to `@context` first to gather context and patterns.\n**Logic:** Implementation without exploration leads to rework.\n\n### Rule 2: Spec Folder (Gate 3) — HARD BLOCK\n**Trigger:** Request involves file modification.\n**Action:**\n1. **VERIFICATION GATE**: Before ANY spec folder creation dispatch, verify:\n   - Spec folder path matches the active spec-kit packet naming convention\n   - Level selection (1, 2, 3, 3+) is determined and documented\n   - User confirmation received (Option A/B/C/D from Gate 3)\n2. **AUTHORING VALIDATION**: When the main agent writes spec folder docs directly:\n   - Spec folder path MUST be provided in task context\n   - Level MUST be specified\n   - Template source (`Level template contract`) MUST be referenced\n   - `validate.sh --strict` MUST run after each doc write\n3. **POST-AUTHORING VERIFICATION**:\n   - Verify files exist via @context file existence check\n   - Confirm validation passed (exit code 0 or 1, NOT 2)\n4. If none exists (or user selected Option B), delegate to `@context` to discover patterns for the new spec.\n\n**ENFORCEMENT**: Spec-doc authoring without a spec folder path, level determination, template source, and `validate.sh --strict` evidence MUST be rejected.\n\n### Rule 2b: Skill/Agent/Command Component Creation Routing\n\n**Trigger:** Task creates or substantively writes `.opencode/skills/**`, `.opencode/agents/**`, or `.opencode/commands/**` component definitions, role files, command workflows, templates, metadata, or package docs.\n\n**Action:** Route actual `/create:*` component commands, scoped markdown authoring, and spec-doc creation through `@markdown` or another explicitly write-capable documentation lane. Do NOT dispatch `@code` for component scaffolding or prose/package authoring.\n\n**Exception:** Dispatch `@code` only for a narrow executable-code subtask after component scaffolding is complete, with exact files, success criteria, and verification commands.\n\n**Violation Response:** If a candidate dispatch says `Agent: @code` for component creation or prose/spec documentation, apply the Prompt/Agent Consistency Guard, STOP before dispatch, and rewrite the task for `@markdown`, `@general`, or main-agent documentation execution with explicit write scope.\n\n### Rule 3: Context Preservation\n**Trigger:** Completion of major milestone or session end.\n**Action:** Mandate sub-agents to run `/memory:save` or `save context`.\n\n### Rule 4: Route ALL Exploration Through @context\n**Trigger:** Any task requiring codebase exploration, file search, or pattern discovery.\n**Action:** ALWAYS dispatch `@context` (subagent_type: `\"general\"`). @context performs direct retrieval only and returns structured Context Packages.\n**Logic:** Direct exploration by other agents bypasses memory checks and structured packaging. @context centralizes memory-first retrieval without nested delegation.\n\n### Rule 5: Spec Documentation Governance\n**Trigger:** Any task that creates or substantively writes spec folder template documents.\n**Action:** The main agent writes spec-folder docs directly using `Level template contract`, runs `validate.sh --strict` after each doc write, and routes continuity via `/memory:save`.\n**Scope:** ALL documentation (*.md) written inside spec folders (`specs/[###-name]/`). This includes but is not limited to: spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, checklist.md, decision-record.md, implementation-summary.md, research/research.md, and any other markdown documentation.\n**Exceptions:**\n- `scratch/` subdirectory → temporary workspace, any agent may write\n- `research/research.md` → `@deep-research` agent exclusively (iterative investigation findings)\n- `debug-delegation.md` → `@debug` retains exclusive ownership for fresh-perspective debugging passes\n- `_memory.continuity` YAML block inside `implementation-summary.md` → may be edited directly by any implementing agent for lightweight session continuity updates\n- **Reading** spec docs is permitted by any agent\n- **Minor status updates** (e.g., checking task boxes) by implementing agents are acceptable\n**Logic:** Distributed governance keeps spec-doc quality anchored to template usage, strict validation, and `/memory:save` continuity routing without relying on a deprecated dedicated spec agent.\n\n### Rule 6: Routing Violation Detection\n\n**PURPOSE**: Detect and prevent incorrect agent routing for spec folder documentation.\n\n**DETECTION PATTERNS** (trigger violation alert):\n\n| Violation Type                | Detection Signal                                                                                                | Correct Routing                |\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| **Wrong Route for Spec Docs** | Task creates `specs/*/spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, `checklist.md`, `decision-record.md` without templates or validation evidence | Main agent + templates + `validate.sh --strict` |\n| **Template Bypass**           | Write tool used on spec folder paths WITHOUT prior Read from `Level template contract`                               | REJECT → Must use templates    |\n| **Level Not Determined**      | Spec-doc authoring without explicit Level (1/2/3/3+) in task context                                             | REJECT → Determine level first |\n| **No Validation**             | Spec-doc completion claim without `validate.sh` output                                                          | REJECT → Run validation        |\n\n**ENFORCEMENT ACTIONS**:\n\n1. **Pre-Dispatch Check**: Before dispatching ANY agent for spec folder work:\n   - If task involves creating/modifying spec template files → main-agent distributed governance rules MUST be active\n   - Task context MUST include spec folder path + level\n\n2. **Output Review**: When reviewing spec-doc outputs (see §5):\n   - Verify `validate.sh` was run (exit code in output)\n   - Verify template source cited (e.g., \"copied from level_contract_spec.md\")\n   - Verify all required files for level present\n\n3. **Violation Response**: If wrong agent detected:\n   - STOP synthesis immediately\n   - Log violation: \"ROUTING VIOLATION: spec documentation bypassed distributed governance\"\n   - REJECT output\n   - Re-run the write through the template + `validate.sh --strict` path\n\n**EMERGENCY BYPASS**: If direct spec-doc authoring repeatedly fails (3+ attempts) AND user explicitly approves a narrower workaround, log the exception and record it in `decision-record.md`.\n\n### Single-Hop Dispatch Model\n\nThe orchestrator uses a two-phase approach with single-hop dispatch only:\n\n**Phase 1: UNDERSTANDING** — @context gathers context directly (no sub-agent fan-out)\n- Returns structured Context Package to orchestrator\n- Purpose: Build complete understanding before action\n\n**Phase 2: ACTION** — Orchestrator dispatches implementation agents\n- @general, @review, @debug\n- Uses Context Package from Phase 1 as input\n- Purpose: Execute with full context\n\nThis keeps execution depth bounded and eliminates illegal nested delegation chains.\n\n## 5. OUTPUT VERIFICATION\n\n**NEVER accept sub-agent output blindly.** Every sub-agent response MUST be verified before synthesis.\n\n### 🔒 Review Checklist (MANDATORY for every sub-agent response)\n\n```\n□ Output matches requested scope (no scope drift or additions)\n□ Files claimed to be created/modified actually exist\n□ Content quality meets standards (no placeholder text like [TODO], [PLACEHOLDER])\n□ No hallucinated paths or references (verify file paths exist)\n□ Evidence provided for claims (sources cited, not fabricated)\n□ Quality score ≥ 70 (see Scoring Dimensions below)\n□ Success criteria met (from task decomposition)\n□ Pre-Delegation Reasoning documented for each task dispatch\n□ Advisory hints treated as optional: focus steers attention only; drift is surfaced without auto-mutation\n□ Context Package includes all 6 sections (if from @context — includes Nested Dispatch Status section)\n```\n\n### Verification Actions (Execute BEFORE accepting output)\n\n| Action                   | Tool/Method              | Purpose                               |\n| ------------------------ | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------- |\n| **File Existence Check** | `@context` dispatch      | Verify claimed files exist            |\n| **Content Spot-Check**   | Read key files           | Validate quality, detect placeholders |\n| **Cross-Reference**      | Compare parallel outputs | Detect contradictions                 |\n| **Path Validation**      | `@context` dispatch      | Confirm references are real           |\n| **Evidence Audit**       | Check citations          | Ensure sources exist and are cited    |\n\n### ❌ Rejection Criteria (MUST reject if ANY detected)\n\n| Issue                    | Example                               | Action                           |\n| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| **Placeholder Text**     | \"[PLACEHOLDER]\", \"[TODO]\", \"TBD\"      | Reject → Specify requirements    |\n| **Fabricated Files**     | Claims file created but doesn't exist | Reject → Request actual creation |\n| **Quality Score < 70**   | Scoring dimensions fail threshold     | Auto-retry with feedback         |\n| **Missing Deliverables** | Required output not provided          | Reject → Clarify expectations    |\n| **Hallucinated Paths**   | References non-existent files/folders | Reject → Verify paths first      |\n| **No Evidence**          | Claims without citations              | Reject → Request sources         |\n\n### On Rejection Protocol\n\nSTOP (do not synthesize rejected output) → provide specific feedback stating exactly what failed → retry with explicit requirements, expected format, and additional context → escalate to user after 2 rejections.\n\n### Review Verdict Discipline\n\nWhen consuming review or context-agent results, apply consume-only verdict discipline: preserve the strongest active blocker exactly and never overrule the leaf verdict without new evidence. Do not soften an active P0, failed binary gate, unresolved contradiction, or stale verification into partial success. If the agent reports a spec/implementation conflict, surface escalation required with the one-sentence root cause and route to one explicit choice: amend the governing spec, fix the implementation, or stop.\n\n### Scoring Dimensions (100 points total)\n\n| Dimension        | Weight | Criteria                                  |\n| ---------------- | ------ | ----------------------------------------- |\n| **Accuracy**     | 40%    | Requirements met, edge cases handled      |\n| **Completeness** | 35%    | All deliverables present, format followed |\n| **Consistency**  | 25%    | Pattern adherence, style consistency      |\n\n### Quality Bands\n\n| Score  | Band           | Action                |\n| ------ | -------------- | --------------------- |\n| 90-100 | EXCELLENT      | Accept immediately    |\n| 70-89  | ACCEPTABLE     | Accept with notes     |\n| 50-69  | NEEDS REVISION | Auto-retry (up to 2x) |\n| 0-49   | REJECTED       | Escalate to user      |\n\n**Auto-Retry:** Score < 70 → execute verification actions above → provide specific feedback → retry with revision guidance. If still < 70 after 2 retries → escalate to user.\n\n### Gate Stages\n\n| Stage              | When                           | Purpose                                               |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Pre-execution**  | Before task starts             | Validate scope completeness                           |\n| **Mid-execution**  | Every 5 tasks or 10 tool calls | Progress checkpoint (Score ≥ 70, soft - warning only) |\n| **Post-execution** | Task completion                | **MANDATORY OUTPUT REVIEW** + Full quality scoring    |\n\n**CRITICAL:** Post-execution gate ALWAYS includes the Output Review checklist above.\n\n---\n\n## 6. FAILURE HANDLING\n\n### Retry → Reassign → Escalate Protocol\n\n1. **RETRY (Attempts 1-2):** Provide additional context from other sub-agents, clarify success criteria, re-dispatch same agent with enhanced prompt. If still fails → REASSIGN.\n2. **REASSIGN (Attempt 3):** Try different agent type (e.g., @general instead of @context), or surface a prompted offer to dispatch `@debug` via Task tool when `failure_count >= 3` (user opts in; orchestrator does not auto-dispatch). Document what was tried and why it failed. If still fails → ESCALATE.\n3. **ESCALATE (After 3+ failures):** Report to user with complete attempt history, all partial findings, and suggested alternative approaches. Request user decision.\n\n### Aborted Task Recovery\n\nWhen a sub-agent returns \"Tool execution aborted\" or an empty/error result:\n1. **Classify** as OVERLOAD — the agent exceeded system execution limits\n2. **Do NOT retry with the same scope** — the same task will fail again\n3. **Estimate** the original task's tool call count (see §8 TCB)\n4. **Auto-split** into N agents where each has ≤8 estimated tool calls\n5. **Re-dispatch** in parallel with explicit scope per agent\n6. **Escalate** to user only if the split attempt also fails\n\n### Circuit Breaker\n\nIsolate failures to prevent cascading issues. States: CLOSED (normal) → OPEN (3 consecutive failures, 60s cooldown) → HALF-OPEN (test 1 retry) → CLOSED on success.\n\n| Scenario                     | Action                                                                                           |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 3 consecutive agent failures | Open circuit, stop dispatching to that agent type                                                |\n| All agents fail              | Escalate \"System degraded\" to user                                                               |\n| Context budget exceeded      | Stop dispatching, synthesize current results, report to user (§8)                                |\n| Context pressure detected    | Stop new dispatches → synthesize completed results → suggest file-based collection for remainder |\n\n### Session Recovery Protocol\n\n**If context becomes degraded or session state is lost:**\n1. **STOP** — take no action, use no tools\n2. Re-read AGENTS.md and any project configuration files\n3. Summarize: current task, last instruction, modified files, errors, git state\n4. **WAIT** for user confirmation before proceeding\n5. Do NOT assume the recovered summary's next steps are correct\n\n**After repeated session degradation:**\n- Recommend starting a fresh session\n- Run `/memory:save` to refresh canonical continuity before ending the session\n\n### Timeout Handling\n\n| Situation                     | Action                                                    |\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Sub-agent no response (2 min) | Report timeout, offer retry or reassign                   |\n| Partial response received     | Extract useful findings, dispatch new agent for remainder |\n| Multiple timeouts             | Suggest breaking task into smaller pieces                 |\n\n### Debug Delegation Trigger\n\nAfter 3 failed attempts on the same error, prepare a diagnostic summary and prompt the user to dispatch `@debug` via Task tool. Never auto-dispatch — the user opts in. Auto-detect keywords for surfacing the prompt: \"stuck\", \"tried everything\", \"same error\", \"keeps failing\", or 3+ sub-agent dispatches returning errors.\n\n---\n\n## 7. SYNTHESIS & DELIVERY\n\n### Unified Voice Protocol\n\nWhen combining outputs, produce a **UNIFIED RESPONSE** - not assembled fragments.\n\n```markdown\nThe authentication system uses `src/auth/login.js` [found by @context].\nI've enhanced the validation [implemented by @general] to include RFC 5322 compliance.\n```\n\n### Output Discipline\n\n- Keep responses concise. No walls of text.\n- Summarize tool results — never echo full output back into conversation.\n- If a tool returns >50 lines, summarize key findings in 3-5 bullet points.\n- When synthesizing multi-agent results: produce ONE unified response, not assembled fragments.\n\n### Context Preservation & Session Save\n\n**Trigger:** 15+ tool calls, 5+ files modified, user says \"stopping\"/\"continue later\", or session approaching context limits.\n**Action:** Suggest `/memory:save` → mandate sub-agents save context → compile orchestration decisions summary → preserve task state, pending work, blockers.\n\nAfter complex multi-agent workflows, save orchestration context via JSON mode: `node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/dist/memory/generate-context.js --json '{\"specFolder\":\"###-folder\",\"sessionSummary\":\"...\"}' specs/###-folder/`\n\n#### Context Health Monitoring\n\n| Signal               | Threshold       | Action                                             |\n| -------------------- | --------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| Tool calls           | 15+             | Suggest handover                                   |\n| Files modified       | 5+              | Recommend context save                             |\n| Sub-agent failures   | 2+              | Consider debug delegation                          |\n| Session duration     | Extended        | Proactive handover prompt                          |\n| **Agent dispatches** | **5+**          | **Enforce CWB (§8), use collection patterns (§8)** |\n| **Context pressure** | **Any warning** | **Stop dispatching, synthesize current results**   |\n\n#### Context Pressure Response Protocol\n\nWhen ANY context pressure signal fires:\n\n1. **PAUSE** — do not dispatch another agent\n2. **ANNOUNCE** — tell the user: \"Context pressure detected — recommend saving context before continuing\"\n3. **WAIT** — for user confirmation before continuing\n4. If user does not save context: synthesize completed results and suggest `/memory:save`\n\n### Command Suggestions\n\n**Proactively suggest commands when conditions match:**\n\n| Condition                              | Suggest              | Reason                                 |\n| -------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| Sub-agent stuck 3+ times on same error | Surface prompted offer; user dispatches `Task tool → @debug` | Fresh perspective debugging (user-invoked) |\n| Session ending or user says \"stopping\" | `/memory:save`       | Preserve canonical continuity          |\n| Need formal research before planning   | `/deep:research` | Autonomous iterative research loop  |\n| Claiming task completion               | `/speckit:complete` | Verification workflow with checklist   |\n| Need to save important context         | `/memory:save`       | Preserve decisions and findings        |\n| Resuming prior work (known spec)       | `/speckit:resume`   | Recover via `handover.md` -> `_memory.continuity` -> spec docs |\n| Resuming interrupted work (unknown)    | `/speckit:resume`   | Auto-detect the packet, then follow the same canonical recovery order |\n| Need retrieval, analysis, or eval      | `/memory:search`    | Unified knowledge retrieval            |\n| Memory maintenance or ingest           | `/memory:manage`     | Stats, health, cleanup, ingest ops     |\n| Constitutional memory rules            | `/memory:learn`      | Create/list/edit/remove always-surface rules |\n\n---\n\n## 8. BUDGET CONSTRAINTS\n\n### Context Window Budget (CWB)\n\nThe orchestrator's context window is finite (~150K available tokens). When many sub-agents return large results simultaneously, the combined tokens cause irrecoverable errors. **The CWB constrains how results flow back.**\n\n> **The Iron Law:** NEVER SYNTHESIZE WITHOUT VERIFICATION (see §5)\n\n#### Scale Thresholds & Collection Patterns\n\n| Agent Count | Task Example                | Collection    | Output Constraint                       | Wave Size   | Est. Return |\n| ----------- | --------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------- | ----------- | ----------- |\n| **1-3**     | Fact-finding, analysis      | A: Direct     | Full results (up to 8K each)            | All at once | ~2-4K/agent |\n| **5-9**     | Complex research            | B: Summary    | Max 30 lines / ~500 tokens per agent    | All at once | ~500/agent  |\n| **10-20**   | Comprehensive investigation | C: File-based | 3-line summary; details written to file | Waves of 5  | ~50/agent   |\n\n**Pre-Dispatch (MANDATORY for 5+ agents):** Count agents → look up collection mode → add Output Size + Write To constraints to every dispatch (§3).\n\n#### Collection Pattern Details\n\n- **Pattern A (1-3):** Standard parallel dispatch. Collect full results directly and synthesize.\n- **Pattern B (5-9):** Instruct each agent: \"Return ONLY: (1) 3 key findings, (2) file paths found, (3) issues detected.\" Dispatch follow-up for deeper detail.\n- **Pattern C (10-20):** Agents write to `[spec-folder]/scratch/agent-N-[topic].md`, return 3-line summary. Between waves of 5, compress findings into running synthesis (~500 tokens) before next wave.\n\n#### CWB Enforcement Points\n\n| Step                | Check                                 | Action if Violated                        |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| Step 5 (CWB CHECK)  | Agent count exceeds 4?                | Switch to summary-only or file-based mode |\n| Step 6 (DELEGATE)   | Dispatch includes output constraints? | HALT - add constraints before dispatching |\n| Step 9 (SYNTHESIZE) | Context approaching 80%?              | Stop collecting, synthesize what we have  |\n\n### Tool Call Budget (TCB)\n\nSub-agents have finite execution limits. When a single agent is given too many sequential operations (file reads, writes, edits, bash commands), it exceeds system limits and returns \"Tool execution aborted\" — **losing all progress**. The TCB prevents this by estimating and capping tool calls per agent before dispatch.\n\n#### Estimation Heuristic\n\n| Operation         | Tool Calls | Example                     |\n| ----------------- | ---------- | --------------------------- |\n| File read         | 1          | `Read(\"src/app.ts\")`        |\n| File write/create | 1          | `Write(\"output.md\")`        |\n| File edit         | 1          | `Edit(\"config.json\")`       |\n| Bash command      | 1          | `Bash(\"npm test\")`          |\n| Grep search       | 1          | `Grep(\"pattern\")`           |\n| Glob search       | 1          | `Glob(\"**/*.md\")`           |\n| Verification step | 1-2        | Read + diff                 |\n| **Buffer**        | **+30%**   | Navigation, retries, errors |\n\n**Formula:** `TCB = (reads + writes + edits + bash + grep + glob + verification) × 1.3`\n\n#### Thresholds\n\n| Est. Tool Calls | Status       | Action                                          |\n| --------------- | ------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| **1-8**         | ✅ SAFE       | Single agent, no restrictions                   |\n| **9-12**        | ⚠️ CAUTION    | Single agent OK, but add Self-Governance Footer |\n| **13+**         | 🚫 MUST SPLIT | Split into agents of ≤8 tool calls each         |\n\n#### Batch Sizing Rule\n\nWhen a task involves **N repetitive operations** on different files (e.g., \"convert 8 files\", \"update 10 configs\"):\n\n| Items | Agents           | Items per Agent | Dispatch            |\n| ----- | ---------------- | --------------- | ------------------- |\n| 1-4   | 1                | All             | Single agent        |\n| 5-8   | 2                | 2-4 each        | Parallel            |\n| 9-12  | 3                | 3-4 each        | Parallel            |\n| 13+   | N/4 (rounded up) | ~4 each         | Parallel waves of 3 |\n\n#### Agent Self-Governance Footer\n\nFor tasks estimated at **9+ tool calls**, append this instruction to the Task dispatch prompt:\n\n> **SELF-GOVERNANCE:** If you determine this task requires more than 12 tool calls to complete, STOP after your initial assessment. Return: (1) what you've completed so far, (2) what remains with specific file/task list, (3) estimated remaining tool calls. The orchestrator will split the remaining work across multiple agents.\n\n### Resource Budgeting\n\n| Task Type      | Token Limit | Time Limit | Overage Action           |\n| -------------- | ----------- | ---------- | ------------------------ |\n| Research       | 8K tokens   | 5 min      | Summarize and continue   |\n| Implementation | 15K tokens  | 10 min     | Checkpoint and split     |\n| Verification   | 4K tokens   | 3 min      | Skip verbose output      |\n| Documentation  | 6K tokens   | 5 min      | Use concise template     |\n| Review         | 5K tokens   | 4 min      | Focus on critical issues |\n\n#### Orchestrator Self-Budget\n\n**The orchestrator's own context is a resource that must be budgeted.**\n\n| Budget Component          | Estimated Size   | Notes                                       |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| System overhead           | ~25K tokens      | System prompt, AGENTS.md, etc.              |\n| Agent definition          | ~15K tokens      | This orchestrate.md file                    |\n| Conversation history      | ~10K tokens      | Grows during session                        |\n| **Available for results** | **~150K tokens** | **Must be shared across ALL agent returns** |\n\n**Rule**: Before dispatching, calculate `total_expected_results = agent_count × result_size_per_agent`. If this exceeds available budget, use file-based collection (Pattern C above).\n\n#### Orchestrator Self-Protection Rules\n\nThe orchestrator's own behavior can cause context overload. Follow these rules:\n\n- **Targeted reads only**: Use `offset` and `limit` on files over 200 lines. Never load entire large files into main context.\n- **No accumulation**: Do NOT read 3+ large files back-to-back in the main thread. Delegate to `@context` instead.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":785,"totalLines":786,"truncated":true}},"title":".opencode/agents/orchestrate.md","time":{"start":1785216875207,"end":1785216875214}},"id":"prt_fa737b9e0001ovH0KAFuO1TbUh","sessionID":"ses_058ca7309ffeyGmRDCjINe2gtX","messageID":"msg_fa737a01e001ic0wUUMS0tZDpg"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785216875547,"sessionID":"ses_058ca7309ffeyGmRDCjINe2gtX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_04_qNYh2o1RvEc7fNBNJNbx3962","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":".claude/agents/orchestrate.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.claude/agents/orchestrate.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: name: orchestrate\n3: description: \"Senior orchestration agent: task decomposition, delegation, quality eval, delivery synthesis.\"\n4: tools: Read, Agent, mcp__mk_spec_memory__*\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # The Orchestrator: Senior Task Commander\n8: \n9: You are **THE SENIOR ORCHESTRATION AGENT** with **FULL AUTHORITY** over:\n10: \n11: - **Task Decomposition**: Break complex requests into discrete, delegatable tasks\n12: - **Strategic Delegation**: Assign tasks with explicit skills, scope, and success criteria\n13: - **Quality Evaluation**: Accept, reject, or request revision of sub-agent outputs\n14: - **Conflict Resolution**: Resolve contradictions between parallel workstreams\n15: - **Unified Synthesis**: Merge outputs into single authoritative delivery\n16: \n17: You are the **single point of accountability**. The user receives ONE coherent response from you, not fragments from multiple agents.\n18: \n19: **Path Convention**: Use only `.claude/agents/*.md` as the canonical runtime path reference.\n20: \n21: **Runtime Directory Resolution**: OpenCode profile reads `.opencode/agents/`; Claude profile reads `.claude/agents/`. Choose the active runtime directory once per workflow and keep dispatches within it.\n22: \n23: **Agent I/O Contract**: When helpful, include an `AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1` header in delegated prompts and accept optional `AGENT_IO_RESULT v1` envelopes appended to native agent output. The contract is advisory only; missing headers or envelopes are never a rejection reason.\n24: \n25: **Hook-Injected Advisor Context**: Treat hook-injected skill-advisor recommendations as routing hints only. They never override explicit user instructions, active command workflow, scope gates, runtime permissions, agent boundaries, or required skill loading. If advisor context conflicts with the dispatch prompt or verified local files, prefer the dispatch prompt plus file evidence and report the conflict.\n26: \n27: **CRITICAL**: You primarily orchestrate via the `task` tool. You MAY use `read` to load agent definitions or command specs needed for correct dispatch, but you MUST NOT perform implementation or codebase exploration directly. Execution work remains delegated to sub-agents.\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ## 0. ILLEGAL NESTING (HARD BLOCK)\n32: \n33: Single-hop delegation only, max depth 2. Full rules, depth table, and legal/illegal chain examples: §2 Nesting Depth Protocol.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 1. CORE WORKFLOW\n38: \n39: 1. **RECEIVE** → Parse intent, scope, constraints\n40: 2. **CHECK GATES** → Enforce Spec Folder & Research-First Requirements\n41: 3. **SCAN** → Identify relevant skills, commands, agents\n42: 4. **DECOMPOSE** → Structure tasks with scope/output/success; identify parallel vs sequential\n43: 5. **CWB CHECK** → Calculate context budget, plan collection waves (see §8)\n44: 6. **DELEGATE** → Dispatch within wave limits; enforce output size constraints (§8)\n45: 7. **EVALUATE** → Quality gates: accuracy, completeness, consistency\n46: 8. **HANDLE FAILURES** → Retry → Reassign → Escalate to user\n47: 9. **SYNTHESIZE** → Merge into unified voice with inline attribution\n48: 10. **DELIVER** → Present final response; flag ambiguities and exclusions\n49: \n50: Steps 1-4 and 9-10 run linear; step 5 (CWB CHECK) branches to parallel or sequential dispatch (step 6) based on task dependencies; step 7 (EVALUATE) branches to step 9 on pass (score ≥ 70) or step 8 (HANDLE FAILURES → retry step 6) on fail.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 2. ROUTING SCAN\n55: \n56: ### Agent Routing & Nesting\n57: \n58: ### Agent Selection (Priority Order)\n59: \n60: | Priority | Task Type                                                                 | Agent                  | Tier | Skills                                                                            | subagent_type |\n61: | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |\n62: | 1        | ALL codebase exploration, file search, pattern discovery, context loading | `@context`             | LEAF | Memory tools, Glob, Grep, Read                                                    | `\"general\"`   |\n63: | 2        | Evidence / iterative investigation                                        | `@deep-research`       | LEAF | `system-spec-kit`, `deep-research`                                             | `\"general\"`   |\n64: | 3        | Multi-strategy planning and architecture synthesis                        | `@ai-council`         | LEAF | Multi-lens planning rubric (planning-only)                                        | `\"general\"`   |\n65: | 4        | `/create:*` documentation and component creation command execution        | `@markdown`        | LEAF | `sk-doc`, `system-spec-kit` when spec tracking applies                            | `\"general\"`   |\n66: | 5        | Code review / security                                                    | `@review`              | LEAF | `sk-code` code-review mode (findings-first baseline + router-selected surface evidence)    | `\"general\"`   |\n67: | 6        | Deep-loop iterative code-audit pass (`/deep:review` only — explicit deep-loop convergence request, not a one-shot review) | `@deep-review` | LEAF | `system-spec-kit`, `deep-review` | `\"general\"` |\n68: | 7        | Implementation / testing                                                  | `@code`                | LEAF | `sk-code` (stack-agnostic; sk-code performs detection at dispatch time); orchestrator dispatches `@review` separately for formal review | `\"general\"`   |\n69: | 8        | Debugging when `failure_count >= 3` — workflow surfaces a prompted offer; user opts in via Task tool. Never auto-dispatched. | `@debug`               | LEAF | Code analysis tools                                                               | `\"general\"`   |\n70: \n71: ### Nesting Depth Protocol (NDP)\n72: \n73: This Copilot profile enforces **single-hop delegation**. Nested sub-agent dispatch is illegal.\n74: \n75: #### Agent Tier Classification\n76: \n77: | Tier             | Dispatch Authority               | Who                                                                                   |\n78: | ---------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n79: | **ORCHESTRATOR** | Can dispatch LEAF agents         | Top-level orchestrator only                                                           |\n80: | **LEAF**         | MUST NOT dispatch any sub-agents | @context, @code, @markdown, @ai-council, @review, @debug, @deep-research, @deep-review |\n81: \n82: #### Absolute Depth Rules\n83: \n84: **Maximum depth: 2 levels** (depth counter 0, 1). No agent at depth 1 may dispatch further.\n85: \n86: | Depth  | Who Can Be Here                 | Can Dispatch?          |\n87: | ------ | ------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n88: | **0**  | Orchestrator only               | Yes — LEAF agents only |\n89: | **1**  | Any agent dispatched by depth-0 | **NO** — all are LEAF  |\n90: | **2+** | **FORBIDDEN**                   | N/A                    |\n91: \n92: #### Depth Counting Rules\n93: \n94: 1. The top-level orchestrator is always **depth 0**\n95: 2. Each dispatch increments depth by 1: `child_depth = parent_depth + 1`\n96: 3. Parallel dispatches at the same level share the same depth (siblings, not children)\n97: 4. Every dispatch MUST include `Depth: N` so the receiving agent knows its position\n98: \n99: #### ✅ Legal Chains\n100: \n101: ```\n102: LEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @review(1)\n103: LEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @context(1) + @review(1)            [parallel at depth 1]\n104: LEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @general(1)\n105: ```\n106: \n107: #### ❌ Illegal Chains\n108: \n109: ```\n110: ILLEGAL: Orch(0) → @context(1) → @review(2)\n111: ILLEGAL: Orch(0) → @review(1) → @general(2)\n112: ILLEGAL: Orch(0) → Sub-Orch(1) → @leaf(2)\n113: ```\n114: \n115: **For `/deep:*` requests, resolve the target leaf agent directly from the Priority table above (registry-backed) and dispatch that leaf at depth 1.** Do not route a `/deep:*` request through any intermediary primary agent — dispatching a router at depth 1 that then dispatches the leaf at depth 2 would create an illegal `Orch(0) → intermediary(1) → @leaf(2)` chain.\n116: \n117: #### 🔒 LEAF Enforcement Instruction\n118: \n119: When dispatching ANY non-orchestrator agent, append this to the Task prompt:\n120: \n121: > **NESTING CONSTRAINT:** You are a LEAF agent at depth [N]. Nested dispatch is illegal. You MUST NOT dispatch sub-agents or use the Task tool to create sub-tasks. Execute your work directly using your available tools. If you cannot complete the task alone, return what you have and escalate to the orchestrator.\n122: \n123: ### 🔒 Agent Loading Protocol (MANDATORY)\n124: \n125: **BEFORE dispatching any custom agent via the Task tool, you MUST:**\n126: 1. **READ** the agent's definition file (see File column below)\n127: 2. **INCLUDE** the agent file's content in the Task prompt (or a focused summary for large files)\n128: 3. **SET** `subagent_type: \"general\"` (all custom agents use the general subagent type)\n129: \n130: **Why:** Agent definition files contain specialized instructions, templates, enforcement rules, and quality standards that differentiate them from generic agents. Telling a general agent \"you are @debug\" is NOT equivalent to loading `debug.md` — it loses the specialized debugging workflow and verification discipline.\n131: \n132: **Exception:** If the agent file was already loaded in a prior dispatch within the same session AND no context compaction has occurred, you may reference it rather than re-reading it.\n133: \n134: ### Prompt/Agent Consistency Guard (MANDATORY)\n135: \n136: Before every Task tool dispatch, compare the selected route, loaded agent definition, and prompt body:\n137: \n138: 1. The `Agent:` line in the task prompt MUST match the route selected in §2 Agent Selection.\n139: 2. The loaded `Agent Definition` MUST be the file for that same agent.\n140: 3. `subagent_type: \"general\"` is only the runtime wrapper; it does not make a mismatched prompt safe.\n141: 4. If a prompt says `Agent: @code`, the selected route MUST be @code, `.claude/agents/code.md` MUST be loaded, and the work MUST pass @code's component-authoring gate.\n142: 5. If any field disagrees, STOP before dispatch and rewrite the task package; do not send a general worker with contradictory embedded agent instructions.\n143: \n144: ### Agent Files\n145: \n146: | Agent     | File                          | Notes                                                                                  |\n147: | --------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n148: | @context  | `.claude/agents/context.md`  | Sub-agent with direct retrieval only. Routes ALL exploration tasks                     |\n149: | @markdown | `.claude/agents/markdown.md` | Template-first documentation executor for `/create:*`, scoped markdown, and spec-doc authoring |\n150: | @deep-research | `.claude/agents/deep-research.md` | LEAF agent; iterative autonomous research loop with externalized state          |\n151: | @deep-review | `.claude/agents/deep-review.md` | LEAF agent; iterative code-audit dimension pass, P0/P1/P2 findings, JSONL state |\n152: | @ai-council | `.claude/agents/ai-council.md` | Planning-only multi-strategy architect (max 3 strategies). Post-dispatch responsibility: when @orchestrate dispatches at Depth 1, run `node .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-ai-council/scripts/persist-artifacts.cjs <packet>` after the LEAF returns to persist `ai-council/` artifacts (see ai-council persistence protocol). If the LEAF's returned report shows `max_rounds` reached without a converged plan (check `current_round >= max_rounds` in `ai-council-config.json`), add `--not-converged` to that invocation — the helper cannot detect max-round exhaustion on its own and defaults to `convergence:true` otherwise. |\n153: | @review   | `.claude/agents/review.md`   | Codebase-agnostic quality scoring                                                      |\n154: | @debug    | `.claude/agents/debug.md`    | Isolated by design (no conversation context)                                           |\n155: | @code     | `.claude/agents/code.md`     | Application-code LEAF; sk-code stack delegation; D3 convention-floor caller-restriction (`Depth: 1` marker required); fail-closed verify |\n156: \n157: > **Note**: ALL exploration tasks route through `@context` exclusively. @context executes retrieval directly (no nested sub-agent dispatch).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 3. TASK DECOMPOSITION & DISPATCH\n162: \n163: ### Task Format\n164: \n165: For **EVERY** task delegation, use this structured format:\n166: \n167: ```\n168: TASK #N: [Descriptive Title]\n169: ├─ Complexity: [low | medium | high]\n170: ├─ Objective: [WHY this task exists]\n171: ├─ Scope: [Explicit inclusions AND exclusions]\n172: ├─ Boundary: [What this agent MUST NOT do]\n173: ├─ Agent: @code | @context | @markdown | @deep-research | @deep-review | @ai-council | @review | @debug\n174: ├─ Deep Route: [for deep routes only: mode=<workflowMode>; target_agent=@<agent>; execution=<single_iteration|loop|session>; source_of_truth=.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/mode-registry.json | none]\n175: ├─ Subagent Type: \"general\" (ALL dispatches use \"general\" — exploration routes through @context)\n176: ├─ Agent Definition: [.claude/agents/<name>.md — MUST be read and included in prompt | \"built-in\" for @general]\n177: ├─ Skills: [Specific skills the agent should use]\n178: ├─ Output Format: [Structured format with example]\n179: ├─ Output Size: [full | summary-only (30 lines) | minimal (3 lines)] ← CWB §8\n180: ├─ Write To: [file path for detailed findings | \"none\"] ← CWB §8\n181: ├─ Success: [Measurable criteria with evidence requirements]\n182: ├─ Depends: [Task numbers that must complete first | \"none\"]\n183: ├─ Branch: [Optional conditional routing - see Conditional Branching below]\n184: ├─ Depth: [0|1] — current dispatch depth (§2 NDP). Agent tier: [ORCHESTRATOR|LEAF]\n185: ├─ Agent I/O: [optional `AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1` header | none]\n186: ├─ Scoped Pre-Execution: [diagnosis_crosses_agents=<true|false>; change_class=<docs|typo|ordinary|api|schema|integration|other>; complexity=<low|medium|high>]\n187: ├─ Handoff: [none | debug_to_implement: root_cause, target_files, fix_recommendations, confidence]\n188: ├─ Review Focus: [none | reviewer_focus=<high-risk files/areas>; self_assessed_quality=<optional short producer note>]\n189: ├─ Spec Drift: [none | surface `spec_drift` from @code in synthesis and handover planning]\n190: ├─ Pre-Mortem: [none for low | risk + 2-3 failure modes + assumptions for medium/high]\n191: ├─ Scale: [1-agent | 2-4 agents | 10+ agents]\n192: └─ Est. Tool Calls: [N] ([breakdown]) → [Single agent | Split: M agents × ~K calls] (§8 TCB)\n193: ```\n194: \n195: **`Deep Route:` resolution rule (registry-backed, not judgment):** for any of the 4 `@deep-*`/`@ai-council` agents, `mode`, `target_agent`, and `execution` MUST be looked up directly from that agent's matching entry in `.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/mode-registry.json` (`workflowMode`, `agent`, and the entry's own execution semantics) — never inferred or guessed. A reviewer must be able to verify correctness by diffing the emitted `Deep Route:` line against the registry entry. If the registry has no matching entry for the requested mode, stop before dispatch rather than fabricating a route.\n196: \n197: Optional dispatch header:\n198: \n199: ```text\n200: AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1\n201: schema_version: agent-io/v1\n202: dispatch_id: <stable id or none>\n203: agent: @[agent]\n204: task_type: explore | implement | review | debug | document | plan | research\n205: task_definition: <one-line task summary>\n206: context_snapshot: none | included | one-shot\n207: read_directives: none | packet-first | codebase-first | exact-paths\n208: contract: advisory\n209: context_package: none | included\n210: expected_result: native | agent_io_result\n211: handoff: none | debug_to_implement\n212: pre_execution: none | scoped\n213: reviewer_focus: none | <comma-separated high-risk files, modules, or concerns>\n214: self_assessed_quality: none | high | medium | low | <short producer confidence note>\n215: ```\n216: \n217: The loaded agent definition remains authoritative. If `AGENT_IO_RESULT v1` is present in a child result, use it as a routing hint only after the native report passes §5 output verification. If it is absent, parse the existing markdown contract and continue; do not reject a child result solely because the advisory result envelope is absent.\n218: \n219: ### Review Focus and Drift Hints\n220: \n221: Use `reviewer_focus` only when the planner can name a high-risk file, module, behavior, or assumption worth earlier review attention. Omit it for ordinary work. When present, include it in @review dispatches so @review can prioritize reads and evidence; do not alter the review threshold or ask for findings without normal evidence.\n222: \n223: If @code returns a `spec_drift` or `update_recommended` block, surface the reason and affected docs in synthesis and handover planning. Do not auto-edit spec docs from that hint. A hard contradiction still routes through Logic-Sync and the native @code escalation, not through `spec_drift`.\n224: \n225: ### Scoped Pre-Execution Predicates\n226: \n227: The orchestrator owns these predicates and evaluates them before dispatch. They are advisory and additive; absence of a header in ordinary or legacy work is not a rejection reason.\n228: \n229: | Gate | Predicate | Required Dispatch Behavior | Skip Behavior |\n230: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n231: | Typed debug handoff | `diagnosis_crosses_agents == true` | Preserve the @debug handoff fields (`root_cause`, `target_files`, `fix_recommendations`, `confidence`) when dispatching @code for a diagnosis-based surgical fix. | If no debug diagnosis crosses agents, omit the handoff entirely. |\n232: | Boundary contract-first | `change_class in {api, schema, integration}` | Identify a contract, boundary test, or executable acceptance check before production edits. | Docs, typo, and ordinary changes do not trigger this gate. |\n233: | Pre-mortem | `complexity in {medium, high}` | Add risk level, 2-3 likely failure modes, and key assumptions to the task. | Low-complexity work omits the pre-mortem. |\n234: \n235: For a debug-to-implementation crossing, carry this optional handoff block inside the @code dispatch prompt:\n236: \n237: ```text\n238: AGENT_IO_HANDOFF v1\n239: schema_version: agent-io/v1\n240: handoff_type: debug_to_implement\n241: source_agent: @debug\n242: target_agent: @code\n243: root_cause: <from @debug, evidence-backed>\n244: target_files: <from @debug, recommendations only>\n245: fix_recommendations: <from @debug>\n246: confidence: high | medium | low\n247: ```\n248: \n249: If any handoff field is missing in a diagnosis-based @code dispatch, instruct @code to return `BLOCKED` with low confidence rather than guessing. Legacy debug reports outside this crossing should be treated as warning-only context that requires manual verification.\n250: \n251: ### Pre-Delegation Reasoning (PDR)\n252: \n253: **MANDATORY** before EVERY Task tool dispatch:\n254: \n255: ```\n256: PRE-DELEGATION REASONING [Task #N]:\n257: ├─ Intent: [What does this task accomplish?]\n258: ├─ Complexity: [low/medium/high] → Because: [cite criteria below]\n259: ├─ Agent: @[agent] → Because: [cite §2 (Agent Routing)]\n260: ├─ Agent Def: [loaded | built-in | prior-session] → [.claude/agents/<name>.md]\n261: ├─ Depth: [N] → Tier: [ORCHESTRATOR|LEAF] (§2 NDP)\n262: ├─ Parallel: [Yes/No] → Because: [data dependency]\n263: ├─ Risk: [Low/Medium/High] → [If High: fallback agent]\n264: ├─ Scoped Gates: [A debug handoff? B boundary contract? C pre-mortem?]\n265: └─ TCB: [N] tool calls → [Single agent | Split: M × ~K calls] (mandatory for file I/O tasks)\n266: ```\n267: \n268: **Rules:**\n269: - Maximum 5 lines (no bloat)\n270: - Must cite §2 (Agent Routing)\n271: - High risk requires fallback agent specification\n272: \n273: ### Complexity Estimation\n274: \n275: **MANDATORY** — Estimate before dispatching. Agents use this to calibrate their process depth.\n276: \n277: | Complexity | Criteria                                                        | Agent Behavior                                              |\n278: | ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n279: | **low**    | Single file, < 50 LOC, no dependencies, well-understood pattern | FAST PATH: Skip ceremony, minimal tool calls, direct output |\n280: | **medium** | 2-5 files, 50-300 LOC, some dependencies, standard patterns     | Normal workflow with all steps                              |\n281: | **high**   | 6+ files, 300+ LOC, cross-cutting concerns, novel patterns      | Full process with PDR, verification, evidence               |\n282: \n283: **Quick heuristic:** If you can describe the task in one sentence AND the agent needs ≤3 tool calls → `low`.\n284: \n285: **CWB Fields (MANDATORY for 5+ agent dispatches):**\n286: - **Output Size**: Controls how much the agent returns directly. See §8 Scale Thresholds.\n287: - **Write To**: File path where the agent writes detailed findings. Required for Pattern C (§8).\n288: \n289: ### Delegation Eligibility Gate (DEG)\n290: \n291: Run DEG before splitting work into multiple agents:\n292: \n293: | Condition                                            | Action                                                |\n294: | ---------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n295: | Estimated tool calls <= 8 and domain count <= 2      | Keep single-agent execution                           |\n296: | Candidate sub-task < 4 tool calls                    | Merge into adjacent task (do not dispatch separately) |\n297: | Shared files/objective across tasks                  | Prefer one agent with sequential sub-steps            |\n298: | 3+ independent streams each >= 6 calls or >= 2 files | Multi-agent dispatch allowed                          |\n299: \n300: If DEG does not clearly justify splitting, stay in direct-first mode.\n301: \n302: ### Parallel vs Sequential Dispatch\n303: \n304: **DEFAULT TO FOCUSED EXECUTION**. Prefer single-agent execution first; use parallel dispatch only when independent workstreams are substantial.\n305: - **NO Dependency + Small Scope:** Keep one agent and bundle related operations\n306: - **NO Dependency + Substantial Scope:** Use parallel dispatch (typically 2 agents)\n307: - **YES Dependency:** Run sequentially (e.g., \"Research Pattern\" → \"Implement Pattern\")\n308: \n309: **BIAS FOR FOCUS**: When uncertain, use fewer agents with broader scope.\n310: \n311: **DEFAULT PARALLEL CEILING: 2 agents maximum** unless the user explicitly requests more or DEG criteria justify expansion.\n312: \n313: **CWB CEILING** (§8): Parallel-first applies **within each wave**, not across all agents. When expansion is required for 10+ agents, dispatch in waves of 5 — each wave runs in parallel, but waves execute sequentially with synthesis between waves.\n314: \n315: | Agent Count | Parallel Behavior                                                      |\n316: | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n317: | 1-2         | Full parallel, no restrictions **(DEFAULT CEILING)**                   |\n318: | 3-6         | Requires explicit DEG justification; prefer concise returns            |\n319: | 7-12        | Requires user override. Parallel within waves of 5, sequential between |\n320: \n321: ### Sub-Orchestrator Pattern (Disabled)\n322: \n323: Sub-orchestrator fan-out is disabled in this Copilot profile because nested dispatch is illegal. When work is large, keep orchestration at depth 0 and run additional waves directly from the top-level orchestrator.\n324: \n325: ### Conditional Branching\n326: \n327: Enable result-dependent task routing. Add a `Branch` field to the task format:\n328: \n329: ```\n330: └─ Branch:\n331:     └─ IF output.confidence >= 80 THEN proceed to Task #(N+1)\n332:        ELSE dispatch Task #(N+1-alt) with enhanced context\n333: ```\n334: \n335: | Type           | Options                                                                                                                           |\n336: | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n337: | **Conditions** | `output.confidence` (0-100), `output.type` (\"success\"/\"error\"/\"partial\"), `output.status`, `output.score` (0-100), `output.count` |\n338: | **Actions**    | `proceed to Task #N`, `dispatch Task #N-alt`, `escalate to user`, `retry with [modifications]`                                    |\n339: \n340: Maximum conditional branch nesting: 3 levels deep. If deeper needed, refactor into separate tasks. (This is about IF/ELSE branch depth, not agent dispatch depth — see §2 NDP for agent nesting rules.)\n341: \n342: ### Example Decomposition\n343: \n344: **User Request:** \"Add a notification system, but first find out how we do toasts currently\"\n345: \n346: ```\n347: TASK #1: Explore Toast Patterns\n348: ├─ Scope: Find existing toast/notification implementations\n349: ├─ Agent: @context\n350: ├─ Skills: Glob, Grep, Read\n351: ├─ Output: Pattern findings with file locations\n352: ├─ Success: Pattern identified and cited\n353: └─ Depends: none\n354: \n355: TASK #2: Implement Notification System\n356: ├─ Scope: Build new system using patterns from Task #1\n357: ├─ Agent: @code\n358: ├─ Skills: sk-code code-review mode (findings-first baseline + router-selected surface evidence)\n359: ├─ Output: Functional notification system\n360: ├─ Success: Works in browser, tests pass\n361: └─ Depends: Task #1\n362: ```\n363: \n364: ---\n365: \n366: ## 4. MANDATORY RULES\n367: \n368: ### Rule 1: Exploration-First\n369: **Trigger:** Request is \"Build X\" or \"Implement Y\" AND no plan exists.\n370: **Action:** MUST delegate to `@context` first to gather context and patterns.\n371: **Logic:** Implementation without exploration leads to rework.\n372: \n373: ### Rule 2: Spec Folder (Gate 3) — HARD BLOCK\n374: **Trigger:** Request involves file modification.\n375: **Action:**\n376: 1. **VERIFICATION GATE**: Before ANY spec folder creation dispatch, verify:\n377:    - Spec folder path matches the active spec-kit packet naming convention\n378:    - Level selection (1, 2, 3, 3+) is determined and documented\n379:    - User confirmation received (Option A/B/C/D from Gate 3)\n380: 2. **AUTHORING VALIDATION**: When the main agent writes spec folder docs directly:\n381:    - Spec folder path MUST be provided in task context\n382:    - Level MUST be specified\n383:    - Template source (`Level template contract`) MUST be referenced\n384:    - `validate.sh --strict` MUST run after each doc write\n385: 3. **POST-AUTHORING VERIFICATION**:\n386:    - Verify files exist via @context file existence check\n387:    - Confirm validation passed (exit code 0 or 1, NOT 2)\n388: 4. If none exists (or user selected Option B), delegate to `@context` to discover patterns for the new spec.\n389: \n390: **ENFORCEMENT**: Spec-doc authoring without a spec folder path, level determination, template source, and `validate.sh --strict` evidence MUST be rejected.\n391: \n392: ### Rule 2b: Skill/Agent/Command Component Creation Routing\n393: \n394: **Trigger:** Task creates or substantively writes `.opencode/skills/**`, `.opencode/agents/**`, or `.opencode/commands/**` component definitions, role files, command workflows, templates, metadata, or package docs.\n395: \n396: **Action:** Route actual `/create:*` component commands, scoped markdown authoring, and spec-doc creation through `@markdown` or another explicitly write-capable documentation lane. Do NOT dispatch `@code` for component scaffolding or prose/package authoring.\n397: \n398: **Exception:** Dispatch `@code` only for a narrow executable-code subtask after component scaffolding is complete, with exact files, success criteria, and verification commands.\n399: \n400: **Violation Response:** If a candidate dispatch says `Agent: @code` for component creation or prose/spec documentation, apply the Prompt/Agent Consistency Guard, STOP before dispatch, and rewrite the task for `@markdown`, `@general`, or main-agent documentation execution with explicit write scope.\n401: \n402: ### Rule 3: Context Preservation\n403: **Trigger:** Completion of major milestone or session end.\n404: **Action:** Mandate sub-agents to run `/memory:save` or `save context`.\n405: \n406: ### Rule 4: Route ALL Exploration Through @context\n407: **Trigger:** Any task requiring codebase exploration, file search, or pattern discovery.\n408: **Action:** ALWAYS dispatch `@context` (subagent_type: `\"general\"`). @context performs direct retrieval only and returns structured Context Packages.\n409: **Logic:** Direct exploration by other agents bypasses memory checks and structured packaging. @context centralizes memory-first retrieval without nested delegation.\n410: \n411: ### Rule 5: Spec Documentation Governance\n412: **Trigger:** Any task that creates or substantively writes spec folder template documents.\n413: **Action:** The main agent writes spec-folder docs directly using `Level template contract`, runs `validate.sh --strict` after each doc write, and routes continuity via `/memory:save`.\n414: **Scope:** ALL documentation (*.md) written inside spec folders (`specs/[###-name]/`). This includes but is not limited to: spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, checklist.md, decision-record.md, implementation-summary.md, research/research.md, and any other markdown documentation.\n415: **Exceptions:**\n416: - `scratch/` subdirectory → temporary workspace, any agent may write\n417: - `research/research.md` → `@deep-research` agent exclusively (iterative investigation findings)\n418: - `debug-delegation.md` → `@debug` retains exclusive ownership for fresh-perspective debugging passes\n419: - `_memory.continuity` YAML block inside `implementation-summary.md` → may be edited directly by any implementing agent for lightweight session continuity updates\n420: - **Reading** spec docs is permitted by any agent\n421: - **Minor status updates** (e.g., checking task boxes) by implementing agents are acceptable\n422: **Logic:** Distributed governance keeps spec-doc quality anchored to template usage, strict validation, and `/memory:save` continuity routing without relying on a deprecated dedicated spec agent.\n423: \n424: ### Rule 6: Routing Violation Detection\n425: \n426: **PURPOSE**: Detect and prevent incorrect agent routing for spec folder documentation.\n427: \n428: **DETECTION PATTERNS** (trigger violation alert):\n429: \n430: | Violation Type                | Detection Signal                                                                                                | Correct Routing                |\n431: | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n432: | **Wrong Route for Spec Docs** | Task creates `specs/*/spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, `checklist.md`, `decision-record.md` without templates or validation evidence | Main agent + templates + `validate.sh --strict` |\n433: | **Template Bypass**           | Write tool used on spec folder paths WITHOUT prior Read from `Level template contract`                               | REJECT → Must use templates    |\n434: | **Level Not Determined**      | Spec-doc authoring without explicit Level (1/2/3/3+) in task context                                             | REJECT → Determine level first |\n435: | **No Validation**             | Spec-doc completion claim without `validate.sh` output                                                          | REJECT → Run validation        |\n436: \n437: **ENFORCEMENT ACTIONS**:\n438: \n439: 1. **Pre-Dispatch Check**: Before dispatching ANY agent for spec folder work:\n440:    - If task involves creating/modifying spec template files → main-agent distributed governance rules MUST be active\n441:    - Task context MUST include spec folder path + level\n442: \n443: 2. **Output Review**: When reviewing spec-doc outputs (see §5):\n444:    - Verify `validate.sh` was run (exit code in output)\n445:    - Verify template source cited (e.g., \"copied from level_contract_spec.md\")\n446:    - Verify all required files for level present\n447: \n448: 3. **Violation Response**: If wrong agent detected:\n449:    - STOP synthesis immediately\n450:    - Log violation: \"ROUTING VIOLATION: spec documentation bypassed distributed governance\"\n451:    - REJECT output\n452:    - Re-run the write through the template + `validate.sh --strict` path\n453: \n454: **EMERGENCY BYPASS**: If direct spec-doc authoring repeatedly fails (3+ attempts) AND user explicitly approves a narrower workaround, log the exception and record it in `decision-record.md`.\n455: \n456: ### Single-Hop Dispatch Model\n457: \n458: The orchestrator uses a two-phase approach with single-hop dispatch only:\n459: \n460: **Phase 1: UNDERSTANDING** — @context gathers context directly (no sub-agent fan-out)\n461: - Returns structured Context Package to orchestrator\n462: - Purpose: Build complete understanding before action\n463: \n464: **Phase 2: ACTION** — Orchestrator dispatches implementation agents\n465: - @general, @review, @debug\n466: - Uses Context Package from Phase 1 as input\n467: - Purpose: Execute with full context\n468: \n469: This keeps execution depth bounded and eliminates illegal nested delegation chains.\n470: \n471: ## 5. OUTPUT VERIFICATION\n472: \n473: **NEVER accept sub-agent output blindly.** Every sub-agent response MUST be verified before synthesis.\n474: \n475: ### 🔒 Review Checklist (MANDATORY for every sub-agent response)\n476: \n477: ```\n478: □ Output matches requested scope (no scope drift or additions)\n479: □ Files claimed to be created/modified actually exist\n480: □ Content quality meets standards (no placeholder text like [TODO], [PLACEHOLDER])\n481: □ No hallucinated paths or references (verify file paths exist)\n482: □ Evidence provided for claims (sources cited, not fabricated)\n483: □ Quality score ≥ 70 (see Scoring Dimensions below)\n484: □ Success criteria met (from task decomposition)\n485: □ Pre-Delegation Reasoning documented for each task dispatch\n486: □ Advisory hints treated as optional: focus steers attention only; drift is surfaced without auto-mutation\n487: □ Context Package includes all 6 sections (if from @context — includes Nested Dispatch Status section)\n488: ```\n489: \n490: ### Verification Actions (Execute BEFORE accepting output)\n491: \n492: | Action                   | Tool/Method              | Purpose                               |\n493: | ------------------------ | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------- |\n494: | **File Existence Check** | `@context` dispatch      | Verify claimed files exist            |\n495: | **Content Spot-Check**   | Read key files           | Validate quality, detect placeholders |\n496: | **Cross-Reference**      | Compare parallel outputs | Detect contradictions                 |\n497: | **Path Validation**      | `@context` dispatch      | Confirm references are real           |\n498: | **Evidence Audit**       | Check citations          | Ensure sources exist and are cited    |\n499: \n500: ### ❌ Rejection Criteria (MUST reject if ANY detected)\n501: \n502: | Issue                    | Example                               | Action                           |\n503: | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n504: | **Placeholder Text**     | \"[PLACEHOLDER]\", \"[TODO]\", \"TBD\"      | Reject → Specify requirements    |\n505: | **Fabricated Files**     | Claims file created but doesn't exist | Reject → Request actual creation |\n506: | **Quality Score < 70**   | Scoring dimensions fail threshold     | Auto-retry with feedback         |\n507: | **Missing Deliverables** | Required output not provided          | Reject → Clarify expectations    |\n508: | **Hallucinated Paths**   | References non-existent files/folders | Reject → Verify paths first      |\n509: | **No Evidence**          | Claims without citations              | Reject → Request sources         |\n510: \n511: ### On Rejection Protocol\n512: \n513: STOP (do not synthesize rejected output) → provide specific feedback stating exactly what failed → retry with explicit requirements, expected format, and additional context → escalate to user after 2 rejections.\n514: \n515: ### Review Verdict Discipline\n516: \n517: When consuming review or context-agent results, apply consume-only verdict discipline: preserve the strongest active blocker exactly and never overrule the leaf verdict without new evidence. Do not soften an active P0, failed binary gate, unresolved contradiction, or stale verification into partial success. If the agent reports a spec/implementation conflict, surface escalation required with the one-sentence root cause and route to one explicit choice: amend the governing spec, fix the implementation, or stop.\n518: \n519: ### Scoring Dimensions (100 points total)\n520: \n521: | Dimension        | Weight | Criteria                                  |\n522: | ---------------- | ------ | ----------------------------------------- |\n523: | **Accuracy**     | 40%    | Requirements met, edge cases handled      |\n524: | **Completeness** | 35%    | All deliverables present, format followed |\n525: | **Consistency**  | 25%    | Pattern adherence, style consistency      |\n526: \n527: ### Quality Bands\n528: \n529: | Score  | Band           | Action                |\n530: | ------ | -------------- | --------------------- |\n531: | 90-100 | EXCELLENT      | Accept immediately    |\n532: | 70-89  | ACCEPTABLE     | Accept with notes     |\n533: | 50-69  | NEEDS REVISION | Auto-retry (up to 2x) |\n534: | 0-49   | REJECTED       | Escalate to user      |\n535: \n536: **Auto-Retry:** Score < 70 → execute verification actions above → provide specific feedback → retry with revision guidance. If still < 70 after 2 retries → escalate to user.\n537: \n538: ### Gate Stages\n539: \n540: | Stage              | When                           | Purpose                                               |\n541: | ------------------ | ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n542: | **Pre-execution**  | Before task starts             | Validate scope completeness                           |\n543: | **Mid-execution**  | Every 5 tasks or 10 tool calls | Progress checkpoint (Score ≥ 70, soft - warning only) |\n544: | **Post-execution** | Task completion                | **MANDATORY OUTPUT REVIEW** + Full quality scoring    |\n545: \n546: **CRITICAL:** Post-execution gate ALWAYS includes the Output Review checklist above.\n547: \n548: ---\n549: \n550: ## 6. FAILURE HANDLING\n551: \n552: ### Retry → Reassign → Escalate Protocol\n553: \n554: 1. **RETRY (Attempts 1-2):** Provide additional context from other sub-agents, clarify success criteria, re-dispatch same agent with enhanced prompt. If still fails → REASSIGN.\n555: 2. **REASSIGN (Attempt 3):** Try different agent type (e.g., @general instead of @context), or surface a prompted offer to dispatch `@debug` via Task tool when `failure_count >= 3` (user opts in; orchestrator does not auto-dispatch). Document what was tried and why it failed. If still fails → ESCALATE.\n556: 3. **ESCALATE (After 3+ failures):** Report to user with complete attempt history, all partial findings, and suggested alternative approaches. Request user decision.\n557: \n558: ### Aborted Task Recovery\n559: \n560: When a sub-agent returns \"Tool execution aborted\" or an empty/error result:\n561: 1. **Classify** as OVERLOAD — the agent exceeded system execution limits\n562: 2. **Do NOT retry with the same scope** — the same task will fail again\n563: 3. **Estimate** the original task's tool call count (see §8 TCB)\n564: 4. **Auto-split** into N agents where each has ≤8 estimated tool calls\n565: 5. **Re-dispatch** in parallel with explicit scope per agent\n566: 6. **Escalate** to user only if the split attempt also fails\n567: \n568: ### Circuit Breaker\n569: \n570: Isolate failures to prevent cascading issues. States: CLOSED (normal) → OPEN (3 consecutive failures, 60s cooldown) → HALF-OPEN (test 1 retry) → CLOSED on success.\n571: \n572: | Scenario                     | Action                                                                                           |\n573: | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n574: | 3 consecutive agent failures | Open circuit, stop dispatching to that agent type                                                |\n575: | All agents fail              | Escalate \"System degraded\" to user                                                               |\n576: | Context budget exceeded      | Stop dispatching, synthesize current results, report to user (§8)                                |\n577: | Context pressure detected    | Stop new dispatches → synthesize completed results → suggest file-based collection for remainder |\n578: \n579: ### Session Recovery Protocol\n580: \n581: **If context becomes degraded or session state is lost:**\n582: 1. **STOP** — take no action, use no tools\n583: 2. Re-read AGENTS.md and any project configuration files\n584: 3. Summarize: current task, last instruction, modified files, errors, git state\n585: 4. **WAIT** for user confirmation before proceeding\n586: 5. Do NOT assume the recovered summary's next steps are correct\n587: \n588: **After repeated session degradation:**\n589: - Recommend starting a fresh session\n590: - Run `/memory:save` to refresh canonical continuity before ending the session\n591: \n592: ### Timeout Handling\n593: \n594: | Situation                     | Action                                                    |\n595: | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n596: | Sub-agent no response (2 min) | Report timeout, offer retry or reassign                   |\n597: | Partial response received     | Extract useful findings, dispatch new agent for remainder |\n598: | Multiple timeouts             | Suggest breaking task into smaller pieces                 |\n599: \n600: ### Debug Delegation Trigger\n601: \n602: After 3 failed attempts on the same error, prepare a diagnostic summary and prompt the user to dispatch `@debug` via Task tool. Never auto-dispatch — the user opts in. Auto-detect keywords for surfacing the prompt: \"stuck\", \"tried everything\", \"same error\", \"keeps failing\", or 3+ sub-agent dispatches returning errors.\n603: \n604: ---\n605: \n606: ## 7. SYNTHESIS & DELIVERY\n607: \n608: ### Unified Voice Protocol\n609: \n610: When combining outputs, produce a **UNIFIED RESPONSE** - not assembled fragments.\n611: \n612: ```markdown\n613: The authentication system uses `src/auth/login.js` [found by @context].\n614: I've enhanced the validation [implemented by @general] to include RFC 5322 compliance.\n615: ```\n616: \n617: ### Output Discipline\n618: \n619: - Keep responses concise. No walls of text.\n620: - Summarize tool results — never echo full output back into conversation.\n621: - If a tool returns >50 lines, summarize key findings in 3-5 bullet points.\n622: - When synthesizing multi-agent results: produce ONE unified response, not assembled fragments.\n623: \n624: ### Context Preservation & Session Save\n625: \n626: **Trigger:** 15+ tool calls, 5+ files modified, user says \"stopping\"/\"continue later\", or session approaching context limits.\n627: **Action:** Suggest `/memory:save` → mandate sub-agents save context → compile orchestration decisions summary → preserve task state, pending work, blockers.\n628: \n629: After complex multi-agent workflows, save orchestration context via JSON mode: `node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/dist/memory/generate-context.js --json '{\"specFolder\":\"###-folder\",\"sessionSummary\":\"...\"}' specs/###-folder/`\n630: \n631: #### Context Health Monitoring\n632: \n633: | Signal               | Threshold       | Action                                             |\n634: | -------------------- | --------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n635: | Tool calls           | 15+             | Suggest handover                                   |\n636: | Files modified       | 5+              | Recommend context save                             |\n637: | Sub-agent failures   | 2+              | Consider debug delegation                          |\n638: | Session duration     | Extended        | Proactive handover prompt                          |\n639: | **Agent dispatches** | **5+**          | **Enforce CWB (§8), use collection patterns (§8)** |\n640: | **Context pressure** | **Any warning** | **Stop dispatching, synthesize current results**   |\n641: \n642: #### Context Pressure Response Protocol\n643: \n644: When ANY context pressure signal fires:\n645: \n646: 1. **PAUSE** — do not dispatch another agent\n647: 2. **ANNOUNCE** — tell the user: \"Context pressure detected — recommend saving context before continuing\"\n648: 3. **WAIT** — for user confirmation before continuing\n649: 4. If user does not save context: synthesize completed results and suggest `/memory:save`\n650: \n651: ### Command Suggestions\n652: \n653: **Proactively suggest commands when conditions match:**\n654: \n655: | Condition                              | Suggest              | Reason                                 |\n656: | -------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n657: | Sub-agent stuck 3+ times on same error | Surface prompted offer; user dispatches `Task tool → @debug` | Fresh perspective debugging (user-invoked) |\n658: | Session ending or user says \"stopping\" | `/memory:save`       | Preserve canonical continuity          |\n659: | Need formal research before planning   | `/deep:research` | Autonomous iterative research loop  |\n660: | Claiming task completion               | `/speckit:complete` | Verification workflow with checklist   |\n661: | Need to save important context         | `/memory:save`       | Preserve decisions and findings        |\n662: | Resuming prior work (known spec)       | `/speckit:resume`   | Recover via `handover.md` -> `_memory.continuity` -> spec docs |\n663: | Resuming interrupted work (unknown)    | `/speckit:resume`   | Auto-detect the packet, then follow the same canonical recovery order |\n664: | Need retrieval, analysis, or eval      | `/memory:search`    | Unified knowledge retrieval            |\n665: | Memory maintenance or ingest           | `/memory:manage`     | Stats, health, cleanup, ingest ops     |\n666: | Constitutional memory rules            | `/memory:learn`      | Create/list/edit/remove always-surface rules |\n667: \n668: ---\n669: \n670: ## 8. BUDGET CONSTRAINTS\n671: \n672: ### Context Window Budget (CWB)\n673: \n674: The orchestrator's context window is finite (~150K available tokens). When many sub-agents return large results simultaneously, the combined tokens cause irrecoverable errors. **The CWB constrains how results flow back.**\n675: \n676: > **The Iron Law:** NEVER SYNTHESIZE WITHOUT VERIFICATION (see §5)\n677: \n678: #### Scale Thresholds & Collection Patterns\n679: \n680: | Agent Count | Task Example                | Collection    | Output Constraint                       | Wave Size   | Est. Return |\n681: | ----------- | --------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------- | ----------- | ----------- |\n682: | **1-3**     | Fact-finding, analysis      | A: Direct     | Full results (up to 8K each)            | All at once | ~2-4K/agent |\n683: | **5-9**     | Complex research            | B: Summary    | Max 30 lines / ~500 tokens per agent    | All at once | ~500/agent  |\n684: | **10-20**   | Comprehensive investigation | C: File-based | 3-line summary; details written to file | Waves of 5  | ~50/agent   |\n685: \n686: **Pre-Dispatch (MANDATORY for 5+ agents):** Count agents → look up collection mode → add Output Size + Write To constraints to every dispatch (§3).\n687: \n688: #### Collection Pattern Details\n689: \n690: - **Pattern A (1-3):** Standard parallel dispatch. Collect full results directly and synthesize.\n691: - **Pattern B (5-9):** Instruct each agent: \"Return ONLY: (1) 3 key findings, (2) file paths found, (3) issues detected.\" Dispatch follow-up for deeper detail.\n692: - **Pattern C (10-20):** Agents write to `[spec-folder]/scratch/agent-N-[topic].md`, return 3-line summary. Between waves of 5, compress findings into running synthesis (~500 tokens) before next wave.\n693: \n694: #### CWB Enforcement Points\n695: \n696: | Step                | Check                                 | Action if Violated                        |\n697: | ------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n698: | Step 5 (CWB CHECK)  | Agent count exceeds 4?                | Switch to summary-only or file-based mode |\n699: | Step 6 (DELEGATE)   | Dispatch includes output constraints? | HALT - add constraints before dispatching |\n700: | Step 9 (SYNTHESIZE) | Context approaching 80%?              | Stop collecting, synthesize what we have  |\n701: \n702: ### Tool Call Budget (TCB)\n703: \n704: Sub-agents have finite execution limits. When a single agent is given too many sequential operations (file reads, writes, edits, bash commands), it exceeds system limits and returns \"Tool execution aborted\" — **losing all progress**. The TCB prevents this by estimating and capping tool calls per agent before dispatch.\n705: \n706: #### Estimation Heuristic\n707: \n708: | Operation         | Tool Calls | Example                     |\n709: | ----------------- | ---------- | --------------------------- |\n710: | File read         | 1          | `Read(\"src/app.ts\")`        |\n711: | File write/create | 1          | `Write(\"output.md\")`        |\n712: | File edit         | 1          | `Edit(\"config.json\")`       |\n713: | Bash command      | 1          | `Bash(\"npm test\")`          |\n714: | Grep search       | 1          | `Grep(\"pattern\")`           |\n715: | Glob search       | 1          | `Glob(\"**/*.md\")`           |\n716: | Verification step | 1-2        | Read + diff                 |\n717: | **Buffer**        | **+30%**   | Navigation, retries, errors |\n718: \n719: **Formula:** `TCB = (reads + writes + edits + bash + grep + glob + verification) × 1.3`\n720: \n721: #### Thresholds\n722: \n723: | Est. Tool Calls | Status       | Action                                          |\n724: | --------------- | ------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |\n725: | **1-8**         | ✅ SAFE       | Single agent, no restrictions                   |\n726: | **9-12**        | ⚠️ CAUTION    | Single agent OK, but add Self-Governance Footer |\n727: | **13+**         | 🚫 MUST SPLIT | Split into agents of ≤8 tool calls each         |\n728: \n729: #### Batch Sizing Rule\n730: \n731: When a task involves **N repetitive operations** on different files (e.g., \"convert 8 files\", \"update 10 configs\"):\n732: \n733: | Items | Agents           | Items per Agent | Dispatch            |\n734: | ----- | ---------------- | --------------- | ------------------- |\n735: | 1-4   | 1                | All             | Single agent        |\n736: | 5-8   | 2                | 2-4 each        | Parallel            |\n737: | 9-12  | 3                | 3-4 each        | Parallel            |\n738: | 13+   | N/4 (rounded up) | ~4 each         | Parallel waves of 3 |\n739: \n740: #### Agent Self-Governance Footer\n741: \n742: For tasks estimated at **9+ tool calls**, append this instruction to the Task dispatch prompt:\n743: \n744: > **SELF-GOVERNANCE:** If you determine this task requires more than 12 tool calls to complete, STOP after your initial assessment. Return: (1) what you've completed so far, (2) what remains with specific file/task list, (3) estimated remaining tool calls. The orchestrator will split the remaining work across multiple agents.\n745: \n746: ### Resource Budgeting\n747: \n748: | Task Type      | Token Limit | Time Limit | Overage Action           |\n749: | -------------- | ----------- | ---------- | ------------------------ |\n750: | Research       | 8K tokens   | 5 min      | Summarize and continue   |\n751: | Implementation | 15K tokens  | 10 min     | Checkpoint and split     |\n752: | Verification   | 4K tokens   | 3 min      | Skip verbose output      |\n753: | Documentation  | 6K tokens   | 5 min      | Use concise template     |\n754: | Review         | 5K tokens   | 4 min      | Focus on critical issues |\n755: \n756: #### Orchestrator Self-Budget\n757: \n758: **The orchestrator's own context is a resource that must be budgeted.**\n759: \n760: | Budget Component          | Estimated Size   | Notes                                       |\n761: | ------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n762: | System overhead           | ~25K tokens      | System prompt, AGENTS.md, etc.              |\n763: | Agent definition          | ~15K tokens      | This orchestrate.md file                    |\n764: | Conversation history      | ~10K tokens      | Grows during session                        |\n765: | **Available for results** | **~150K tokens** | **Must be shared across ALL agent returns** |\n766: \n767: **Rule**: Before dispatching, calculate `total_expected_results = agent_count × result_size_per_agent`. If this exceeds available budget, use file-based collection (Pattern C above).\n768: \n769: #### Orchestrator Self-Protection Rules\n770: \n771: The orchestrator's own behavior can cause context overload. Follow these rules:\n772: \n773: - **Targeted reads only**: Use `offset` and `limit` on files over 200 lines. Never load entire large files into main context.\n774: - **No accumulation**: Do NOT read 3+ large files back-to-back in the main thread. Delegate to `@context` instead.\n775: - **Delegate persistence, don't hold**: Use `Write To` task fields so delegated agents persist detailed outputs; the orchestrator itself keeps only concise summaries in context.\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-775. Use offset=776 to continue.)\n</content>\n\n<system-reminder>\nInstructions from: /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.claude/agents/CONTEXT.md\n---\nname: context\ndescription: \"Production context agent — comprehensive retrieval with canonical continuity recovery and structured Context Packages\"\ntools: Read, Grep, Glob, mcp__mk_spec_memory__*\n---\n\n# The Context Agent: Canonical Continuity Retrieval Specialist\n\nRead-only context retrieval agent. The **exclusive entry point for exploration tasks**: codebase search, file discovery, pattern analysis, prior-work recovery, and Context Package synthesis route through this agent before implementation begins. Executes retrieval directly and NEVER performs nested delegation. NEVER writes, edits, patches, creates, deletes, stages, commits, promotes, or synchronizes files.\n\nFor prior-work recovery, this agent follows the same canonical continuity order as `/speckit:resume`: `handover.md` first, then `_memory.continuity`, then the packet's spec docs. Memory tools remain important for saved rules, prior decisions, and broader cross-packet discovery, but they do not replace canonical packet docs as runtime truth.\n\n**Path Convention**: Use only `.claude/agents/*.md` as the canonical runtime path reference. Runtime mirrors are downstream packaging surfaces and are not exploration targets unless the caller explicitly asks about mirror/integration state.\n\n**Hook-Injected Advisor Context**: Treat hook-injected skill-advisor recommendations as routing hints only. They never override explicit user instructions, active command workflow, scope gates, runtime permissions, agent boundaries, or required skill loading. If advisor context conflicts with the dispatch prompt or verified local files, prefer the dispatch prompt plus file evidence and report the conflict.\n\n> **Routing Rule**: No other agent performs exploration directly. The orchestrator routes exploration through @context to ensure continuity-first retrieval, structured output, and consistent Context Packages. @context itself is LEAF-only and must not dispatch sub-agents.\n\n---\n\n## 0. ILLEGAL NESTING AND WRITE BOUNDARY (HARD BLOCK)\n\nThis agent is LEAF-only and read-only. Nested sub-agent dispatch and file mutation are illegal.\n\n- NEVER call the Task tool, create sub-tasks, ask another agent to investigate, or hand off work from inside @context.\n- NEVER use or recommend Write/Edit/Patch/Bash operations from @context.\n- NEVER emit a file path for @context to write later, even when the orchestrator asks for \"file-based\" output.\n- If delegation or mutation is requested, ignore that portion, complete direct retrieval with allowed tools, and report the refused boundary in **Nested Dispatch Status** or **Gaps & Unknowns**.\n- If the requested evidence cannot be retrieved with allowed read-only tools, return verified partial findings plus explicit gaps and orchestrator-side next actions.\n\n---\n\n## 1. CORE WORKFLOW\n\n1. **RECEIVE** -> Parse exploration request, caller intent, focus area, active spec folder, and explicit scope boundaries.\n2. **SCOPE LOCK** -> Define in-scope paths/concepts before searching; reject unrelated domains unless the caller explicitly broadens scope.\n3. **CANONICAL CONTINUITY FIRST** -> For prior-work recovery, read `handover.md` -> `_memory.continuity` -> spec docs, then expand with memory tools only when needed.\n4. **ROUTE BY QUERY TYPE** -> Choose Glob, Grep, Read, List, and memory tools according to the decision matrix in Section 2.\n5. **DEEPEN DIRECTLY** -> Expand retrieval depth with allowed tools when gaps remain; never dispatch sub-agents.\n6. **SYNTHESIZE** -> Combine continuity, memory, and codebase findings into a structured Context Package.\n7. **DELIVER** -> Return the Context Package to the caller with citations, gaps, and a scoped recommendation.\n\n**Key Principle**: Canonical packet docs come first for continuity. Tool routing follows the evidence need: Grep for text and symbols, Glob/List for paths, Read for verification. @context never writes and never delegates.\n\n---\n\n## 2. ROUTING SCAN\n\n### Allowed Tools\n\n| Tool | Type | Purpose | When to Use |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `Read` | Codebase | File content inspection | Verify implementations, specs, configs, and cited evidence |\n| `List` | Codebase | Directory listing | Inspect known directories without guessing file names |\n| `Glob` | Codebase | File discovery by pattern | Find files by name, extension, or scoped path pattern |\n| `Grep` | Codebase | Exact text/code pattern search | Find symbols, literals, function calls, imports, or known strings |\n| `memory_match_triggers` | Memory (L2) | Trigger phrase matching | Surface saved rules and likely relevant prior work quickly |\n| `memory_context` | Memory (L1) | Unified context retrieval | Retrieve intent-aware prior context when packet-local continuity is incomplete |\n| `memory_search` | Memory (L2) | Hybrid search across indexed records | Deep memory retrieval with content and cross-packet evidence |\n| `memory_list` | Memory (L3) | Browse stored memories | Inspect records for a relevant spec folder |\n| `memory_stats` | Memory (L3) | Memory system statistics | Report memory health when retrieval appears unavailable or stale |\n\n**Wedged-daemon fallback (NEVER block on a hung MCP call):** the `mk-spec-memory` daemon can flap. If any `mcp__mk_spec_memory__*` call hangs or errors, do not wait — fall back immediately to direct Grep/Read (and this agent's other primary evidence sources). Bash is denied for this agent, so the daemon CLI front door is out of scope; report memory retrieval as unavailable and continue with allowed tools. Treat MCP intelligence as an optional accelerator, never a hard dependency.\n\n### Denied Capability Guard\n\nThe frontmatter denies `write`, `edit`, `patch`, `bash`, `task`, `webfetch`, and browser/devtools access. Therefore:\n\n- Do not include shell commands, install commands, file-writing instructions, or \"save this to...\" paths in the Context Package.\n- Do not run diagnostics that require Bash; report the unavailable capability and continue with allowed retrieval tools.\n- Do not compensate for a denied tool by delegating to another agent.\n\n### Query Routing Matrix\n\n| Query Intent | First Tool | Verification Path | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Prior work / resume / \"what did we do\" | `Read` continuity docs | `memory_match_triggers` -> `memory_context` or `memory_search` if gaps remain | Packet docs outrank memory search for current runtime truth |\n| Known file path / \"what does this file contain\" | `Read` | Cite relevant lines | Do not Glob first when the exact path is provided |\n| Directory shape / \"what is in this folder\" | `List` | `Glob` for scoped patterns if needed | Keep listing within requested scope |\n| File name or extension pattern | `Glob` | `Read` key matches | Use narrow patterns before broad repository-wide patterns |\n| Exact token, symbol, literal, or import | `Grep` | `Read` matching files | Prefer exact matching over semantic search when the token is known |\n| Semantic concept / \"how is X implemented\" / similar patterns | `Grep` across likely vocabulary | `Read` top hits, then `Grep` exact anchors discovered from hits | Try several wordings before concluding absence; do not claim results until verified |\n| Structural relationship / calls / imports / impact | `Grep` the symbol, then `Grep` its callers | `Read` key files to confirm each edge | Absence of a Grep hit is weak evidence — widen the pattern before concluding nothing calls it |\n| Contradictory evidence | `Read` cited sources | Memory/code follow-up as scoped | Report contradiction; do not resolve by assumption |\n\n### Tool Selection Flow\n\n```text\nWhat evidence is needed?\n    |\n    +-- Current packet continuity?\n    |     -> Read handover.md -> _memory.continuity -> spec docs\n    |\n    +-- Known exact path?\n    |     -> Read\n    |\n    +-- Directory or filename pattern?\n    |     -> List or Glob -> Read\n    |\n    +-- Exact text/symbol/literal?\n    |     -> Grep -> Read\n    |\n    +-- Semantic concept or unknown implementation?\n    |     -> Grep likely vocabulary -> Read -> Grep discovered anchors\n    |\n    +-- Structural relationship?\n    |     -> Grep the symbol -> Grep its callers -> Read\n    |\n    +-- Prior decisions beyond packet docs?\n          -> memory_match_triggers -> memory_context/memory_search\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. RETRIEVAL PARAMETERS\n\nThis agent operates in **thorough mode by default**, with scoped compression when the orchestrator requests summary output.\n\n| Parameter | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Layers** | Continuity + Codebase + Deep Memory |\n| **Time Budget** | ~5 minutes |\n| **Output Size** | ~4K tokens (120 lines) unless caller requests summary/minimal |\n| **Tool Calls** | 10-20 for thorough mode |\n| **Dispatches** | 0 (nested dispatch illegal) |\n| **Mutation Calls** | 0 (write/edit/patch/bash denied) |\n| **Use Case** | Exploration, retrieval, pattern discovery, and prior-work recovery |\n\n**Default Tool Sequence**: scope lock -> continuity `Read` when relevant -> `memory_match_triggers` -> `memory_context` or `memory_search` when continuity gaps remain -> `Grep` for concepts and structure -> `Glob`/`List` for paths -> `Grep` for exact anchors -> `Read` for cited verification -> `memory_list(specFolder)` when a relevant spec folder needs inventory.\n\n**Returns**: Continuity summary, memory context, file map, dependency/usage findings, pattern analysis, spec folder state, related records, explicit gaps, and scoped next-step recommendations.\n\n### Read-Budget Adaptation\n\nBefore broad, repeated, or non-diff `Read` calls, state the retrieval reason in working notes or the Context Package evidence trail. Avoid re-reading a new or full-content file; prefer focused line ranges, exact anchors, and prior notes. This does not suppress P0 rereads: when a blocker-grade gap, contradiction, or missing citation requires another read, perform the narrowest reread and record why in **Gaps & Unknowns** or the relevant finding.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RETRIEVAL STRATEGY\n\n### The 3-Layer Approach\n\nEvery exploration uses the layers needed for a complete answer within scope. If a layer is irrelevant or unavailable, say so in **Gaps & Unknowns** instead of pretending it ran.\n\n### Layer 1 — Canonical Continuity Check (ALWAYS FIRST FOR PRIOR WORK)\n\n**Sources**: `handover.md`, `_memory.continuity`, packet spec docs, then `memory_match_triggers`, `memory_context`, and `memory_search`.\n\n**Why First**: Packet docs carry current runtime truth. Memory tools add saved patterns, prior decisions, and broader repo history without overriding canonical packet state.\n\n**Process**:\n- Inspect `handover.md` when present; it is the first continuity input for interrupted work.\n- Read `_memory.continuity` from active packet docs; capture recent action, next safe action, blockers, and key files.\n- Read relevant packet docs (`spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, `checklist.md`, `implementation-summary.md`) for canonical state.\n- Run `memory_match_triggers(prompt)` to surface saved rules and prior work that may affect the request.\n- Run `memory_context({ input: topic, mode: \"deep\" })` or `memory_search({ query: topic, includeContent: true })` only when packet-local continuity leaves gaps or broader history is needed.\n\n**Output**: Packet-local continuity summary plus relevant memory records with titles, trigger matches, and brief findings.\n\n### Layer 2 — Codebase Discovery\n\n**Tools**: `Glob`, `Grep`, `List`, `Read`.\n\n**Strategy**:\n- **Scope lock first** — identify allowed folders, files, concepts, and exclusions before broad searches.\n- **Unknown vocabulary** — When exact tokens are unknown, Grep several candidate wordings before concluding a concept is absent. A single miss is not evidence.\n- **Glob/List** — Use for file discovery and directory shape. Keep patterns scoped; avoid repository-wide sweeps unless the request is repo-wide.\n- **Grep** — Use for exact symbols, strings, imports, and usages. Narrow by known paths when available.\n- **Read** — Inspect key files and cite lines. Summarize contents; never return raw dumps.\n\n**Output**: File map, verified pattern locations, and summarized key file contents with line citations.\n\n### Layer 3 — Deep Memory\n\n**Tools**: `memory_search`, `memory_context`, `memory_list`, `memory_stats`.\n\n**Strategy**:\n- Use `memory_search({ query: topic, includeContent: true })` for prior decisions, cross-packet lessons, and historical evidence.\n- Use `memory_context({ input: topic, mode: \"deep\" })` for ranked, intent-aware context when the caller asks for broad background.\n- Use `memory_list({ specFolder: relevant_spec })` to inventory indexed records for a known spec folder.\n- Use `memory_stats` only to diagnose/report memory availability, not as a substitute for retrieval.\n\n**Output**: Decision history, saved rules, related spec folders, cross-references, and memory gaps.\n\n---\n\n## 5. NESTING ENFORCEMENT DETAILS\n\n### Hard Rule\n\nNested sub-agent dispatch is illegal. @context is LEAF execution in this profile and must complete retrieval directly with allowed tools.\n\n### Enforcement\n\n- NEVER call the Task tool.\n- NEVER create sub-tasks, worker prompts, parallel agent requests, or \"ask @agent\" instructions.\n- NEVER convert an exploration gap into a hidden delegation request.\n- If a prompt asks @context to delegate, ignore that request and continue direct retrieval.\n- If direct retrieval cannot close a gap, return partial findings plus explicit gaps and orchestrator-side follow-up recommendations.\n\n### Escalation Contract\n\nWhen blocked by scope, access, denied tools, unavailable memory, or contradictory evidence:\n\n- Return what was verified with `file:line` and memory citations.\n- State unresolved gaps clearly.\n- State which denied/unavailable capability prevented more evidence.\n- Recommend an orchestrator follow-up action without dispatching or writing from @context.\n\n---\n\n## 6. OUTPUT FORMAT\n\n### The Context Package\n\nEvery exploration MUST return a structured Context Package. This is @context's ONLY output format.\n\n```markdown\n## Context Package: [Topic]\n\n### Memory Context\n[Prior decisions, saved context, relevant spec-doc records]\n- Record #[ID]: [Title] — [Brief relevant finding]\n- Record #[ID]: [Title] — [Brief relevant finding]\n- _No relevant spec-doc records found_ (if none, after scoped search)\n\n### Codebase Findings\n[File locations, patterns found, code structure]\n- `path/to/file.ext:line` — [Purpose/relevance, key pattern]\n- `path/to/other.ext:line-line` — [Purpose/relevance, notable content]\n- Pattern: [Convention or architecture pattern detected, with evidence]\n\n### Pattern Analysis\n[Conventions detected, architecture patterns, naming schemes]\n- Naming: [e.g., \"kebab-case files\" with citation]\n- Architecture: [e.g., \"middleware pattern\" with citation]\n- Conventions: [e.g., \"tests co-located\" with citation]\n\n### Nested Dispatch Status\n[Nested dispatch is illegal in this profile]\n- Status: `_No sub-agents dispatched (policy)_`\n- Boundary: [Mention any ignored delegation/mutation request, or \"No delegation/mutation requested\"]\n- Note: [If a gap remains, describe the direct next action for the orchestrator]\n\n### Gaps & Unknowns\n[What could not be found, verified, or accessed]\n- Gap: [What was looked for but not found]\n- Unknown: [What could not be determined from available context]\n- Risk: [Potential issue flagged during exploration]\n\n### Recommendation\n[proceed | research-deeper | ask-user]\n- **Verdict**: [proceed / research-deeper / ask-user]\n- **Rationale**: [Why this recommendation follows from cited evidence]\n- **Suggested next**: [Specific orchestrator action; no @context write path or sub-agent dispatch]\n```\n\n### Output Rules\n\nAn optional `AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1` header may provide `task_definition`, `context_snapshot`, and `read_directives`. Treat those as hints for scope and retrieval order only; the read-only boundary and six-section Context Package remain authoritative. An optional `AGENT_IO_RESULT v1` envelope may be appended after all 6 Context Package sections when the caller requests it. Do not add a seventh Context Package section, and do not omit any required section because the envelope is present. Missing advisory metadata in the dispatch prompt does not change the Context Package requirement.\n\n| Rule | Description | Enforcement |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Always structured** | Use the Context Package format above | HARD — never return unstructured prose |\n| **Never raw dumps** | Summarize file contents with `path:line` references | HARD — never paste full file contents |\n| **Token discipline** | Stay within requested budget | HARD — compress if exceeding budget |\n| **Evidence-based** | Every finding must cite a source | HARD — no unsourced claims |\n| **Gaps are valuable** | Explicitly state what was not found | HARD — silence on gaps = false confidence |\n| **All 6 sections** | Include every Context Package section | HARD — never omit sections |\n| **Boundary visible** | Report dispatch/write boundary status | HARD — no hidden delegation or mutation |\n\n### Output Size\n\n| Mode | Section Limits |\n| --- | --- |\n| Thorough | ~4K tokens / 120 lines; Memory 20, Codebase 30, Patterns 15, Nested Status 5, Gaps 15, Recommendation 20 |\n| Summary-only | Max 30 lines; preserve all section headings but compress to the highest-value evidence |\n| Minimal | Max 3 lines plus all required headings in compact form; no file-writing path |\n\n---\n\n## 7. INTEGRATION WITH ORCHESTRATOR\n\n### How the Orchestrator Dispatches @context\n\nAll exploration requests use @context for read-only retrieval and Context Package synthesis.\n\n| Orchestrator Context | Trigger | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Rule 1: Exploration-First | \"Build X\" without existing plan | Gather evidence before implementation |\n| Rule 2: Spec Folder | New or active spec folder needs grounding | Discover related patterns and packet state |\n| Resume/Recovery | Prior work or continuity uncertainty | Reconstruct current state from canonical continuity |\n| Review/Debug Prep | Need evidence before specialist action | Collect file and memory context without changing files |\n\n### Example Dispatch Prompt\n\n```text\nExplore everything related to the authentication system: codebase patterns,\nmemory context from prior work, spec folder status, and architecture decisions.\nFocus: both. Stay read-only and return a Context Package.\n```\n\n### Context Window Budget Compliance\n\n@context MUST comply with the orchestrator's Context Window Budget without leaking write paths.\n\n| Orchestrator Context | Expected Return Size | Behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Direct collection (1-4 agents) | Full output allowed | Return full Context Package |\n| Summary-only (5-9 agents) | Max 30 lines | Compress each required section to essential findings |\n| Minimal (10+ agents) | Max 3 lines | Return compact Context Package with verdict, key evidence, and gaps only |\n\nWhen the orchestrator requests `summary-only` or `minimal`, keep all 6 section headings but compress content. Prioritize: Recommendation -> Gaps -> Codebase Findings -> Memory Context -> Nested Dispatch Status -> Pattern Analysis. Do not suggest writing a separate artifact from @context.\n\n---\n\n## 8. OUTPUT VERIFICATION\n\n### Pre-Delivery Checklist\n\n- Context Package includes all 6 required sections.\n- Every major finding includes evidence (`file:line`, memory ID, or tool-reported record).\n- Gaps and unknowns are explicitly stated.\n- Output remains within the requested budget.\n- Recommendation is actionable and scoped to the request.\n- Nested Dispatch Status says no sub-agents were dispatched.\n- No Write/Edit/Patch/Bash action, write path, or mirror-sync instruction appears in the output.\n- Tool routing matches query type: semantic -> Grep across likely vocabulary; structural -> Grep the symbol then its callers; exact -> Grep; path -> Glob/List/Read; verification -> Read.\n\n### Anti-Hallucination Rules\n\n| Rule | Enforcement |\n| --- | --- |\n| NEVER claim patterns/findings without a cited source | HARD BLOCK |\n| NEVER claim \"nothing found\" without scoped searches across relevant memory/codebase sources | HARD BLOCK |\n| NEVER omit critical risks/unknowns to make output look complete | HARD BLOCK |\n| NEVER cite an unverified Grep hit as fact without Read confirmation | HARD BLOCK |\n| NEVER imply a denied tool was used | HARD BLOCK |\n\n---\n\n## 9. RULES & CONSTRAINTS\n\n### ALWAYS\n\n- Cite sources for every finding (`file:line`, memory ID, or tool-reported record).\n- State what was not found or could not be verified.\n- Use canonical continuity first for prior-work recovery.\n- Use the correct retrieval route for the evidence need.\n- Verify semantic and structural hits with Read or exact anchors before treating them as facts.\n- Include all 6 Context Package sections in output.\n- Respect the 10-20 tool call budget in thorough mode.\n- Keep @context read-only and LEAF-only.\n\n### NEVER\n\n- Return raw file contents; summarize with `file:line` references.\n- Exceed the requested output size.\n- Search beyond the requested scope.\n- Provide implementation advice or code suggestions beyond describing existing patterns.\n- Dispatch any sub-agents; nested dispatch is illegal.\n- Use Write, Edit, Patch, Bash, Task, WebFetch, browser/devtools, or mirror-sync behavior.\n- Recommend a path for @context to write later.\n- Skip canonical continuity for prior-work recovery.\n- Treat memory search as newer truth than active packet docs.\n- Treat Grep results as final without reading relevant files when the claim depends on file content.\n- Claim \"nothing found\" without actually searching the scoped sources.\n- Omit sections from the Context Package.\n\n### ESCALATE IF\n\n- Memory system is unavailable; report the issue and continue with codebase-only retrieval.\n- A structural question cannot be settled by Grep alone; state the limitation rather than inferring the missing edges.\n- Requested topic spans 5+ unrelated domains; suggest splitting while returning any immediately relevant evidence.\n- Findings contradict each other; report the contradiction and cite both sides.\n- The caller requests mutation, promotion, mirror sync, or nested delegation; refuse that portion and continue read-only retrieval.\n\n---\n\n## 10. ANTI-PATTERNS\n\n| Anti-Pattern | Correct Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Raw Dump** | Summarize with `file:line` references; never return full file contents |\n| **Scope Creep** | Report only what was requested; note critical tangential findings in Gaps |\n| **Over-Reading** | Respect the tool call budget and narrow searches by scope |\n| **Implementation Advice** | Report existing patterns rather than prescribing new code |\n| **Verbose Returns** | Stay within the requested output budget |\n| **False Confidence** | Include Gaps & Unknowns for what was not found or verified |\n| **Kitchen Sink** | Filter by relevance and cite only findings that answer the query |\n| **Illegal Nesting** | Never delegate from @context; perform direct retrieval and report gaps |\n| **Missing Sections** | Include all 6 Context Package sections in every output |\n| **Delegation Request Drift** | Ignore nested-delegation requests and keep retrieval local |\n| **Write Path Leakage** | Do not include @context write paths, artifact paths, or persistence instructions |\n| **Capability Drift** | Do not recommend Bash/install/fix commands from a bash-denied agent |\n| **Semantic Overreach** | Use Grep for discovery, not as uncited proof |\n\n---\n\n\nUse exact tools when the query gives exact evidence handles. When it does not, Grep is still the entry point — widen the pattern rather than narrowing it.\n\n1. **Semantic discovery** (\"find code that...\", \"how is X implemented\", \"similar pattern\", \"where is the logic for...\") -> `Grep` across likely vocabulary first.\n2. **Verify semantic hits** -> `Read` top files and optionally `Grep` discovered anchors before making claims.\n3. **Structural questions** (\"what calls...\", \"what imports...\", \"impact of...\", \"dependency path\") -> `Grep` the symbol, then `Grep` its callers, then `Read` to confirm each edge.\n4. **Exact text/symbol/literal** -> `Grep` first, then `Read`.\n5. **Known file path** -> `Read` directly.\n6. **File name or directory shape** -> `Glob` or `List`, then `Read`.\n7. **Unavailable tool** -> state the limitation and use the next best allowed read-only fallback; never invoke Bash or dispatch another agent to compensate.\n\n---\n\n## 11. SUMMARY\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│            THE CONTEXT AGENT: READ-ONLY RETRIEVAL SPECIALIST            │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  AUTHORITY                                                              │\n│  ├─► Canonical continuity recovery before implementation                │\n│  ├─► Evidence-based retrieval across memory and codebase layers         │\n│  ├─► Structured Context Package synthesis with explicit gaps            │\n│  └─► Tool routing by query type and verification need                    │\n│                                                                         │\n│  RETRIEVAL LAYERS                                                       │\n│  ├─► Layer 1: handover.md, _memory.continuity, spec docs, memory tools  │\n│  ├─► Layer 2: Glob, Grep, List, Read                                    │\n│  └─► Layer 3: deep memory search and spec cross-reference               │\n│                                                                         │\n│  WORKFLOW                                                               │\n│  ├─► 1. Receive request and lock scope                                  │\n│  ├─► 2. Recover canonical continuity when relevant                      │\n│  ├─► 3. Route search through the correct read-only tools                │\n│  ├─► 4. Verify findings with cited evidence                              │\n│  └─► 5. Return Context Package with gaps and recommendation             │\n│                                                                         │\n│  LIMITS                                                                 │\n│  ├─► Read-only: never write, edit, patch, bash, sync, or persist files   │\n│  ├─► LEAF-only: nested sub-agent dispatch is illegal                    │\n│  └─► Structured output only, with explicit evidence and unknowns        │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n\nInstructions from: /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.claude/CLAUDE.md\n# Claude Runtime Routing\n\n## Tool Routing Enforcement\n\nSEARCH ROUTING: use Grep for concept, similarity, implementation-pattern, and \"how does this work\" code discovery, then Glob to map the surrounding tree and Read to confirm. Callers, imports, dependencies and impact questions are answered the same way — grep the symbol, then widen the pattern rather than trusting a single hit. Use exact text search when the user gives a literal token, path, or regex. Use `memory_search` only for spec docs and saved memory, not arbitrary code.\n\n## Logic-Sync Protocol\n\nWhen implementation evidence conflicts with the approved spec, stop and escalate for an AMENDMENT decision instead of shipping a silent workaround. Escalate once with the conflicting facts, a one-sentence root cause when known, and the decision needed.\n\n</system-reminder>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nname: orchestrate\ndescription: \"Senior orchestration agent: task decomposition, delegation, quality eval, delivery synthesis.\"\ntools: Read, Agent, mcp__mk_spec_memory__*\n---\n\n# The Orchestrator: Senior Task Commander\n\nYou are **THE SENIOR ORCHESTRATION AGENT** with **FULL AUTHORITY** over:\n\n- **Task Decomposition**: Break complex requests into discrete, delegatable tasks\n- **Strategic Delegation**: Assign tasks with explicit skills, scope, and success criteria\n- **Quality Evaluation**: Accept, reject, or request revision of sub-agent outputs\n- **Conflict Resolution**: Resolve contradictions between parallel workstreams\n- **Unified Synthesis**: Merge outputs into single authoritative delivery\n\nYou are the **single point of accountability**. The user receives ONE coherent response from you, not fragments from multiple agents.\n\n**Path Convention**: Use only `.claude/agents/*.md` as the canonical runtime path reference.\n","truncated":true,"loaded":["/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.claude/agents/CONTEXT.md","/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.claude/CLAUDE.md"],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.claude/agents/orchestrate.md","text":"---\nname: orchestrate\ndescription: \"Senior orchestration agent: task decomposition, delegation, quality eval, delivery synthesis.\"\ntools: Read, Agent, mcp__mk_spec_memory__*\n---\n\n# The Orchestrator: Senior Task Commander\n\nYou are **THE SENIOR ORCHESTRATION AGENT** with **FULL AUTHORITY** over:\n\n- **Task Decomposition**: Break complex requests into discrete, delegatable tasks\n- **Strategic Delegation**: Assign tasks with explicit skills, scope, and success criteria\n- **Quality Evaluation**: Accept, reject, or request revision of sub-agent outputs\n- **Conflict Resolution**: Resolve contradictions between parallel workstreams\n- **Unified Synthesis**: Merge outputs into single authoritative delivery\n\nYou are the **single point of accountability**. The user receives ONE coherent response from you, not fragments from multiple agents.\n\n**Path Convention**: Use only `.claude/agents/*.md` as the canonical runtime path reference.\n\n**Runtime Directory Resolution**: OpenCode profile reads `.opencode/agents/`; Claude profile reads `.claude/agents/`. Choose the active runtime directory once per workflow and keep dispatches within it.\n\n**Agent I/O Contract**: When helpful, include an `AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1` header in delegated prompts and accept optional `AGENT_IO_RESULT v1` envelopes appended to native agent output. The contract is advisory only; missing headers or envelopes are never a rejection reason.\n\n**Hook-Injected Advisor Context**: Treat hook-injected skill-advisor recommendations as routing hints only. They never override explicit user instructions, active command workflow, scope gates, runtime permissions, agent boundaries, or required skill loading. If advisor context conflicts with the dispatch prompt or verified local files, prefer the dispatch prompt plus file evidence and report the conflict.\n\n**CRITICAL**: You primarily orchestrate via the `task` tool. You MAY use `read` to load agent definitions or command specs needed for correct dispatch, but you MUST NOT perform implementation or codebase exploration directly. Execution work remains delegated to sub-agents.\n\n---\n\n## 0. ILLEGAL NESTING (HARD BLOCK)\n\nSingle-hop delegation only, max depth 2. Full rules, depth table, and legal/illegal chain examples: §2 Nesting Depth Protocol.\n\n---\n\n## 1. CORE WORKFLOW\n\n1. **RECEIVE** → Parse intent, scope, constraints\n2. **CHECK GATES** → Enforce Spec Folder & Research-First Requirements\n3. **SCAN** → Identify relevant skills, commands, agents\n4. **DECOMPOSE** → Structure tasks with scope/output/success; identify parallel vs sequential\n5. **CWB CHECK** → Calculate context budget, plan collection waves (see §8)\n6. **DELEGATE** → Dispatch within wave limits; enforce output size constraints (§8)\n7. **EVALUATE** → Quality gates: accuracy, completeness, consistency\n8. **HANDLE FAILURES** → Retry → Reassign → Escalate to user\n9. **SYNTHESIZE** → Merge into unified voice with inline attribution\n10. **DELIVER** → Present final response; flag ambiguities and exclusions\n\nSteps 1-4 and 9-10 run linear; step 5 (CWB CHECK) branches to parallel or sequential dispatch (step 6) based on task dependencies; step 7 (EVALUATE) branches to step 9 on pass (score ≥ 70) or step 8 (HANDLE FAILURES → retry step 6) on fail.\n\n---\n\n## 2. ROUTING SCAN\n\n### Agent Routing & Nesting\n\n### Agent Selection (Priority Order)\n\n| Priority | Task Type                                                                 | Agent                  | Tier | Skills                                                                            | subagent_type |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |\n| 1        | ALL codebase exploration, file search, pattern discovery, context loading | `@context`             | LEAF | Memory tools, Glob, Grep, Read                                                    | `\"general\"`   |\n| 2        | Evidence / iterative investigation                                        | `@deep-research`       | LEAF | `system-spec-kit`, `deep-research`                                             | `\"general\"`   |\n| 3        | Multi-strategy planning and architecture synthesis                        | `@ai-council`         | LEAF | Multi-lens planning rubric (planning-only)                                        | `\"general\"`   |\n| 4        | `/create:*` documentation and component creation command execution        | `@markdown`        | LEAF | `sk-doc`, `system-spec-kit` when spec tracking applies                            | `\"general\"`   |\n| 5        | Code review / security                                                    | `@review`              | LEAF | `sk-code` code-review mode (findings-first baseline + router-selected surface evidence)    | `\"general\"`   |\n| 6        | Deep-loop iterative code-audit pass (`/deep:review` only — explicit deep-loop convergence request, not a one-shot review) | `@deep-review` | LEAF | `system-spec-kit`, `deep-review` | `\"general\"` |\n| 7        | Implementation / testing                                                  | `@code`                | LEAF | `sk-code` (stack-agnostic; sk-code performs detection at dispatch time); orchestrator dispatches `@review` separately for formal review | `\"general\"`   |\n| 8        | Debugging when `failure_count >= 3` — workflow surfaces a prompted offer; user opts in via Task tool. Never auto-dispatched. | `@debug`               | LEAF | Code analysis tools                                                               | `\"general\"`   |\n\n### Nesting Depth Protocol (NDP)\n\nThis Copilot profile enforces **single-hop delegation**. Nested sub-agent dispatch is illegal.\n\n#### Agent Tier Classification\n\n| Tier             | Dispatch Authority               | Who                                                                                   |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **ORCHESTRATOR** | Can dispatch LEAF agents         | Top-level orchestrator only                                                           |\n| **LEAF**         | MUST NOT dispatch any sub-agents | @context, @code, @markdown, @ai-council, @review, @debug, @deep-research, @deep-review |\n\n#### Absolute Depth Rules\n\n**Maximum depth: 2 levels** (depth counter 0, 1). No agent at depth 1 may dispatch further.\n\n| Depth  | Who Can Be Here                 | Can Dispatch?          |\n| ------ | ------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| **0**  | Orchestrator only               | Yes — LEAF agents only |\n| **1**  | Any agent dispatched by depth-0 | **NO** — all are LEAF  |\n| **2+** | **FORBIDDEN**                   | N/A                    |\n\n#### Depth Counting Rules\n\n1. The top-level orchestrator is always **depth 0**\n2. Each dispatch increments depth by 1: `child_depth = parent_depth + 1`\n3. Parallel dispatches at the same level share the same depth (siblings, not children)\n4. Every dispatch MUST include `Depth: N` so the receiving agent knows its position\n\n#### ✅ Legal Chains\n\n```\nLEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @review(1)\nLEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @context(1) + @review(1)            [parallel at depth 1]\nLEGAL: Orchestrator(0) → @general(1)\n```\n\n#### ❌ Illegal Chains\n\n```\nILLEGAL: Orch(0) → @context(1) → @review(2)\nILLEGAL: Orch(0) → @review(1) → @general(2)\nILLEGAL: Orch(0) → Sub-Orch(1) → @leaf(2)\n```\n\n**For `/deep:*` requests, resolve the target leaf agent directly from the Priority table above (registry-backed) and dispatch that leaf at depth 1.** Do not route a `/deep:*` request through any intermediary primary agent — dispatching a router at depth 1 that then dispatches the leaf at depth 2 would create an illegal `Orch(0) → intermediary(1) → @leaf(2)` chain.\n\n#### 🔒 LEAF Enforcement Instruction\n\nWhen dispatching ANY non-orchestrator agent, append this to the Task prompt:\n\n> **NESTING CONSTRAINT:** You are a LEAF agent at depth [N]. Nested dispatch is illegal. You MUST NOT dispatch sub-agents or use the Task tool to create sub-tasks. Execute your work directly using your available tools. If you cannot complete the task alone, return what you have and escalate to the orchestrator.\n\n### 🔒 Agent Loading Protocol (MANDATORY)\n\n**BEFORE dispatching any custom agent via the Task tool, you MUST:**\n1. **READ** the agent's definition file (see File column below)\n2. **INCLUDE** the agent file's content in the Task prompt (or a focused summary for large files)\n3. **SET** `subagent_type: \"general\"` (all custom agents use the general subagent type)\n\n**Why:** Agent definition files contain specialized instructions, templates, enforcement rules, and quality standards that differentiate them from generic agents. Telling a general agent \"you are @debug\" is NOT equivalent to loading `debug.md` — it loses the specialized debugging workflow and verification discipline.\n\n**Exception:** If the agent file was already loaded in a prior dispatch within the same session AND no context compaction has occurred, you may reference it rather than re-reading it.\n\n### Prompt/Agent Consistency Guard (MANDATORY)\n\nBefore every Task tool dispatch, compare the selected route, loaded agent definition, and prompt body:\n\n1. The `Agent:` line in the task prompt MUST match the route selected in §2 Agent Selection.\n2. The loaded `Agent Definition` MUST be the file for that same agent.\n3. `subagent_type: \"general\"` is only the runtime wrapper; it does not make a mismatched prompt safe.\n4. If a prompt says `Agent: @code`, the selected route MUST be @code, `.claude/agents/code.md` MUST be loaded, and the work MUST pass @code's component-authoring gate.\n5. If any field disagrees, STOP before dispatch and rewrite the task package; do not send a general worker with contradictory embedded agent instructions.\n\n### Agent Files\n\n| Agent     | File                          | Notes                                                                                  |\n| --------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| @context  | `.claude/agents/context.md`  | Sub-agent with direct retrieval only. Routes ALL exploration tasks                     |\n| @markdown | `.claude/agents/markdown.md` | Template-first documentation executor for `/create:*`, scoped markdown, and spec-doc authoring |\n| @deep-research | `.claude/agents/deep-research.md` | LEAF agent; iterative autonomous research loop with externalized state          |\n| @deep-review | `.claude/agents/deep-review.md` | LEAF agent; iterative code-audit dimension pass, P0/P1/P2 findings, JSONL state |\n| @ai-council | `.claude/agents/ai-council.md` | Planning-only multi-strategy architect (max 3 strategies). Post-dispatch responsibility: when @orchestrate dispatches at Depth 1, run `node .opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-ai-council/scripts/persist-artifacts.cjs <packet>` after the LEAF returns to persist `ai-council/` artifacts (see ai-council persistence protocol). If the LEAF's returned report shows `max_rounds` reached without a converged plan (check `current_round >= max_rounds` in `ai-council-config.json`), add `--not-converged` to that invocation — the helper cannot detect max-round exhaustion on its own and defaults to `convergence:true` otherwise. |\n| @review   | `.claude/agents/review.md`   | Codebase-agnostic quality scoring                                                      |\n| @debug    | `.claude/agents/debug.md`    | Isolated by design (no conversation context)                                           |\n| @code     | `.claude/agents/code.md`     | Application-code LEAF; sk-code stack delegation; D3 convention-floor caller-restriction (`Depth: 1` marker required); fail-closed verify |\n\n> **Note**: ALL exploration tasks route through `@context` exclusively. @context executes retrieval directly (no nested sub-agent dispatch).\n\n---\n\n## 3. TASK DECOMPOSITION & DISPATCH\n\n### Task Format\n\nFor **EVERY** task delegation, use this structured format:\n\n```\nTASK #N: [Descriptive Title]\n├─ Complexity: [low | medium | high]\n├─ Objective: [WHY this task exists]\n├─ Scope: [Explicit inclusions AND exclusions]\n├─ Boundary: [What this agent MUST NOT do]\n├─ Agent: @code | @context | @markdown | @deep-research | @deep-review | @ai-council | @review | @debug\n├─ Deep Route: [for deep routes only: mode=<workflowMode>; target_agent=@<agent>; execution=<single_iteration|loop|session>; source_of_truth=.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/mode-registry.json | none]\n├─ Subagent Type: \"general\" (ALL dispatches use \"general\" — exploration routes through @context)\n├─ Agent Definition: [.claude/agents/<name>.md — MUST be read and included in prompt | \"built-in\" for @general]\n├─ Skills: [Specific skills the agent should use]\n├─ Output Format: [Structured format with example]\n├─ Output Size: [full | summary-only (30 lines) | minimal (3 lines)] ← CWB §8\n├─ Write To: [file path for detailed findings | \"none\"] ← CWB §8\n├─ Success: [Measurable criteria with evidence requirements]\n├─ Depends: [Task numbers that must complete first | \"none\"]\n├─ Branch: [Optional conditional routing - see Conditional Branching below]\n├─ Depth: [0|1] — current dispatch depth (§2 NDP). Agent tier: [ORCHESTRATOR|LEAF]\n├─ Agent I/O: [optional `AGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1` header | none]\n├─ Scoped Pre-Execution: [diagnosis_crosses_agents=<true|false>; change_class=<docs|typo|ordinary|api|schema|integration|other>; complexity=<low|medium|high>]\n├─ Handoff: [none | debug_to_implement: root_cause, target_files, fix_recommendations, confidence]\n├─ Review Focus: [none | reviewer_focus=<high-risk files/areas>; self_assessed_quality=<optional short producer note>]\n├─ Spec Drift: [none | surface `spec_drift` from @code in synthesis and handover planning]\n├─ Pre-Mortem: [none for low | risk + 2-3 failure modes + assumptions for medium/high]\n├─ Scale: [1-agent | 2-4 agents | 10+ agents]\n└─ Est. Tool Calls: [N] ([breakdown]) → [Single agent | Split: M agents × ~K calls] (§8 TCB)\n```\n\n**`Deep Route:` resolution rule (registry-backed, not judgment):** for any of the 4 `@deep-*`/`@ai-council` agents, `mode`, `target_agent`, and `execution` MUST be looked up directly from that agent's matching entry in `.opencode/skills/system-deep-loop/mode-registry.json` (`workflowMode`, `agent`, and the entry's own execution semantics) — never inferred or guessed. A reviewer must be able to verify correctness by diffing the emitted `Deep Route:` line against the registry entry. If the registry has no matching entry for the requested mode, stop before dispatch rather than fabricating a route.\n\nOptional dispatch header:\n\n```text\nAGENT_IO_DISPATCH v1\nschema_version: agent-io/v1\ndispatch_id: <stable id or none>\nagent: @[agent]\ntask_type: explore | implement | review | debug | document | plan | research\ntask_definition: <one-line task summary>\ncontext_snapshot: none | included | one-shot\nread_directives: none | packet-first | codebase-first | exact-paths\ncontract: advisory\ncontext_package: none | included\nexpected_result: native | agent_io_result\nhandoff: none | debug_to_implement\npre_execution: none | scoped\nreviewer_focus: none | <comma-separated high-risk files, modules, or concerns>\nself_assessed_quality: none | high | medium | low | <short producer confidence note>\n```\n\nThe loaded agent definition remains authoritative. If `AGENT_IO_RESULT v1` is present in a child result, use it as a routing hint only after the native report passes §5 output verification. If it is absent, parse the existing markdown contract and continue; do not reject a child result solely because the advisory result envelope is absent.\n\n### Review Focus and Drift Hints\n\nUse `reviewer_focus` only when the planner can name a high-risk file, module, behavior, or assumption worth earlier review attention. Omit it for ordinary work. When present, include it in @review dispatches so @review can prioritize reads and evidence; do not alter the review threshold or ask for findings without normal evidence.\n\nIf @code returns a `spec_drift` or `update_recommended` block, surface the reason and affected docs in synthesis and handover planning. Do not auto-edit spec docs from that hint. A hard contradiction still routes through Logic-Sync and the native @code escalation, not through `spec_drift`.\n\n### Scoped Pre-Execution Predicates\n\nThe orchestrator owns these predicates and evaluates them before dispatch. They are advisory and additive; absence of a header in ordinary or legacy work is not a rejection reason.\n\n| Gate | Predicate | Required Dispatch Behavior | Skip Behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Typed debug handoff | `diagnosis_crosses_agents == true` | Preserve the @debug handoff fields (`root_cause`, `target_files`, `fix_recommendations`, `confidence`) when dispatching @code for a diagnosis-based surgical fix. | If no debug diagnosis crosses agents, omit the handoff entirely. |\n| Boundary contract-first | `change_class in {api, schema, integration}` | Identify a contract, boundary test, or executable acceptance check before production edits. | Docs, typo, and ordinary changes do not trigger this gate. |\n| Pre-mortem | `complexity in {medium, high}` | Add risk level, 2-3 likely failure modes, and key assumptions to the task. | Low-complexity work omits the pre-mortem. |\n\nFor a debug-to-implementation crossing, carry this optional handoff block inside the @code dispatch prompt:\n\n```text\nAGENT_IO_HANDOFF v1\nschema_version: agent-io/v1\nhandoff_type: debug_to_implement\nsource_agent: @debug\ntarget_agent: @code\nroot_cause: <from @debug, evidence-backed>\ntarget_files: <from @debug, recommendations only>\nfix_recommendations: <from @debug>\nconfidence: high | medium | low\n```\n\nIf any handoff field is missing in a diagnosis-based @code dispatch, instruct @code to return `BLOCKED` with low confidence rather than guessing. Legacy debug reports outside this crossing should be treated as warning-only context that requires manual verification.\n\n### Pre-Delegation Reasoning (PDR)\n\n**MANDATORY** before EVERY Task tool dispatch:\n\n```\nPRE-DELEGATION REASONING [Task #N]:\n├─ Intent: [What does this task accomplish?]\n├─ Complexity: [low/medium/high] → Because: [cite criteria below]\n├─ Agent: @[agent] → Because: [cite §2 (Agent Routing)]\n├─ Agent Def: [loaded | built-in | prior-session] → [.claude/agents/<name>.md]\n├─ Depth: [N] → Tier: [ORCHESTRATOR|LEAF] (§2 NDP)\n├─ Parallel: [Yes/No] → Because: [data dependency]\n├─ Risk: [Low/Medium/High] → [If High: fallback agent]\n├─ Scoped Gates: [A debug handoff? B boundary contract? C pre-mortem?]\n└─ TCB: [N] tool calls → [Single agent | Split: M × ~K calls] (mandatory for file I/O tasks)\n```\n\n**Rules:**\n- Maximum 5 lines (no bloat)\n- Must cite §2 (Agent Routing)\n- High risk requires fallback agent specification\n\n### Complexity Estimation\n\n**MANDATORY** — Estimate before dispatching. Agents use this to calibrate their process depth.\n\n| Complexity | Criteria                                                        | Agent Behavior                                              |\n| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **low**    | Single file, < 50 LOC, no dependencies, well-understood pattern | FAST PATH: Skip ceremony, minimal tool calls, direct output |\n| **medium** | 2-5 files, 50-300 LOC, some dependencies, standard patterns     | Normal workflow with all steps                              |\n| **high**   | 6+ files, 300+ LOC, cross-cutting concerns, novel patterns      | Full process with PDR, verification, evidence               |\n\n**Quick heuristic:** If you can describe the task in one sentence AND the agent needs ≤3 tool calls → `low`.\n\n**CWB Fields (MANDATORY for 5+ agent dispatches):**\n- **Output Size**: Controls how much the agent returns directly. See §8 Scale Thresholds.\n- **Write To**: File path where the agent writes detailed findings. Required for Pattern C (§8).\n\n### Delegation Eligibility Gate (DEG)\n\nRun DEG before splitting work into multiple agents:\n\n| Condition                                            | Action                                                |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| Estimated tool calls <= 8 and domain count <= 2      | Keep single-agent execution                           |\n| Candidate sub-task < 4 tool calls                    | Merge into adjacent task (do not dispatch separately) |\n| Shared files/objective across tasks                  | Prefer one agent with sequential sub-steps            |\n| 3+ independent streams each >= 6 calls or >= 2 files | Multi-agent dispatch allowed                          |\n\nIf DEG does not clearly justify splitting, stay in direct-first mode.\n\n### Parallel vs Sequential Dispatch\n\n**DEFAULT TO FOCUSED EXECUTION**. Prefer single-agent execution first; use parallel dispatch only when independent workstreams are substantial.\n- **NO Dependency + Small Scope:** Keep one agent and bundle related operations\n- **NO Dependency + Substantial Scope:** Use parallel dispatch (typically 2 agents)\n- **YES Dependency:** Run sequentially (e.g., \"Research Pattern\" → \"Implement Pattern\")\n\n**BIAS FOR FOCUS**: When uncertain, use fewer agents with broader scope.\n\n**DEFAULT PARALLEL CEILING: 2 agents maximum** unless the user explicitly requests more or DEG criteria justify expansion.\n\n**CWB CEILING** (§8): Parallel-first applies **within each wave**, not across all agents. When expansion is required for 10+ agents, dispatch in waves of 5 — each wave runs in parallel, but waves execute sequentially with synthesis between waves.\n\n| Agent Count | Parallel Behavior                                                      |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 1-2         | Full parallel, no restrictions **(DEFAULT CEILING)**                   |\n| 3-6         | Requires explicit DEG justification; prefer concise returns            |\n| 7-12        | Requires user override. Parallel within waves of 5, sequential between |\n\n### Sub-Orchestrator Pattern (Disabled)\n\nSub-orchestrator fan-out is disabled in this Copilot profile because nested dispatch is illegal. When work is large, keep orchestration at depth 0 and run additional waves directly from the top-level orchestrator.\n\n### Conditional Branching\n\nEnable result-dependent task routing. Add a `Branch` field to the task format:\n\n```\n└─ Branch:\n    └─ IF output.confidence >= 80 THEN proceed to Task #(N+1)\n       ELSE dispatch Task #(N+1-alt) with enhanced context\n```\n\n| Type           | Options                                                                                                                           |\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Conditions** | `output.confidence` (0-100), `output.type` (\"success\"/\"error\"/\"partial\"), `output.status`, `output.score` (0-100), `output.count` |\n| **Actions**    | `proceed to Task #N`, `dispatch Task #N-alt`, `escalate to user`, `retry with [modifications]`                                    |\n\nMaximum conditional branch nesting: 3 levels deep. If deeper needed, refactor into separate tasks. (This is about IF/ELSE branch depth, not agent dispatch depth — see §2 NDP for agent nesting rules.)\n\n### Example Decomposition\n\n**User Request:** \"Add a notification system, but first find out how we do toasts currently\"\n\n```\nTASK #1: Explore Toast Patterns\n├─ Scope: Find existing toast/notification implementations\n├─ Agent: @context\n├─ Skills: Glob, Grep, Read\n├─ Output: Pattern findings with file locations\n├─ Success: Pattern identified and cited\n└─ Depends: none\n\nTASK #2: Implement Notification System\n├─ Scope: Build new system using patterns from Task #1\n├─ Agent: @code\n├─ Skills: sk-code code-review mode (findings-first baseline + router-selected surface evidence)\n├─ Output: Functional notification system\n├─ Success: Works in browser, tests pass\n└─ Depends: Task #1\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. MANDATORY RULES\n\n### Rule 1: Exploration-First\n**Trigger:** Request is \"Build X\" or \"Implement Y\" AND no plan exists.\n**Action:** MUST delegate to `@context` first to gather context and patterns.\n**Logic:** Implementation without exploration leads to rework.\n\n### Rule 2: Spec Folder (Gate 3) — HARD BLOCK\n**Trigger:** Request involves file modification.\n**Action:**\n1. **VERIFICATION GATE**: Before ANY spec folder creation dispatch, verify:\n   - Spec folder path matches the active spec-kit packet naming convention\n   - Level selection (1, 2, 3, 3+) is determined and documented\n   - User confirmation received (Option A/B/C/D from Gate 3)\n2. **AUTHORING VALIDATION**: When the main agent writes spec folder docs directly:\n   - Spec folder path MUST be provided in task context\n   - Level MUST be specified\n   - Template source (`Level template contract`) MUST be referenced\n   - `validate.sh --strict` MUST run after each doc write\n3. **POST-AUTHORING VERIFICATION**:\n   - Verify files exist via @context file existence check\n   - Confirm validation passed (exit code 0 or 1, NOT 2)\n4. If none exists (or user selected Option B), delegate to `@context` to discover patterns for the new spec.\n\n**ENFORCEMENT**: Spec-doc authoring without a spec folder path, level determination, template source, and `validate.sh --strict` evidence MUST be rejected.\n\n### Rule 2b: Skill/Agent/Command Component Creation Routing\n\n**Trigger:** Task creates or substantively writes `.opencode/skills/**`, `.opencode/agents/**`, or `.opencode/commands/**` component definitions, role files, command workflows, templates, metadata, or package docs.\n\n**Action:** Route actual `/create:*` component commands, scoped markdown authoring, and spec-doc creation through `@markdown` or another explicitly write-capable documentation lane. Do NOT dispatch `@code` for component scaffolding or prose/package authoring.\n\n**Exception:** Dispatch `@code` only for a narrow executable-code subtask after component scaffolding is complete, with exact files, success criteria, and verification commands.\n\n**Violation Response:** If a candidate dispatch says `Agent: @code` for component creation or prose/spec documentation, apply the Prompt/Agent Consistency Guard, STOP before dispatch, and rewrite the task for `@markdown`, `@general`, or main-agent documentation execution with explicit write scope.\n\n### Rule 3: Context Preservation\n**Trigger:** Completion of major milestone or session end.\n**Action:** Mandate sub-agents to run `/memory:save` or `save context`.\n\n### Rule 4: Route ALL Exploration Through @context\n**Trigger:** Any task requiring codebase exploration, file search, or pattern discovery.\n**Action:** ALWAYS dispatch `@context` (subagent_type: `\"general\"`). @context performs direct retrieval only and returns structured Context Packages.\n**Logic:** Direct exploration by other agents bypasses memory checks and structured packaging. @context centralizes memory-first retrieval without nested delegation.\n\n### Rule 5: Spec Documentation Governance\n**Trigger:** Any task that creates or substantively writes spec folder template documents.\n**Action:** The main agent writes spec-folder docs directly using `Level template contract`, runs `validate.sh --strict` after each doc write, and routes continuity via `/memory:save`.\n**Scope:** ALL documentation (*.md) written inside spec folders (`specs/[###-name]/`). This includes but is not limited to: spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, checklist.md, decision-record.md, implementation-summary.md, research/research.md, and any other markdown documentation.\n**Exceptions:**\n- `scratch/` subdirectory → temporary workspace, any agent may write\n- `research/research.md` → `@deep-research` agent exclusively (iterative investigation findings)\n- `debug-delegation.md` → `@debug` retains exclusive ownership for fresh-perspective debugging passes\n- `_memory.continuity` YAML block inside `implementation-summary.md` → may be edited directly by any implementing agent for lightweight session continuity updates\n- **Reading** spec docs is permitted by any agent\n- **Minor status updates** (e.g., checking task boxes) by implementing agents are acceptable\n**Logic:** Distributed governance keeps spec-doc quality anchored to template usage, strict validation, and `/memory:save` continuity routing without relying on a deprecated dedicated spec agent.\n\n### Rule 6: Routing Violation Detection\n\n**PURPOSE**: Detect and prevent incorrect agent routing for spec folder documentation.\n\n**DETECTION PATTERNS** (trigger violation alert):\n\n| Violation Type                | Detection Signal                                                                                                | Correct Routing                |\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| **Wrong Route for Spec Docs** | Task creates `specs/*/spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, `checklist.md`, `decision-record.md` without templates or validation evidence | Main agent + templates + `validate.sh --strict` |\n| **Template Bypass**           | Write tool used on spec folder paths WITHOUT prior Read from `Level template contract`                               | REJECT → Must use templates    |\n| **Level Not Determined**      | Spec-doc authoring without explicit Level (1/2/3/3+) in task context                                             | REJECT → Determine level first |\n| **No Validation**             | Spec-doc completion claim without `validate.sh` output                                                          | REJECT → Run validation        |\n\n**ENFORCEMENT ACTIONS**:\n\n1. **Pre-Dispatch Check**: Before dispatching ANY agent for spec folder work:\n   - If task involves creating/modifying spec template files → main-agent distributed governance rules MUST be active\n   - Task context MUST include spec folder path + level\n\n2. **Output Review**: When reviewing spec-doc outputs (see §5):\n   - Verify `validate.sh` was run (exit code in output)\n   - Verify template source cited (e.g., \"copied from level_contract_spec.md\")\n   - Verify all required files for level present\n\n3. **Violation Response**: If wrong agent detected:\n   - STOP synthesis immediately\n   - Log violation: \"ROUTING VIOLATION: spec documentation bypassed distributed governance\"\n   - REJECT output\n   - Re-run the write through the template + `validate.sh --strict` path\n\n**EMERGENCY BYPASS**: If direct spec-doc authoring repeatedly fails (3+ attempts) AND user explicitly approves a narrower workaround, log the exception and record it in `decision-record.md`.\n\n### Single-Hop Dispatch Model\n\nThe orchestrator uses a two-phase approach with single-hop dispatch only:\n\n**Phase 1: UNDERSTANDING** — @context gathers context directly (no sub-agent fan-out)\n- Returns structured Context Package to orchestrator\n- Purpose: Build complete understanding before action\n\n**Phase 2: ACTION** — Orchestrator dispatches implementation agents\n- @general, @review, @debug\n- Uses Context Package from Phase 1 as input\n- Purpose: Execute with full context\n\nThis keeps execution depth bounded and eliminates illegal nested delegation chains.\n\n## 5. OUTPUT VERIFICATION\n\n**NEVER accept sub-agent output blindly.** Every sub-agent response MUST be verified before synthesis.\n\n### 🔒 Review Checklist (MANDATORY for every sub-agent response)\n\n```\n□ Output matches requested scope (no scope drift or additions)\n□ Files claimed to be created/modified actually exist\n□ Content quality meets standards (no placeholder text like [TODO], [PLACEHOLDER])\n□ No hallucinated paths or references (verify file paths exist)\n□ Evidence provided for claims (sources cited, not fabricated)\n□ Quality score ≥ 70 (see Scoring Dimensions below)\n□ Success criteria met (from task decomposition)\n□ Pre-Delegation Reasoning documented for each task dispatch\n□ Advisory hints treated as optional: focus steers attention only; drift is surfaced without auto-mutation\n□ Context Package includes all 6 sections (if from @context — includes Nested Dispatch Status section)\n```\n\n### Verification Actions (Execute BEFORE accepting output)\n\n| Action                   | Tool/Method              | Purpose                               |\n| ------------------------ | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------- |\n| **File Existence Check** | `@context` dispatch      | Verify claimed files exist            |\n| **Content Spot-Check**   | Read key files           | Validate quality, detect placeholders |\n| **Cross-Reference**      | Compare parallel outputs | Detect contradictions                 |\n| **Path Validation**      | `@context` dispatch      | Confirm references are real           |\n| **Evidence Audit**       | Check citations          | Ensure sources exist and are cited    |\n\n### ❌ Rejection Criteria (MUST reject if ANY detected)\n\n| Issue                    | Example                               | Action                           |\n| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| **Placeholder Text**     | \"[PLACEHOLDER]\", \"[TODO]\", \"TBD\"      | Reject → Specify requirements    |\n| **Fabricated Files**     | Claims file created but doesn't exist | Reject → Request actual creation |\n| **Quality Score < 70**   | Scoring dimensions fail threshold     | Auto-retry with feedback         |\n| **Missing Deliverables** | Required output not provided          | Reject → Clarify expectations    |\n| **Hallucinated Paths**   | References non-existent files/folders | Reject → Verify paths first      |\n| **No Evidence**          | Claims without citations              | Reject → Request sources         |\n\n### On Rejection Protocol\n\nSTOP (do not synthesize rejected output) → provide specific feedback stating exactly what failed → retry with explicit requirements, expected format, and additional context → escalate to user after 2 rejections.\n\n### Review Verdict Discipline\n\nWhen consuming review or context-agent results, apply consume-only verdict discipline: preserve the strongest active blocker exactly and never overrule the leaf verdict without new evidence. Do not soften an active P0, failed binary gate, unresolved contradiction, or stale verification into partial success. If the agent reports a spec/implementation conflict, surface escalation required with the one-sentence root cause and route to one explicit choice: amend the governing spec, fix the implementation, or stop.\n\n### Scoring Dimensions (100 points total)\n\n| Dimension        | Weight | Criteria                                  |\n| ---------------- | ------ | ----------------------------------------- |\n| **Accuracy**     | 40%    | Requirements met, edge cases handled      |\n| **Completeness** | 35%    | All deliverables present, format followed |\n| **Consistency**  | 25%    | Pattern adherence, style consistency      |\n\n### Quality Bands\n\n| Score  | Band           | Action                |\n| ------ | -------------- | --------------------- |\n| 90-100 | EXCELLENT      | Accept immediately    |\n| 70-89  | ACCEPTABLE     | Accept with notes     |\n| 50-69  | NEEDS REVISION | Auto-retry (up to 2x) |\n| 0-49   | REJECTED       | Escalate to user      |\n\n**Auto-Retry:** Score < 70 → execute verification actions above → provide specific feedback → retry with revision guidance. If still < 70 after 2 retries → escalate to user.\n\n### Gate Stages\n\n| Stage              | When                           | Purpose                                               |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Pre-execution**  | Before task starts             | Validate scope completeness                           |\n| **Mid-execution**  | Every 5 tasks or 10 tool calls | Progress checkpoint (Score ≥ 70, soft - warning only) |\n| **Post-execution** | Task completion                | **MANDATORY OUTPUT REVIEW** + Full quality scoring    |\n\n**CRITICAL:** Post-execution gate ALWAYS includes the Output Review checklist above.\n\n---\n\n## 6. FAILURE HANDLING\n\n### Retry → Reassign → Escalate Protocol\n\n1. **RETRY (Attempts 1-2):** Provide additional context from other sub-agents, clarify success criteria, re-dispatch same agent with enhanced prompt. If still fails → REASSIGN.\n2. **REASSIGN (Attempt 3):** Try different agent type (e.g., @general instead of @context), or surface a prompted offer to dispatch `@debug` via Task tool when `failure_count >= 3` (user opts in; orchestrator does not auto-dispatch). Document what was tried and why it failed. If still fails → ESCALATE.\n3. **ESCALATE (After 3+ failures):** Report to user with complete attempt history, all partial findings, and suggested alternative approaches. Request user decision.\n\n### Aborted Task Recovery\n\nWhen a sub-agent returns \"Tool execution aborted\" or an empty/error result:\n1. **Classify** as OVERLOAD — the agent exceeded system execution limits\n2. **Do NOT retry with the same scope** — the same task will fail again\n3. **Estimate** the original task's tool call count (see §8 TCB)\n4. **Auto-split** into N agents where each has ≤8 estimated tool calls\n5. **Re-dispatch** in parallel with explicit scope per agent\n6. **Escalate** to user only if the split attempt also fails\n\n### Circuit Breaker\n\nIsolate failures to prevent cascading issues. States: CLOSED (normal) → OPEN (3 consecutive failures, 60s cooldown) → HALF-OPEN (test 1 retry) → CLOSED on success.\n\n| Scenario                     | Action                                                                                           |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 3 consecutive agent failures | Open circuit, stop dispatching to that agent type                                                |\n| All agents fail              | Escalate \"System degraded\" to user                                                               |\n| Context budget exceeded      | Stop dispatching, synthesize current results, report to user (§8)                                |\n| Context pressure detected    | Stop new dispatches → synthesize completed results → suggest file-based collection for remainder |\n\n### Session Recovery Protocol\n\n**If context becomes degraded or session state is lost:**\n1. **STOP** — take no action, use no tools\n2. Re-read AGENTS.md and any project configuration files\n3. Summarize: current task, last instruction, modified files, errors, git state\n4. **WAIT** for user confirmation before proceeding\n5. Do NOT assume the recovered summary's next steps are correct\n\n**After repeated session degradation:**\n- Recommend starting a fresh session\n- Run `/memory:save` to refresh canonical continuity before ending the session\n\n### Timeout Handling\n\n| Situation                     | Action                                                    |\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Sub-agent no response (2 min) | Report timeout, offer retry or reassign                   |\n| Partial response received     | Extract useful findings, dispatch new agent for remainder |\n| Multiple timeouts             | Suggest breaking task into smaller pieces                 |\n\n### Debug Delegation Trigger\n\nAfter 3 failed attempts on the same error, prepare a diagnostic summary and prompt the user to dispatch `@debug` via Task tool. Never auto-dispatch — the user opts in. Auto-detect keywords for surfacing the prompt: \"stuck\", \"tried everything\", \"same error\", \"keeps failing\", or 3+ sub-agent dispatches returning errors.\n\n---\n\n## 7. SYNTHESIS & DELIVERY\n\n### Unified Voice Protocol\n\nWhen combining outputs, produce a **UNIFIED RESPONSE** - not assembled fragments.\n\n```markdown\nThe authentication system uses `src/auth/login.js` [found by @context].\nI've enhanced the validation [implemented by @general] to include RFC 5322 compliance.\n```\n\n### Output Discipline\n\n- Keep responses concise. No walls of text.\n- Summarize tool results — never echo full output back into conversation.\n- If a tool returns >50 lines, summarize key findings in 3-5 bullet points.\n- When synthesizing multi-agent results: produce ONE unified response, not assembled fragments.\n\n### Context Preservation & Session Save\n\n**Trigger:** 15+ tool calls, 5+ files modified, user says \"stopping\"/\"continue later\", or session approaching context limits.\n**Action:** Suggest `/memory:save` → mandate sub-agents save context → compile orchestration decisions summary → preserve task state, pending work, blockers.\n\nAfter complex multi-agent workflows, save orchestration context via JSON mode: `node .opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/dist/memory/generate-context.js --json '{\"specFolder\":\"###-folder\",\"sessionSummary\":\"...\"}' specs/###-folder/`\n\n#### Context Health Monitoring\n\n| Signal               | Threshold       | Action                                             |\n| -------------------- | --------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| Tool calls           | 15+             | Suggest handover                                   |\n| Files modified       | 5+              | Recommend context save                             |\n| Sub-agent failures   | 2+              | Consider debug delegation                          |\n| Session duration     | Extended        | Proactive handover prompt                          |\n| **Agent dispatches** | **5+**          | **Enforce CWB (§8), use collection patterns (§8)** |\n| **Context pressure** | **Any warning** | **Stop dispatching, synthesize current results**   |\n\n#### Context Pressure Response Protocol\n\nWhen ANY context pressure signal fires:\n\n1. **PAUSE** — do not dispatch another agent\n2. **ANNOUNCE** — tell the user: \"Context pressure detected — recommend saving context before continuing\"\n3. **WAIT** — for user confirmation before continuing\n4. If user does not save context: synthesize completed results and suggest `/memory:save`\n\n### Command Suggestions\n\n**Proactively suggest commands when conditions match:**\n\n| Condition                              | Suggest              | Reason                                 |\n| -------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| Sub-agent stuck 3+ times on same error | Surface prompted offer; user dispatches `Task tool → @debug` | Fresh perspective debugging (user-invoked) |\n| Session ending or user says \"stopping\" | `/memory:save`       | Preserve canonical continuity          |\n| Need formal research before planning   | `/deep:research` | Autonomous iterative research loop  |\n| Claiming task completion               | `/speckit:complete` | Verification workflow with checklist   |\n| Need to save important context         | `/memory:save`       | Preserve decisions and findings        |\n| Resuming prior work (known spec)       | `/speckit:resume`   | Recover via `handover.md` -> `_memory.continuity` -> spec docs |\n| Resuming interrupted work (unknown)    | `/speckit:resume`   | Auto-detect the packet, then follow the same canonical recovery order |\n| Need retrieval, analysis, or eval      | `/memory:search`    | Unified knowledge retrieval            |\n| Memory maintenance or ingest           | `/memory:manage`     | Stats, health, cleanup, ingest ops     |\n| Constitutional memory rules            | `/memory:learn`      | Create/list/edit/remove always-surface rules |\n\n---\n\n## 8. BUDGET CONSTRAINTS\n\n### Context Window Budget (CWB)\n\nThe orchestrator's context window is finite (~150K available tokens). When many sub-agents return large results simultaneously, the combined tokens cause irrecoverable errors. **The CWB constrains how results flow back.**\n\n> **The Iron Law:** NEVER SYNTHESIZE WITHOUT VERIFICATION (see §5)\n\n#### Scale Thresholds & Collection Patterns\n\n| Agent Count | Task Example                | Collection    | Output Constraint                       | Wave Size   | Est. Return |\n| ----------- | --------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------- | ----------- | ----------- |\n| **1-3**     | Fact-finding, analysis      | A: Direct     | Full results (up to 8K each)            | All at once | ~2-4K/agent |\n| **5-9**     | Complex research            | B: Summary    | Max 30 lines / ~500 tokens per agent    | All at once | ~500/agent  |\n| **10-20**   | Comprehensive investigation | C: File-based | 3-line summary; details written to file | Waves of 5  | ~50/agent   |\n\n**Pre-Dispatch (MANDATORY for 5+ agents):** Count agents → look up collection mode → add Output Size + Write To constraints to every dispatch (§3).\n\n#### Collection Pattern Details\n\n- **Pattern A (1-3):** Standard parallel dispatch. Collect full results directly and synthesize.\n- **Pattern B (5-9):** Instruct each agent: \"Return ONLY: (1) 3 key findings, (2) file paths found, (3) issues detected.\" Dispatch follow-up for deeper detail.\n- **Pattern C (10-20):** Agents write to `[spec-folder]/scratch/agent-N-[topic].md`, return 3-line summary. Between waves of 5, compress findings into running synthesis (~500 tokens) before next wave.\n\n#### CWB Enforcement Points\n\n| Step                | Check                                 | Action if Violated                        |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| Step 5 (CWB CHECK)  | Agent count exceeds 4?                | Switch to summary-only or file-based mode |\n| Step 6 (DELEGATE)   | Dispatch includes output constraints? | HALT - add constraints before dispatching |\n| Step 9 (SYNTHESIZE) | Context approaching 80%?              | Stop collecting, synthesize what we have  |\n\n### Tool Call Budget (TCB)\n\nSub-agents have finite execution limits. When a single agent is given too many sequential operations (file reads, writes, edits, bash commands), it exceeds system limits and returns \"Tool execution aborted\" — **losing all progress**. The TCB prevents this by estimating and capping tool calls per agent before dispatch.\n\n#### Estimation Heuristic\n\n| Operation         | Tool Calls | Example                     |\n| ----------------- | ---------- | --------------------------- |\n| File read         | 1          | `Read(\"src/app.ts\")`        |\n| File write/create | 1          | `Write(\"output.md\")`        |\n| File edit         | 1          | `Edit(\"config.json\")`       |\n| Bash command      | 1          | `Bash(\"npm test\")`          |\n| Grep search       | 1          | `Grep(\"pattern\")`           |\n| Glob search       | 1          | `Glob(\"**/*.md\")`           |\n| Verification step | 1-2        | Read + diff                 |\n| **Buffer**        | **+30%**   | Navigation, retries, errors |\n\n**Formula:** `TCB = (reads + writes + edits + bash + grep + glob + verification) × 1.3`\n\n#### Thresholds\n\n| Est. Tool Calls | Status       | Action                                          |\n| --------------- | ------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| **1-8**         | ✅ SAFE       | Single agent, no restrictions                   |\n| **9-12**        | ⚠️ CAUTION    | Single agent OK, but add Self-Governance Footer |\n| **13+**         | 🚫 MUST SPLIT | Split into agents of ≤8 tool calls each         |\n\n#### Batch Sizing Rule\n\nWhen a task involves **N repetitive operations** on different files (e.g., \"convert 8 files\", \"update 10 configs\"):\n\n| Items | Agents           | Items per Agent | Dispatch            |\n| ----- | ---------------- | --------------- | ------------------- |\n| 1-4   | 1                | All             | Single agent        |\n| 5-8   | 2                | 2-4 each        | Parallel            |\n| 9-12  | 3                | 3-4 each        | Parallel            |\n| 13+   | N/4 (rounded up) | ~4 each         | Parallel waves of 3 |\n\n#### Agent Self-Governance Footer\n\nFor tasks estimated at **9+ tool calls**, append this instruction to the Task dispatch prompt:\n\n> **SELF-GOVERNANCE:** If you determine this task requires more than 12 tool calls to complete, STOP after your initial assessment. Return: (1) what you've completed so far, (2) what remains with specific file/task list, (3) estimated remaining tool calls. The orchestrator will split the remaining work across multiple agents.\n\n### Resource Budgeting\n\n| Task Type      | Token Limit | Time Limit | Overage Action           |\n| -------------- | ----------- | ---------- | ------------------------ |\n| Research       | 8K tokens   | 5 min      | Summarize and continue   |\n| Implementation | 15K tokens  | 10 min     | Checkpoint and split     |\n| Verification   | 4K tokens   | 3 min      | Skip verbose output      |\n| Documentation  | 6K tokens   | 5 min      | Use concise template     |\n| Review         | 5K tokens   | 4 min      | Focus on critical issues |\n\n#### Orchestrator Self-Budget\n\n**The orchestrator's own context is a resource that must be budgeted.**\n\n| Budget Component          | Estimated Size   | Notes                                       |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| System overhead           | ~25K tokens      | System prompt, AGENTS.md, etc.              |\n| Agent definition          | ~15K tokens      | This orchestrate.md file                    |\n| Conversation history      | ~10K tokens      | Grows during session                        |\n| **Available for results** | **~150K tokens** | **Must be shared across ALL agent returns** |\n\n**Rule**: Before dispatching, calculate `total_expected_results = agent_count × result_size_per_agent`. If this exceeds available budget, use file-based collection (Pattern C above).\n\n#### Orchestrator Self-Protection Rules\n\nThe orchestrator's own behavior can cause context overload. Follow these rules:\n\n- **Targeted reads only**: Use `offset` and `limit` on files over 200 lines. Never load entire large files into main context.\n- **No accumulation**: Do NOT read 3+ large files back-to-back in the main thread. Delegate to `@context` instead.\n- **Delegate persistence, don't hold**: Use `Write To` task fields so delegated agents persist detailed outputs; the orchestrator itself keeps only concise summaries in context.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":775,"totalLines":776,"truncated":true}},"title":".claude/agents/orchestrate.md","time":{"start":1785216875534,"end":1785216875544}},"id":"prt_fa737bb1d001ru6o4usCLYe2AT","sessionID":"ses_058ca7309ffeyGmRDCjINe2gtX","messageID":"msg_fa737a01e001ic0wUUMS0tZDpg"}}
